      "text": "71,487? Wait, but let me check again because when I first thought, I thought maybe 71,487, but let me confirm.\n\nWait, actually, let's see:.\n\n61,100 + 6,110 = 67,210. Then adding 4,277 to that. 67,210 + 4,277. Let me add 67,210 + 4,000 first, which is 71,210. Then add 277: 71,210 + 277 = 71,487. Okay, that matches. So the total is 71,487.\n\nBut wait, let me cross-verify using another method to be sure. Maybe the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 611 by 117. Let's do it step by step.\n\nFirst, write 611  \n           \u00d7117  \n       __________\n\nStart with multiplying 611 by 7 (the units digit of 117):\n\n611 \u00d77:  \n7\u00d71 =7, 7\u00d71=7, 7\u00d76=42. So that gives 4,277. Correct as before.\n\nNext, multiply 611 by 1 (the tens place). Since it's the tens place, this 1 represents 10, so the result will be shifted one position to the left. So 611 \u00d71 = 611, but shifted one place: 6,110.\n\nThen multiply 611 by 1 (the hundreds place, which is the first digit here since 117 is three digits). Wait, actually, 117 is hundreds, tens, units. So the first digit from the right is units (7), then tens (1), then hundreds (1). Wait, no. Wait, 117 is 1 hundred, 1 ten, 7 units. So the leftmost digit is hundreds place. So when multiplying, the second line should be multiplying by the tens digit (1), and the third line by the hundreds digit (1), shifted accordingly.\n\nSo let me correct that:\n\nFirst line: 611 \u00d77 =4,277  \nSecond line: 611 \u00d71 (tens place, so shifted one left) = 6,110  \nThird line: 611 \u00d71 (hundreds place, shifted two left) = 61,100  \n\nThen add them all together: 4,277 + 6,110 + 61,100. Which is exactly the same as before, so adding those gives 71,487. Okay, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the distributive property in another way, or perhaps the difference of squares or something? Hmm, but 611 and 117 don't seem to form numbers that would make that approach easier. Maybe breaking down 117 as 120 - 3?\n\nLet me try that. So 611 \u00d7 (120 - 3) = 611\u00d7120 - 611\u00d73.\n\nCalculating 611\u00d7120: 611\u00d712\u00d710. Let's compute 611\u00d712 first. 611\u00d710 is 6,110, plus 611\u00d72 is 1,222. So 6,110 +1,222 =7,332. Then \u00d710 is 73,320.\n\nThen 611\u00d73 is 1,833. So subtracting: 73,320 -1,833 = ?\n\nLet me subtract: 73,320 - 1,800 =71,520. Then subtract 33 more: 71,520 -33 =71,487. So same answer again. Okay, so that method also gives 71,487.\n\nHmm, that's reassuring. Let me try another way for thoroughness. Maybe using the standard vertical multiplication again but writing it step by step.\n\nMultiplying 611 by 117:\n\nFirst, write:\n\n      611  \n    \u00d7117  \n    ______  \nFirst multiply 611 by 7: 4,277  \nThen multiply 611 by 1 (tens place, so shifted one to the left): 6,110  \nThen multiply 611 by 1 (hundreds place, shifted two to the left):61,100  \nAdd them up: 4,277 + 6,110 =10,387; then 10,387 +61,100 =71,487. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using calculator-like step-by-step:\n\n611 \u00d7117. Let's see, 600\u00d7100=60,000; 600\u00d717=10,200; 11\u00d7100=1,100; 11\u00d717=187. Wait, no, that's breaking into (600+11)\u00d7(100+17). That might be more complicated, but let's try.\n\nExpanding (600 +11)(100 +17) = 600\u00d7100 +600\u00d717 +11\u00d7100 +11\u00d717.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n600\u00d7100 =60,000  \n600\u00d717: 600\u00d710=6,000; 600\u00d77=4,200 \u2192 total 10,200  \n11\u00d7100 =1,100  \n11\u00d717: 10\u00d717=170; 1\u00d717=17 \u2192 170+17=187.\n\nAdding all together: 60,000 +10,200 =70,200; plus 1,100 is 71,300; plus 187 is 71,487. Again the same answer. Okay, so that's consistent.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 71,487. Therefore, I think the answer is 71,487.\n\nWait, but just to be absolutely sure, maybe I should do the multiplication in another way, perhaps using the complement method or another approach? Not sure if that's necessary here, but maybe I can check with smaller numbers. Wait, perhaps I can divide the result by one of the numbers to see if I get the other. Let me try dividing 71,487 by 117 and see if I get 611.\n\nLet me perform 71,487 \u00f7 117. Let's see:\n\nFirst, how many times does 117 go into 714? (since 117\u00d76=702, which is less than 714; 117\u00d76=702, 117\u00d77=819 which is too much). So 6 times. 6\u00d7117=702. Subtract 702 from 714: 12. Bring down the 8, making 128.\n\nNow, how many times does 117 go into 128? Once. 1\u00d7117=117. Subtract: 128-117=11. Bring down the 7, making 117.\n\nNow, 117 goes into 117 exactly once. So the result is 6 (hundreds place), 1 (tens place), 1 (units place) \u2192 611. Exactly. So that division gives back 611, which confirms the multiplication is correct.\n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and verification through division, I can confidently say that the product of 611 and 117 is 71,487.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 611 and 117 is \\boxed{71487}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 611 and 117, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 117 into 100, 10, and 7:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 611 \\times 100 \\):\n   \\[\n   611 \\times 100 = 61100\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\( 611 \\times 10 \\):\n   \\[\n   611 \\times 10 = 6110\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\( 611 \\times 7 \\):\n   \\[\n   611 \\times 7 = 4277\n   \\]\n\nNext, add the results of these three products together:\n\\[\n61100 + 6110 + 4277\n\\]\n\nAdding these step-by-step:\n- First, add \\( 61100 \\) and \\( 6110 \\):\n  \\[\n  61100 + 6110 = 67210\n  \\]\n\n- Then, add the result to \\( 4277 \\):\n  \\[\n  67210 + 4277 = 71487\n  \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by breaking down 117 as \\( 120 - 3 \\):\n1. Calculate \\( 611 \\times 120 \\):\n   \\[\n   611 \\times 120 = 611 \\times 12 \\times 10 = 7332 \\times 10 = 73320\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\( 611 \\times 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   611 \\times 3 = 1833\n   \\]\n\n3. Subtract the two results:\n   \\[\n   73320 - 1833 = 71487\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is correct. Additionally, verifying through division:\n\\[\n71487 \\div 117 = 611\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 611 and 117 is \\(\\boxed{71487}\\).",
