since it's the tens place, it's actually 40, so you write 4 in the tens place, but since we're writing it shifted, the first digit after the initial zero is 4 in the tens place. Wait, maybe it's better to write the partial products properly.\n\nLet me try again:.\n\nFirst partial product (units digit 5):.\n\n424 × 5 = 2120.\n\nSecond partial product (tens digit 1):.\n\n424 × 1 (but since it's the tens place, it's actually 10, so 424 × 10 = 4240).\n\nThird partial product (hundreds digit 7):.\n\n424 × 7 (but since it's the hundreds place, it's actually 700, so 424 × 700 = 296,800).\n\nThen add all three partial products together: 2120 + 4240 + 296,800. Wait, that might be the way.\n\nAlternatively, in the standard algorithm, when multiplying by the tens digit, you shift one position to the left, so:.\n\nFirst line (units digit 5): 2120.\n\nSecond line (tens digit 1): 424 shifted one place (so 4240).\n\nThird line (hundreds digit 7): 424 × 7 shifted two places (since hundreds place), so 424 ×7 = 2968, then shifted two places becomes 296,800.\n\nAdding them up:.\n\n2120.\n\n+4240.\n\n= 6360. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
