      "text": "91,000 + 455 = 91,455. Wait, is that right? Let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake in the arithmetic.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do the multiplication the long way to verify. Let me set it up:\n\n\u2003\u2003455  \n\u00d7201  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \nFirst, multiply 455 by 1 (units place): 455 \u00d7 1 = 455.  \nThen, multiply 455 by 0 (tens place): 455 \u00d7 0 = 0. But since this is the tens place, we actually shift one position to the left, so it's 0 \u00d7 10 = 0.  \nNext, multiply 455 by 2 (hundreds place): 455 \u00d7 2 = 910, but since this is the hundreds place, we shift two positions, so it becomes 910 \u00d7 100 = 91,000.  \nAdding them all together: 455 (from the first step) + 0 (from the second step) + 91,000 (from the third step) = 91,455. Okay, so that matches my previous result. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, another way to check could be using the distributive property differently. Let me see if there's another approach. Maybe breaking down 455 into 400 + 55? Let's try that:\n\n455 \u00d7 201 = (400 + 55) \u00d7 201 = 400\u00d7201 + 55\u00d7201. .\n\nCalculating 400\u00d7201: 400 \u00d7 200 = 80,000 and 400 \u00d71 = 400, so total is 80,400. .\n\nThen 55\u00d7201. Hmm, let's compute that. 55\u00d7200 = 11,000 and 55\u00d71 = 55, so 11,000 +55 = 11,055. .\n\nAdding both parts: 80,400 + 11,055. Let me add 80,000 + 11,000 = 91,000, and 400 +55 = 455. So total is again 91,000 + 455 = 91,455. Same result. Okay, that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the lattice method or some other, but I think the first method is solid here. Let me confirm with a calculator approach, since I can mentally verify step by step. Wait, 201 times 455. Alternatively, 200 times 455 is 91,000. Adding 1\u00d7455 gives 91,455. So that's exactly the same as before. \n\nHmm, so all methods lead to the same result. Therefore, I think the product is indeed 91,455. I don't see any errors in my calculations. Let me just do a rough estimate to check if this is in the ballpark. 455 is approximately 500, and 201 is approximately 200. 500 \u00d7 200 = 100,000. Our answer is 91,455, which is a bit less, which makes sense because both numbers were rounded up. So 500 vs. 455 (difference of -45) and 200 vs. 201 (difference of +1). The net effect would be a slight decrease, so the actual product being 9k less than 100k makes sense. So the estimate checks out.\n\nAlternatively, if I do 455 \u00d7 200 = 91,000, and adding another 455 gives 91,455. Yep, that's exactly what I did first. So I think I'm confident that the answer is 91,455. I can't see any mistakes here. Maybe write it out in another form? Let me try the vertical multiplication again to be thorough.\n\n\u2003\u2003\u2003455  \n\u00d7\u202f\u202f201  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \n\u2003\u2003455\u202f (455 \u00d7 1)  \n\u2003\u202f0\u202f\u202f\u202f (455 \u00d7 0, shifted one position)  \n91000\u202f (455 \u00d7 2, shifted two positions)  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \nAdding these:  \n455  \n+\u202f\u202f0  \n+91000  \n= 91,455. \n\nYep, same result. I think that's solid. I don't think there's any chance I made an arithmetic mistake here because multiple methods confirm the same answer. So I'm pretty sure the product of 455 and 201 is 91,455.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 455 and 201 is \\boxed{91455}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 455 and 201, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 201 into 200 + 1:\n\n\\[\n455 \\times 201 = 455 \\times (200 + 1) = (455 \\times 200) + (455 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(455 \\times 200\\):\n\n\\[\n455 \\times 200 = 455 \\times 2 \\times 100 = 910 \\times 100 = 91000\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(455 \\times 1\\):\n\n\\[\n455 \\times 1 = 455\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\n\\[\n91000 + 455 = 91455\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 455 \\\\\n\\times 201 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\quad 455 \\quad (\\text{455 \u00d7 1}) \\\\\n  \\quad 0 \\quad \\quad (\\text{455 \u00d7 0, shifted one position}) \\\\\n+ 91000 \\quad \\quad (\\text{455 \u00d7 2, shifted two positions}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   91455 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 455 and 201 is \\(\\boxed{91455}\\).",
