      "text": "349,530?\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I missed a term. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the last terms: after adding 349,200 + 300 is 349,500. Then there's still the last term: +30. So 349,500 + 30 = 349,530. Okay, so that's 349,530.\n\nBut let me verify this another way to make sure I didn't make an addition mistake. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nLet me write it out vertically:\n\n       955\n     x 366\n     ______\n\nFirst, multiply 955 by 6 (the units place of 366):\n\n955 x 6: 900*6=5,400; 50*6=300; 5*6=30. Adding those gives 5,400 + 300 = 5,700 + 30 = 5,730.\n\nSo, first partial product: 5,730.\n\nNext, multiply 955 by 60 (the tens place of 366). Since it's the tens place, we'll shift one position to the left:\n\n955 x 60 = 955 x 6 x 10 = 5,730 x 10 = 57,300.\n\nSecond partial product: 57,300.\n\nThird, multiply 955 by 300 (the hundreds place of 366). Shift two positions to the left:\n\n955 x 300 = 955 x 3 x 100 = 2,865 x 100 = 286,500.\n\nThird partial product: 286,500.\n\nNow, add all three partial products together:\n\n5,730\n\n+57,300\n\n+286,500\n\nLet me add 5,730 + 57,300 first. That should be 63,030.\n\nThen, 63,030 + 286,500 = 349,530.\n\nHmm, so that's the same result as before. So that's a good sign.\n\nAlternatively, let me try another method to cross-verify. Maybe using the difference of squares or some other algebraic identity?\n\nWait, 955 and 366 don't seem to be numbers that fit into a difference of squares or anything like that. Maybe I can approximate?\n\nAlternatively, I can use another multiplication technique, like the lattice method, but that might take longer. Alternatively, maybe breaking down the numbers differently?\n\nLet me see, 955 * 366. Let me think of 366 as 300 + 60 +6, but maybe group differently?\n\nAlternatively, 955 * 366 = 955*(350 +16) = 955*350 + 955*16.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nFirst, 955 * 350.\n\n350 is 35*10, so 955*35*10.\n\nCompute 955 * 35 first.\n\n955 *35: 955*30 =28,650 and 955*5=4,775. Adding those gives 28,650 +4,775 = 33,425. Then multiply by 10 to get 334,250.\n\nThen 955*16.\n\n955*10=9,550; 955*6=5,730. Adding those gives 9,550 +5,730 =15,280.\n\nAdding both parts: 334,250 +15,280 = 349,530. Same result again.\n\nHmm, so three different methods give me the same answer. That's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? Wait, but since this is a problem-solving exercise, I have to do it manually. But given that all my manual methods give 349,530, I think that's the correct answer.\n\nWait, let me check once more with another breakdown.\n\nExpressing 955 as 1000 -45. Then, 955*366 = (1000 -45)*366 = 1000*366 -45*366.\n\nCompute 1000*366 = 366,000.\n\nNow compute 45*366. Let's break that down:\n\n45*300 =13,500.\n\n45*60=2,700.\n\n45*6=270.\n\nAdding those:13,500+2,700=16,200; 16,200 +270=16,470.\n\nTherefore, 366,000 -16,470 = 366,000 -16,000 = 350,000; then subtract 470 more: 350,000 -470 = 349,530. Yep, same result again.\n\nOkay, so that's four different methods all leading to 349,530. That gives me confidence.\n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't make an arithmetic mistake in any step. Let me check one of the steps again. Let's take the first method where I broke down both numbers into hundreds, tens, and units.\n\nFirst, multiplying 900 by 300: 900*300. 9*3=27, and then 000*00, so total 270,000. That's correct.\n\n900*60: 9*6=54, then three zeros and one zero, so 54,000. Correct.\n\n900*6: 9*6=54, plus three zeros (since 900 is three zeros), so 5,400. Correct.\n\nThen 50*300: 5*3=15, plus three zeros, so 15,000. Correct.\n\n50*60: 5*6=30, plus two zeros, so 3,000. Correct.\n\n50*6: 5*6=30, plus one zero, so 300. Correct.\n\n5*300: 5*3=15, plus three zeros: 1,500. Correct.\n\n5*60: 5*6=30, plus two zeros: 300. Correct.\n\n5*6: 30. Correct.\n\nAdding all those:\n\n270,000\n\n+54,000 \u2192 324,000\n\n+5,400 \u2192 329,400\n\n+15,000 \u2192 344,400\n\n+3,000 \u2192 347,400\n\n+300 \u2192 347,700\n\n+1,500 \u2192 349,200\n\n+300 \u2192 349,500\n\n+30 \u2192 349,530.\n\nYes, each step adds up correctly. So that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, the standard algorithm:\n\n955\n\nx366\n\nFirst partial product: 955*6=5,730. Correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 955*60=57,300. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 955*300=286,500. Correct.\n\nAdding those three gives 5,730 +57,300 =63,030 +286,500 =349,530. Correct.\n\nAnother way with 1000-45: 1000*366=366,000. 45*366=16,470. 366,000-16,470=349,530. Correct.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is 349,530. I can't find any errors in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm it. So I'm confident that's the correct product.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 955 and 366 is \\boxed{349530}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 955 and 366, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify using different methods:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers using the distributive property:**\n   - Break down 955 into 900, 50, and 5.\n   - Break down 366 into 300, 60, and 6.\n   - Multiply each part of 955 by each part of 366 and sum the results:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{align*}\n     900 \\times 300 & = 270000, \\\\\n     900 \\times 60 & = 54000, \\\\\n     900 \\times 6 & = 5400, \\\\\n     50 \\times 300 & = 15000, \\\\\n     50 \\times 60 & = 3000, \\\\\n     50 \\times 6 & = 300, \\\\\n     5 \\times 300 & = 1500, \\\\\n     5 \\times 60 & = 300, \\\\\n     5 \\times 6 & = 30.\n     \\end{align*}\n     \\]\n   - Summing these products:\n     \\[\n     270000 + 54000 + 5400 + 15000 + 3000 + 300 + 1500 + 300 + 30 = 349530.\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 955 by 6 (units place): \\(955 \\times 6 = 5730\\).\n   - Multiply 955 by 60 (tens place): \\(955 \\times 60 = 57300\\).\n   - Multiply 955 by 300 (hundreds place): \\(955 \\times 300 = 286500\\).\n   - Sum these partial products:\n     \\[\n     5730 + 57300 + 286500 = 349530.\n     \\]\n\n3. **Using the difference of squares method:**\n   - Express 955 as