ybe using another distributive approach. For instance, breaking down both numbers? \n\nAlternatively, maybe 790 can be written as 800 - 10. Let's see if that helps. \n\nSo, 790 = 800 - 10. Then, 790 \u00d7 198 = (800 - 10) \u00d7 198 = 800\u00d7198 - 10\u00d7198. \n\nHmm, let's see if this is easier. \n\nFirst, compute 800 \u00d7 198. \n\n800 \u00d7 198: 8 \u00d7 198 = 1,584, so 800 \u00d7 198 = 158,400. \n\nThen, 10 \u00d7 198 = 1,980. \n\nSo subtracting: 158,400 - 1,980 = ?\n\n158,400 minus 1,000 is 157,400. Then subtract another 980: 157,400 - 980. \n\n157,400 minus 1,000 would be 156,400, but since it's only 980, add back 20: 156,400 + 20 = 156,420. \n\nSame result again. That's reassuring. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm but in another way. Let me see. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use the fact that 198 is 200 - 2, as I did first. So 790 \u00d7 200 = 158,000, minus 790 \u00d7 2 = 1,580, so 158,000 - 1,580 = 156,420. \n\nSame answer. \n\nHmm, so all three methods are giving me the same result, which is 156,420. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me check with a calculator-like approach. \n\nLet me compute 790 \u00d7 198. \n\nFirst, 700 \u00d7 200 = 140,000. But that's an overestimation. Wait, maybe breaking down both numbers. \n\nAlternatively, 790 \u00d7 200 = 158,000, then subtract 790 \u00d7 2 = 1,580. \n\nYes, that's the first method again. \n\nAlternatively, maybe adding 790 multiplied by 200 and then subtracting twice 790. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps using logarithms? Probably overkill here, but let me see. Wait, that might be more complicated. \n\nAlternatively, let me just verify the multiplication step-by-step again. \n\nMultiplying 790 by 198. \n\nLet me write it vertically:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0790  \n\u00d7\u00a0198  \n________\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of 198, which is 8:\n\nMultiply 790 by 8:  \n8 \u00d7 0 = 0  \n8 \u00d7 9 = 72, write down 2 and carryover 7  \n8 \u00d7 7 = 56, plus the carried over 7 = 63. So the first partial product is 6,320. \n\nNext, the middle digit is 9 (tens place), so multiply by 10:\n\nMultiply 790 by 9:  \n9 \u00d7 0 = 0  \n9 \u00d7 9 = 81, write 1, carryover 8  \n9 \u00d7 7 = 63 + 8 = 71. So that's 7,110. But since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left, making it 71,100. \n\nThird digit is 1 (hundreds place), so multiply by 100:\n\n790 \u00d7 1 = 790, shifted two places to the left: 79,000. \n\nAdding them all together: \n\n6,320  \n+71,100  \n=77,420  \n+79,000  \n=156,420. \n\nYes, that's consistent. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can do a quick estimation to see if 156,420 makes sense. \n\nLet me approximate 790 to 800 and 198 to 200. \n\n800 \u00d7 200 = 160,000. \n\nOur computed result is 156,420, which is 3,580 less than 160,000. \n\nSince we rounded both numbers up, the exact result should be lower, which it is. The difference is 800\u00d7200 - 790\u00d7198 = 160,000 - 156,420 = 3,580. \n\nAlternatively, using the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. Wait, but perhaps that complicates more. \n\nAlternatively, think of the difference: \n\n(800 - 10)(200 - 2) = 800\u00d7200 - 800\u00d72 -10\u00d7200 +10\u00d72 = 160,000 - 1,600 - 2,000 + 20 = 160,000 - 3,600 +20 = 156,420. \n\nHmm, that's another way to compute it. \n\nBreaking it down as (a - b)(c - d), but in this case, it's (800 -10)(200 -2). \n\nSo expanding, that's 800\u00d7200 minus 800\u00d72 minus 10\u00d7200 plus 10\u00d72. \n\nSo, 160,000 - 1,600 - 2,000 + 20. \n\nCalculating that step by step: \n\n160,000 minus 1,600 is 158,400. \n\n158,400 minus 2,000 is 156,400. \n\n156,400 plus 20 is 156,420. \n\nSame result. \n\nTherefore, regardless of the method, the answer is consistent. \n\nI think that's thorough enough. All methods lead to 156,420, so I'm confident that's the correct product of 790 and 198. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 790 and 198 is \\boxed{156420}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 790 and 198, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 198 into 200 - 2:\n\n\\[\n790 \\times 198 = 790 \\times (200 - 2)\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n790 \\times 200 - 790 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(790 \\times 200\\):\n\n\\[\n790 \\times 200 = 790 \\times 2 \\times 100 = 1580 \\times 100 = 158000\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(790 \\times 2\\):\n\n\\[\n790 \\times 2 = 1580\n\\]\n\nSubtract the second result from the first:\n\n\\[\n158000 - 1580 = 156420\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 790 \\\\\n\\times 198 \\\\\n\\hline\n   6320 \\quad (\\text{790} \\times 8) \\\\\n  71100 \\quad (\\text{790} \\times 90) \\\\\n+79000 \\quad (\\text{790} \\times 100) \\\\\n\\hline\n  156420 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the partial products:\n\n\\[\n6320 + 71100 = 77420\n\\]\n\\[\n77420 + 79000 = 156420\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. Therefore, the product of 790 and 198 is \\(\\boxed{156420}\\).",
