actually 1000x138, which is 138,000. Hmm, perhaps my estimation is conflicting, but anyway, since the exact calculation shows 133,860, which is 140,000 - approx 6,140. Yeah, seems reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, 970x138: 1000x138=138,000, so subtract 30x138=4,140, so 138,000 -4,140=133,860. Yep, that's the same as before. So that's another way. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe even use logarithms? Probably overkill, but just for fun.\n\nWait, taking log(970) + log(138). Let's see:\n\nlog10(970)= log10(9.7x10^2)=2 + log10(9.7). Log10(9.7)\u22480.9868. So total\u22482.9868.\n\nlog10(138)=log10(1.38x10^2)=2 + log10(1.38). Log10(1.38)\u22480.1399. So total\u22482.1399.\n\nAdding the two logs: 2.9868 +2.1399\u22485.1267.\n\nSo the log of the product is ~5.1267. Then 10^5.1267\u224810^0.1267 x10^5.\n\n10^0.1267: since 10^0.12= approx 1.32, and 10^0.13\u22481.348. Let me compute 10^0.1267.\n\nLet me recall that log10(1.35)=0.1303. Since 0.1267 is a bit less than that, so maybe 1.35^(some?) Alternatively, use natural logs?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should just accept that this might not be precise. But 10^0.1267 is approximately 1.333 (since 10^(log10(1.333))=1.333). Let me check:\n\nlog10(1.333)= log10(4/3)= log10(4)-log10(3)=0.60206 -0.4771\u22480.1249. Close to 0.1267. So 10^0.1267\u2248~1.335.\n\nThus, total is approx 1.335 x10^5 =133,500. Which is close to our exact answer of 133,860. Hmm, so that's within a thousand, which is reasonable for an estimation. So that gives me more confidence.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps an even rougher estimate: 970 is 1000-30, so 1000*138=138,000, minus 30*138=4,140, so 138,000-4,140=133,860. Exactly, so that's the same as before.\n\nTherefore, I think 133,860 is the correct product. I don't see any errors in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm the same result. So I feel confident that this is the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 970 and 138 is \\boxed{133860}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 970 and 138, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify it with different methods:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 970 by 8 (the units place of 138):\n     \\[\n     970 \\times 8 = 7760\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 970 by 30 (the tens place of 138):\n     \\[\n     970 \\times 30 = 29100\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 970 by 100 (the hundreds place of 138):\n     \\[\n     970 \\times 100 = 97000\n     \\]\n   - Add the results together:\n     \\[\n     7760 + 29100 + 97000 = 133860\n     \\]\n\n2. **Verification using the distributive property:**\n   - Express 138 as \\(140 - 2\\):\n     \\[\n     970 \\times (140 - 2) = 970 \\times 140 - 970 \\times 2\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     970 \\times 140 = 135800 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 970 \\times 2 = 1940\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the second term from the first:\n     \\[\n     135800 - 1940 = 133860\n     \\]\n\n3. **Alternative Verification:**\n   - Express 970 as \\(1000 - 30\\):\n     \\[\n     (1000 - 30) \\times 138 = 1000 \\times 138 - 30 \\times 138\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     1000 \\times 138 = 138000 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 30 \\times 138 = 4140\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the second term from the first:\n     \\[\n     138000 - 4140 = 133860\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 970 and 138 is \\(\\boxed{133860}\\).",
