natively, maybe using the standard algorithm again but more carefully:\n\n       280\n     \u00d7259\n     -------\n        2520  (280\u00d79)\n       14000  (280\u00d750, shifted one position)\n      56000   (280\u00d7200, shifted two positions)\n     ---------\n      72520\n\nWait, but when you add them vertically:\n\nThe first partial product is 2520 (units place).\nSecond is 14,000 (tens place, so shifted one to the left, so aligned at the tens place? Wait, actually, in the standard algorithm, when multiplying by the tens digit, the second partial product is shifted one to the left, so it's actually 14,000 (since 280\u00d750 is 14,000, written with a zero in the units place). Wait, actually, when you do it step by step:\n\nMultiplying 280 \u00d7 259:\n\nStart with the 9 (units digit):\n\n280\n\u00d7  9\n-----\n2520 (since 9\u00d70=0, 9\u00d78=72, etc.)\n\nThen multiply by the 5 (tens digit):\n\n280\n\u00d7 5\n----\n1400, but shifted one to the left (because it's the tens place), so it's 14,000.\n\nThen multiply by the 2 (hundreds digit):\n\n280\n\u00d7 2\n----\n560, shifted two places to the left (since it's the hundreds digit), so 56,000.\n\nAdding all three:\n\n2520\n14000\n56000\n------\n2520 +14000 = 16520; 16520 +56000 = 72520. So that's correct.\n\nHmm, okay, so all methods are giving me 72,520. So maybe I was just doubting myself unnecessarily. Wait, but let me check with a calculator approach, perhaps approximate to see if it's reasonable.\n\n280 \u00d7 259. Let me approximate. 280 is near 300, 259 is near 250. So 300\u00d7250 = 75,000. The actual numbers are a bit less: 280 is 20 less than 300, and 259 is 1 more than 250. So the approximate difference: (300-20)*(250+1) = 300\u00d7250 +300\u00d71 -20\u00d7250 -20\u00d71 = 75,000 +300 -5,000 -20 = 75,000 -4,720 = 70,280? Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe my approximation is off.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps better approximation: 280 \u00d7259 = 280\u00d7(260 -1) = 280\u00d7260 -280. 280\u00d7260 is 280\u00d7200 +280\u00d760 = 56,000 +16,800 = 72,800. Then subtract 280 gives 72,520, which matches. So that's good.\n\nAlternatively, another way: 259 \u00d7 280 = 259 \u00d7 28 \u00d710. Let's compute 259 \u00d728 first, then multiply by 10.\n\n259 \u00d728: Break it into 200\u00d728 +50\u00d728 +9\u00d728.\n\n200\u00d728=5,600.\n\n50\u00d728=1,400.\n\n9\u00d728=252.\n\nAdding up: 5,600 +1,400 =7,000; 7,000 +252 =7,252. Then multiply by 10 gives 72,520. Yep, same answer again.\n\nAlright, so all methods are confirming that the product is 72,520. Therefore, I think that's the correct answer. I don't see any mistakes in the calculations now that I've checked them multiple times with different methods. Maybe initially I was just second-guessing because of the large numbers, but the cross-verification helps.\n\nAnother way to check could be using prime factors, but that might be more time-consuming. Let me see.\n\nFactor 280: 280 = 2\u00d7140 = 2\u00d72\u00d770 = 2\u00d72\u00d72\u00d735 = 2^3 \u00d75\u00d77.\n\nFactor 259: Let's see, 259 divided by 7 is approximately 37, 7\u00d737 is 259? 7\u00d730=210, 7\u00d737=259. Yes, 7\u00d737=259. Is 37 a prime? Yes, 37 is a prime number. So 259 =7\u00d737.\n\nTherefore, multiplying 280 \u00d7259 = (2^3 \u00d75\u00d77) \u00d7 (7\u00d737) = 2^3 \u00d75 \u00d77^2 \u00d737.\n\nCalculating that: 2^3 =8, 8\u00d75=40, 40\u00d77\u00b2=40\u00d749=1,960, 1,960\u00d737. Let me compute 1,960 \u00d737:\n\nFirst, 1,960 \u00d730 =58,800.\n\nThen 1,960 \u00d77=13,720.\n\nAdding them together: 58,800 +13,720 =72,520. Yep, same result. So that's another confirmation.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident that the product is 72,520. I think that's solid. No mistakes detected in the steps now. So the answer is 72,520.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 280 and 259 is \\boxed{72520}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 280 and 259, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify with alternative methods.\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 280 by 9 (units place):\n     \\[\n     280 \\times 9 = 2520\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 280 by 50 (tens place) and shift one position left:\n     \\[\n     280 \\times 50 = 14000\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 280 by 200 (hundreds place) and shift two positions left:\n     \\[\n     280 \\times 200 = 56000\n     \\]\n   - Add all partial products:\n     \\[\n     2520 + 14000 + 56000 = 72520\n     \\]\n\n2. **Verification using Distributive Property:**\n   - Break down 259 as \\(260 - 1\\):\n     \\[\n     280 \\times (260 - 1) = 280 \\times 260 - 280 \\times 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     280 \\times 260 = 280 \\times (200 + 60) = 56000 + 16800 = 72800\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     72800 - 280 = 72520\n     \\]\n\n3. **Prime Factorization Method:**\n   - Factorize the numbers:\n     \\[\n     280 = 2^3 \\times 5 \\times 7\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     259 = 7 \\times 37\n     \\]\n   - Multiply the factors:\n     \\[\n     (2^3 \\times 5 \\times 7) \\times (7 \\times 37) = 2^3 \\times 5 \\times 7^2 \\times 37\n     \\]\n   - Calculate step-by-step:\n     \\[\n     2^3 = 8, \\quad 8 \\times 5 = 40, \\quad 40 \\times 49 = 1960, \\quad 1960 \\times 37 = 72520\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 280 and 259 is \\(\\boxed{72520}\\).",
