write7 carryover6.\n\n5\u00d77=35 +6=41. So 4172. Then times 10 is 41,720. That's correct.\n\nThird partial product: 596\u00d7200 =596\u00d72\u00d7100=1192\u00d7100=119,200. Correct.\n\nSo adding 1,788 +41,720 +119,200.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, 1,788 + 41,720.\n\n1,788 + 41,720:\n\n1,788 + 40,000 =41,788\n\n41,788 +1,720 =43,508? Wait, no, that can't be. Wait, 1,788 +41,720:\n\nBreak it down:\n\n1,788 + 40,000 =41,788\n\nThen add 1,720 (since 41,720 is 40k +1,720? Wait, no, 41,720 is 40,000 +1,720? Wait no, 41,720 is 4*10,000 +1*1,000 +7*100 +20. So adding 1,788 to 41,720:\n\nLet me do it properly:\n\n41,720.\n\n+ 1,788.\n\n=?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 +8 =8.\n\n2 +8 =10. Write 0 carryover1.\n\n7 +7 +1 =15. Write 5 carryover1.\n\n1 +4 (wait, the thousands place: in 41,720, the thousands digit is 1 (the second digit from the left is 1, so thousands place is 1). So 1 (thousands) +1 (from the carried over?) Wait, perhaps better to write it out:\n\n41,720\n\n+ 1,788\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:0 +8 =8\n\nTens:2 +8=10 \u2192 0, carryover1\n\nHundreds:7 +7=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nThousands:1 +1=2 +1=3, carryover0\n\nTen thousands:4 +0=4\n\nSo total is 43,508.\n\nWait, so 41,720 +1,788 =43,508.\n\nThen add the third partial product: 119,200.\n\n43,508 +119,200.\n\nAdding these:\n\n43,508 +100,000 =143,508\n\n143,508 +19,200 =162,708.\n\nSo that's the same as before. So the total is 162,708.\n\nHmm, but let me check using another method to be sure. Maybe using the distributive property.\n\n596 \u00d7273 =596 \u00d7(200 +70 +3) =596\u00d7200 +596\u00d770 +596\u00d73, which is exactly what I did. So that's the same as the partial products.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can do 600 \u00d7273 and subtract 4\u00d7273.\n\nLet me try this approach for cross-verification.\n\n600 \u00d7273: 600\u00d7200=120,000; 600\u00d770=42,000; 600\u00d73=1,800. So total 120,000 +42,000=162,000 +1,800=163,800.\n\nThen subtract 4\u00d7273: 4\u00