      "text": "88,000 + 830 = 88,830. Hmm, is that right?\n\nWait a second, let me verify using another method to be sure. Maybe the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n      846\n    \u00d7 105\n    -------\nFirst, multiply 846 by 5 (the units place of 105). 846\u00d75=4,230. Then multiply 846 by 0 (the tens place of 105), which would be 0, but shifted one position to the left, so 0000. Then multiply 846 by 1 (the hundreds place of 105), which is 846 shifted two positions to the left, so 846\u00d7100=84,600. Now add those two results: 4,230 + 84,600. That's the same as before, which totals to 88,830. Okay, that's consistent. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another approach. Let me think. Maybe breaking down 105 into 100 + 5 was the right move. Alternatively, maybe there's a way to do 846 \u00d7 100 plus 846 \u00d7 5, which is exactly what I did. So that's good. \n\nWait, another way to check could be to do the multiplication all in one step. Let's see:\n\nMultiply 846 by 5: 846\u00d75=4,230. \n\nMultiply 846 by 100: 846\u00d7100=84,600. \n\nAdding them together gives 84,600 + 4,230. Let me add the thousands first: 84,600 + 4,000 = 88,600. Then add the remaining 230: 88,600 + 230 = 88,830. Yep, same result.\n\nHmm, maybe I can also check using estimation to see if this makes sense. If I round 846 to 850 and 105 to 100, then 850\u00d7100=85,000. Since I rounded both numbers down, the actual result should be a bit higher. 88,830 is indeed higher, so that seems plausible. \n\nAlternatively, if I round 846 to 800 and 105 to 100, that's 80,000, which is lower, so again 88k is in the right ballpark. \n\nAnother way to verify is to use the distributive property in a different way. Let me see:\n\n846 \u00d7 105 = 846 \u00d7 (100 + 5) = 846\u00d7100 + 846\u00d75. Yep, that's exactly what I did, so that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the commutative property, multiply 105 by 846. Let me try that:\n\n105 \u00d7 800 = 84,000\n\n105