88}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 124 and 912, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy.\n\n### Method 1: Distributive Property\nBreak down the numbers using the distributive property:\n\\[\n124 \\times 912 = 124 \\times (900 + 12)\n\\]\nCalculate each part separately:\n\\[\n124 \\times 900 = 111,600\n\\]\n\\[\n124 \\times 12 = 1,488\n\\]\nAdd the results:\n\\[\n111,600 + 1,488 = 113,088\n\\]\n\n### Method 2: Standard Algorithm\nMultiply 912 by each digit of 124 and sum the results:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   912 \\\\\n\\times 124 \\\\\n\\hline\n   3648 \\quad (\\text{912} \\times 4) \\\\\n  18240 \\quad (\\text{912} \\times 20) \\\\\n+91200 \\quad (\\text{912} \\times 100) \\\\\n\\hline\n  113088 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nAdding these results:\n\\[\n3,648 + 18,240 + 91,200 = 113,088\n\\]\n\n### Method 3: Expanded Form\nBreak down both numbers into smaller components:\n\\[\n(100 + 24) \\times (900 + 12)\n\\]\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n100 \\times 900 = 90,000\n\\]\n\\[\n100 \\times 12 = 1,200\n\\]\n\\[\n24 \\times 900 = 21,600\n\\]\n\\[\n24 \\times 12 = 288\n\\]\nSum all terms:\n\\[\n90,000 + 1,200 + 21,600 + 288 = 113,088\n\\]\n\n### Method 4: Alternative Expanded Form\nBreak down 124 and 912 differently:\n\\[\n(120 + 4) \\times (900 + 12)\n\\]\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n120 \\times 900 = 108,000\n\\]\n\\[\n120 \\times 12 = 1,440\n\\]\n\\[\n4 \\times 900 = 3,600\n\\]\n\\[\n4 \\times 12 = 48\n\\]\nSum all terms:\n\\[\n108,000 + 1,440 + 3,600 + 48 = 113,088\n\\]\n\nAll methods consistently yield the same result. Therefore, the product of 124 and 912 is \\(\\boxed{113088}\\).",
