messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 967 and 691?', "<think>Okay, so I need to multiply 967 by 691. Hmm, let me think about the best way to do this. I remember that multiplying large numbers can be done using the standard algorithm, where you break it down into parts. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write down the two numbers vertically, aligning them by their place values. So,.\n\n      967.\n    × 691.\n----------.\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the bottom number, which is 1. Multiply 1 by each digit of 967 from right to left. .\n\n1 × 7 is 7, so I write down 7. Then 1 × 6 is 6, and 1 × 9 is 9. So the first partial product is 967.\n\nNext, move to the second digit from the right in 691, which is 9. But since this is the tens place, I need to remember to shift one position to the left. So I'll write a 0 in the ones place for the next line.\n\nNow, multiplying 9 by each digit of 967:.\n\n9 × 7 is 63. I write down 3 and carry over 6.\n\n9 × 6 is 54, plus the carried over 6 is 60. So I write 0 and carry over 6 again.\n\n9 × 9 is 81, plus the carried over 6 is 87. So I write down 87. .\n\nPutting it all together, the second partial product is 87030 (since we shifted one place to the left).\n\nWait, hold on, let me check that again. When multiplying by the 9 (which is in the tens place), the second partial product should be 9×967 shifted one to the left. Let me recalculate that:.\n\n9 × 7 = 63, write 3, carry 6.\n\n9 × 6 = 54 + 6 = 60, write 0, carry 6.\n\n9 × 9 = 81 + 6 = 87. So it's 8703? Wait, no, because we have to shift one digit. So actually, the second partial product is 8703, but shifted one place, so it becomes 87030? Wait, no, maybe not. Let's see:.\n\nWait, when you multiply by the tens digit (9), the first partial product was 967 (from the units digit 1). The second partial product (from the 9 in the tens place) should be 9×967, which is 8703, but since it's the tens place, it's actually 8703 × 10, so 87030.