o the highest place (hundred thousands):\n\n2,855: 0 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 2 (hundreds?), Wait, hold on. Wait, 2,855 is:\n\n2 thousands, 8 hundreds, 5 tens, 5 units. So written in digits:\n\nPosition: hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, units.\n\nSo 2,855 is 0 0 2 8 5 5.\n\n39,970 is 0 3 9 9 7 0.\n\n114,200 is 1 1 4 2 0 0.\n\nSo when adding:\n\nStarting from the units column (rightmost):\n\nUnits: 5 + 0 + 0 =5.\n\nTens: 5 +7 +0 =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:8 +9 +2 =19 +1 (carryover) =20. Write 0, carryover 2.\n\nThousands:2 (from first number) +9 (from second) +4 (from third) =15 +2 (carryover) =17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:0 (first) +3 (second) +1 (third) =4 +1 (carryover) =5. Write 5, no carryover?\n\nWait, 3 (second number's ten thousands place?) Wait, wait:\n\nWait, the first number's ten thousands place is 0,\n\nsecond number's ten thousands place is 3,\n\nthird number's ten thousands place is 1 (since 114,200 has 1 in the hundred thousands, 1 in ten thousands, and 4 in thousands). Wait, 114,200: the first digit is 1 (hundred thousands), then 1 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), etc. So ten thousands place is 1.\n\nTherefore, ten thousands column: 0 +3 +1 =4, plus the carryover 1 from thousands column gives 5. So write 5, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands column: 0 (first) +0 (second) +1 (third) =1.\n\nSo putting it all together from right to left:\n\nUnits:5,\n\nTens:2,\n\nHundreds:0,\n\nThousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nSo the total is 157,025.\n\nWait, so the sum is 157,025. Hmm, but let me confirm with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can compute 571 \u00d7275 using another approach to verify. Maybe breaking down the numbers into smaller components.\n\nLet me try using distributive property: 571 \u00d7 275 = 571 \u00d7 (200 +70 +5) =571\u00d7200 +571\u00d770 +571\u00d75. Which is exactly what I did before, so that gives the same partial products. So 571\u00d7200=114,200, 571\u00d770=39,970, and 571\u00d75=2,855. Adding them gives 114,200 +39,970 = 154,170 +2,855 =157,025. Yes, that's the same result. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 571 \u00d7275 as (500 +70 +1) \u00d7 (200 +70 +5). But that would be more complicated, but let me try expanding it:\n\nUsing the distributive property fully:\n\n(500 +70 +1)(200 +70 +5) =500\u00d7200 +500\u00d770 +500\u00d75 +70\u00d7200 +70\u00d770 +70\u00d75 +1\u00d7200 +1\u00d770 +1\u00d75.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n500\u00d7200 =100,000.\n\n500\u00d770=35,000.\n\n500\u00d75=2,500.\n\n70\u00d7200=14,000.\n\n70\u00d770=4,900.\n\n70\u00d75=350.\n\n1\u00d7200=200.\n\n1\u00d770=70.\n\n1\u00d75=5.\n\nNow, adding all these up:\n\nStart with 100,000.\n\n+35,000 =135,000.\n\n+2,500 =137,500.\n\n+14,000 =151,500.\n\n+4,900 =156,400.\n\n+350 =156,750.\n\n+200 =156,950.\n\n+70 =157,020.\n\n+5 =157,025.\n\nYes! So that also gives 157,025. So that confirms the result again. Therefore, the product is indeed 157,025.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use another method like the lattice method or some other, but I think that might take longer. Since two different approaches have given me the same result, I can be more confident.\n\nAlternatively, I can use estimation to check if it's in the ballpark. Let's see:\n\n571 is approximately 600, and 275 is approximately 300. 600\u00d7300=180,000. Our result is 157,025, which is less than that, which makes sense because both numbers were rounded up. Alternatively, 571 is 600-29, so 600\u00d7275=165,000, minus 29\u00d7275. Let me see:\n\nWait, 571 is 600 minus 29. So 571\u00d7275 = (600 -29)\u00d7275 =600\u00d7275 -29\u00d7275.\n\nCompute 600\u00d7275: 6\u00d7275=1,650, so times 100 is 165,000.\n\n29\u00d7275: Let's compute that. 30\u00d7275 is 8,250, minus 1\u00d7275 is 275, so 8,250 -275 =7,975.\n\nTherefore, 165,000 -7,975=157,025. Exactly the same result. So that also confirms it.\n\nTherefore, after multiple methods, I can be confident that the product of 571 and 275 is 157,025.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 571 and 275 is \\boxed{157025}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 571 and 275, we use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 571 by 5 (units place of 275):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     571 \\\\\n   \\times  \\ 5 \\\\\n   \\hline\n    2855 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Result: 2855\n\n2. **Multiply 571 by 70 (tens place of 275):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     571 \\\\\n   \\times 70 \\\\\n   \\hline\n    39970 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Result: 39970 (shifted one position to the left)\n\n3. **Multiply 571 by 200 (hundreds place of 275):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     571 \\\\\n   \\times 200 \\\\\n   \\hline\n   114200 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Result: 114200 (shifted two positions to the left)\n\n4. **Add all the partial products:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\ \\ 2855 \\\\\n    +39970 \\\\\n   +114200 \\\\\n   \\hline\n   157025 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   - Units place: \\(5 + 0 + 0 = 5\\)\n   - Tens place: \\(5 + 7 + 0 = 12\\) (write 2, carryover 1)\n   - Hundreds place: \\(8 + 9 + 2 + 1 = 20\\) (write 0, carryover 2)\n   - Thousands place: \\(2 + 9 + 4 + 2 = 17\\) (write 7, carryover 1)\n   - Ten thousands place: \\(0 + 3 + 1 + 1 = 5\\)\n   - Hundred thousands place: \\(0 + 0 + 1 = 1\\)\n\nThus, the product of 571 and 275 is \\(\\boxed{157025}\\).",
