at's exactly the same as before. Adding 582,600 +48,550 gives 631,150. So that's the same answer as before. So that seems consistent.\n\nHmm, but just to make sure, maybe I can check with another method, like using the distributive property differently. \n\nAlternatively, 971 \u00d7 650 = 971 \u00d7 (600 + 50) as I did before, so that's the same as the first method. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use some other decomposition. Let's see. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps approximate to check. Let me see. 971 is close to 1000, so 1000 \u00d7 650 = 650,000. But since 971 is 29 less than 1000, so 650 \u00d7 29 less. \n\nSo 650 \u00d7 29: Let's compute that. 650 \u00d730 = 19,500. Subtract 650 gives 19,500 - 650 = 18,850. Therefore, 650,000 - 18,850 = 631,150. That's the same result! So that's another way to cross-verify. \n\nSo that gives me confidence that 631,150 is the correct answer. \n\nAlternatively, maybe do the multiplication in another way. Let me try reversing the numbers. 650 \u00d7 971. Let's see.\n\n650 \u00d7 900 = 585,000 \n\n650 \u00d7 70 = 45,500 \n\n650 \u00d7 1 = 650 \n\nAdding them up: 585,000 + 45,500 = 630,500. Then 630,500 + 650 = 631,150. Yep, same answer again.\n\nOkay, so all methods are pointing towards 631,150. \n\nWait, but let me do the multiplication digit by digit again step by step to ensure I didn't make a mistake in any step.\n\nMultiplying 971 by 650:\n\nFirst, write it as:\n\n          971  \n        \u00d7 650  \n        ---------\n\nStart with the units digit of 650, which is 0. \n\n0 \u00d7 971 = 0. So the first partial product is 0000 (since we're in the units place).\n\nNext, the tens digit is 5. \n\n5 \u00d7 971: \n\n5 \u00d71 =5 (units place, but since it's the tens digit, this will be in the tens place. Wait, actually, when multiplying by the tens digit, the result is shifted one place to the left.\n\nWait, perhaps better to compute step by step:\n\n5 \u00d7 1 (units digit of 971): 5\u00d71=5, write down 5 in the tens place (since it's shifted). Wait, actually, when multiplying by the tens digit (5), each digit's product is shifted one place to the left. Let me do it properly:\n\n5 \u00d71 (units digit of the top number) is 5, so write down 5 in the tens place (since this is the tens multiplication). \n\nThen 5 \u00d77 (tens digit of top number): 5\u00d77=35. So write down 5 in the hundreds place and carry over 3.\n\nThen 5 \u00d79 (hundreds digit of top number): 5\u00d79=45, plus the carried over 3 is 48. So write 8 in the thousands place and 4 in the ten-thousands place. \n\nWait, so the partial product is 4855, but shifted one place to the left (because it's the tens digit), so it becomes 48,550. That's correct.\n\nNext, the hundreds digit of 650 is 6. Multiply 6 \u00d7971, shifted two places to the left (since it's the hundreds place).\n\n6 \u00d71 (units digit):6, so write 6 in the hundreds place (since shifted two places). \n\n6 \u00d77 (tens digit):6\u00d77=42. Write 2 in the thousands place, carry over 4.\n\n6 \u00d79 (hundreds digit):6\u00d79=54, plus 4 is 58. So write 8 in the ten-thousands place and 5 in the hundred-thousands place. \n\nThus, the partial product is 5826 shifted two places, so 582,600. \n\nAdding the two partial products:\n\n582,600  \n+  48,550  \n= 631,150.\n\nYes, that's consistent again. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps use a calculator for verification, but since I can't do that here, but given that multiple methods give me the same result, I can be confident.\n\nWait, another way: 971 \u00d7 650. Let me think of 971 \u00d7 65 \u00d7 10. Let me compute 971 \u00d765 first, then multiply by 10.\n\nCalculating 971 \u00d765:\n\nBreakdown: 971 \u00d760 + 971 \u00d75.\n\n971 \u00d760: 971\u00d76\u00d710. 971\u00d76 is 5,826 (as before), so \u00d710 is 58,260.\n\n971\u00d75 is 4,855 (as before).\n\nAdding 58,260 + 4,855: 58,260 +4,000 =62,260; 62,260 +855=63,115. \n\nThen multiply by 10: 63,115 \u00d710 =631,150. \n\nSame result. \n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and cross-verifying each step, I think the product of 971 and 650 is definitely 631,150.\n\nJust to recap all steps once again:\n\nMethod 1: Breaking 650 into 600+50:\n\n971\u00d7600 = (971\u00d76)\u00d7100 = 5,826 \u00d7100 =582,600.\n\n971\u00d750 = (971\u00d75)\u00d710 =4,855 \u00d710=48,550.\n\nAdding these gives 582,600 +48,550=631,150.\n\nMethod 2: Standard multiplication algorithm:\n\nPartial products 0 (from units digit), 48,550 (from tens digit), 582,600 (from hundreds digit). Sum is same.\n\nMethod3: Using approximation with 1000-29:\n\n(1000-29)\u00d7650 =1000\u00d7650 -29\u00d7650 =650,000 -18,850=631,150.\n\nMethod4: Reversing the multiplication (650\u00d7971):\n\n650\u00d7900=585,000; 650\u00d770=45,500; 650\u00d71=650. Total 631,150.\n\nMethod5: Breaking 650 into 65\u00d710, compute 971\u00d765 first, then \u00d710.\n\nAll methods give same answer, so I think it's safe to conclude that 971 multiplied by 650 is 631,150.\n\nI don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and cross-verification through different methods solidifies that the result is correct. Therefore, the final answer should be 631,150.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 971 and 650 is \\boxed{631150}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 971 and 650, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n### Method 1: Breaking Down 650 into 600 and 50\n1. Calculate \\(971 \\times 600\\):\n   \\[\n   971 \\times 6 = 5826 \\implies 5826 \\times 100 = 582600\n   \\]\n2. Calculate \\(971 \\times 50\\):\n   \\[\n   971 \\times 5 = 4855 \\implies 4855 \\times 10 = 48550\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   582600 + 48550 = 631150\n   \\]\n\n### Method 2: Standard Multiplication Algorithm\n1. Multiply 971 by 0 (units place):\n   \\[\n   971 \\times 0 = 0\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 971 by 5 (tens place, shifted one position left):\n   \\[\n   971 \\times 5 = 4855 \\implies 4855 \\times 10 = 48550\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 971 by 6 (hundreds place, shifted two positions left):\n   \\[\n   971 \\times 6 = 5826 \\implies 5826 \\times 100 = 582600\n   \\]\n4. Add the partial products:\n   \\[\n   582600 + 48550 = 631150\n   \\]\n\n### Method 3: Approximation with 1000 - 29\n1. Calculate \\(1000 \\times 650\\):\n   \\[\n   1000 \\times 650 = 650000\n   \\]\n2. Subtract the product of 29 and 650:\n   \\[\n   29 \\times 650 = 18850 \\implies 650000 - 18850 = 631150\n   \\]\n\n### Method 4: Reversing the Multiplication (650 \u00d7 971)\n1. Break down 971 into 900, 70, and 1:\n   \\[\n   650 \\times 900 = 585000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   650 \\times 70 = 45500\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   650 \\times 1 = 650\n   \\]\n2. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   585000 + 45500 + 650 = 631150\n   \\]\n\n### Method 5: Breaking 650 into 65 \u00d7 10\n1. Calculate \\(971 \\times 65\\):\n   \\[\n   971 \\times 60 = 58260 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 971 \\times 5 = 4855\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   58260 + 4855 = 63115\n   \\]\n2. Multiply by 10:\n   \\[\n   63115 \\times 10 = 631150\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 971 and 650 is \\(\\boxed{631150}\\).",
