wer is 467,016.\n\nWait a second, but let me confirm once more with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use estimation to check if it's reasonable.\n\n957 is approximately 1000, and 488 is approximately 500. 1000\u00d7500 = 500,000. Our answer is 467,016, which is a bit less, which makes sense because both numbers are a bit less than 1000 and 500. So that seems plausible.\n\nAlternatively, let me do the multiplication step-by-step again without breaking into partial products, just to see.\n\nWait, the standard algorithm:\n\nMultiplying 957 by 488:\n\nFirst, multiply 957 \u00d7 8 = 7,656.\n\nThen, multiply 957 \u00d7 80 (shift one position left): 7,656 \u00d710 = 76,560.\n\nThen multiply 957 \u00d7 400 (shift two positions left): 957 \u00d74 \u00d7100 = 3,828 \u00d7100 = 382,800.\n\nAdding them up: 7,656 + 76,560 = 84,216; then 84,216 + 382,800 = 467,016. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe a calculator check? Since I don't have a calculator here, but since all the methods I've tried so far give me the same result, I can be more confident.\n\nAlternatively, let me try multiplying 488 \u00d7 957, reversing the order, maybe I can see another way.\n\nLet's try 488 \u00d7 900 = 439,200.\n\n488 \u00d7 50 = 24,400.\n\n488 \u00d77= 3,416.\n\nAdding those together:\n\n439,200 +24,400 = 463,600.\n\n463,600 +3,416 = 467,016. Same answer again.\n\nHmm, so that's consistent as well. So I think that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the product of 957 and 488 is 467,016.\n\nWait, just one last check with another method: let's use the formula (a+b)(a-b) but not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, maybe using some other algebraic identity?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 957 \u00d7 488 = (900 + 57)(400 + 88).\n\nExpanding this, we get 900\u00d7400 + 900\u00d788 +57\u00d7400 +57\u00d788.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n900\u00d7400 = 360,000.\n\n900\u00d788: 900\u00d780=72,000; 900\u00d78=7,200 \u2192 total 79,200.\n\n57\u00d7400 = 22,800.\n\n57\u00d788: Let's compute 57\u00d780=4,560 and 57\u00d78=