to the left, which is equivalent to multiplying by 10. So actually, it's 2576 × 10 = 25760. Hmm, okay, that makes sense.\n\nMoving on to the next digit in 683, which is 6 (the hundreds place). This will be shifted two places to the left, so we need to add two zeros at the end of the partial product or shift accordingly. Let's compute 6 × 322.\n\nStarting with 6 ×2 (units place): 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n6 ×2 (tens place) +1 =12 +1=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n6 ×3 (hundreds place) +1 =18 +1=19. So the partial product is 1932. But since this is the hundreds place, we need to multiply by 100, so it becomes 193200.\n\nNow, adding all the partial products together:.\n\nFirst partial product:      966.\n\nSecond partial product:   25760.\n\nThird partial product: 193200.\n\nAdding them up step by step.\n\nLet me line them up properly:.\n\n      966.\n\n    25760.\n\n  +193200.\n\n----------.\n\nFirst, add 966 and 25760.\n\n966 + 25760:.\n\nAdding units digits: 6 +0 =6.\n\nTens: 6 +6 =12, write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 9 +7 =16 +1 (carryover) =17, write 7, carryover1.\n\nThousands: 2 (from 25760) + 1 (carryover) =3.\n\nWait, actually, maybe I should write them aligned:.\n\n  25760.\n\n+   966.\n\n=?.\n\nLet me write it vertically:.\n\n   25760.\n\n+   966.\n\n--------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n0 +6 =6.\n\n6 +6 =12, write 2, carryover1.\n\n7 +9 =16 +1=17, write7, carryover1.\n\n5 +0=5 +1=6.\n\n2 remains as is.\n\nSo total is 26726.\n\nWait, let me check again:.\n\n25760.\n\n+  966.\n\n=25760 + 900 +66 =25760+900=26660 +66=26726. Yes, that's correct.\n\nNow add this result to the third partial product, which is 193200.\n\n26726 +193200.\n\nAdding these:.\n\n193200 +26726.\n\nBreak it down:.\n\n193200 +20000 =213200. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
