reds digit (2) by 5 gives 10, plus the carryover 3 gives 13, so I wrote 13, but then concatenated with the 8 and 5, thinking that was the full number. But actually, the partial product is 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 5 (ones), so 1,385. Not 13,885. So I added an extra digit there, which threw off the entire calculation. That was a big mistake.\n\nOkay, so correcting that, the first partial product is 1,385 (not 13,885). Then the second partial product was 277\u00d77 (tens place), so 277\u00d770 = 19,390. Wait, but actually in standard multiplication, when you multiply by the tens digit (7), you shift one place, so it's 277\u00d77\u00d710 = 19,390. That part was correct.\n\nThird partial product, 277\u00d74 (hundreds place) \u00d7100 =277\u00d7400=110,800. That was correct.\n\nSo adding the three partial products corrected:\n\nFirst:1,385\n\nSecond:19,390 (shifted one left)\n\nThird:110,800 (shifted two left)\n\nSo total is 1,385 +19,390 +110,800.\n\nLet me compute that step by step.\n\nFirst, 1,385 +19,390.\n\n1,385 +19,390:.\n\n1,385 +19,390 =?\n\nAdding 1,385 +19,390:\n\n1,385 + 19,390.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,000 + 19,000 =20,000.\n\n385 + 390 =775.\n\nTotal is 20,000 +775 =20,775.\n\nThen add the third partial product:20,775 +110,800 = ?\n\n20,775 +110,800 =131,575. So that matches the second method. So the correct answer should be 131,575. So my initial mistake was in the first calculation where I incorrectly wrote the first partial product as 13,885 instead of 1,385. That's a common error when doing vertical multiplication, not keeping track of the place values correctly. So the correct product is 131,575.\n\nBut let me confirm once more using another method to be absolutely sure. Let's use the distributive property again, breaking down 475 into 500 -25.\n\nWait, 475 = 500 -25. Then, 277\u00d7475 =277\u00d7(500 -25)=277\u00d7500 -277\u00d725.\n\nCompute 277\u00d7500 =138,500.\n\n277\u00d725: Let's compute that. 277\u00d725 is same as 277\u00d7(20+5)=277\u00d720 +277\u00d75 =5,540 +1,385=6,925.\n\nSo total is 138,500 -6,925 = ?\n\n138,500 -6,925:.\n\n138,500 -6,000 =132,500.\n\n132,500 -925 =131,575. Yes, same result. So that confirms it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using calculator-like step-by-step:\n\n277 \u00d7475.\n\nLet me compute 277 \u00d7 400 = 110,800.\n\n277 \u00d770=19,390.\n\n277 \u00d75=1,385.\n\nAdding them up:110,800 +19,390 =130,190; then 130,190 +1,385 =131,575. Exactly.\n\nSo the correct answer is 131,575. My initial mistake was a miscalculation in writing down the first partial product incorrectly, which led to an erroneous result. After correcting that, all methods agree. So I need to make sure I write down the partial products correctly next time, keeping track of the place values.\n\nAnother way I can check is to approximate the product to see if it's in the ballpark. 277 is approximately 300, and 475 is approximately 500. 300\u00d7500=150,000. Our answer is 131,575, which is a bit less, which makes sense because both numbers were rounded up. So that seems reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, compute 277 \u00d7 475:\n\nLet me do 277 \u00d7 475 step-by-step again carefully:\n\nFirst, write them down:\n\n```.\n    277\n\n  \u00d7475\n\n  --------\n\n```.\n\nMultiply 277 by 5 (units place):\n\n5 \u00d7 7 = 35, write down 5, carryover 3.\n\n5 \u00d77 =35 +3=38, write down 8, carryover3.\n\n5 \u00d72=10 +3=13. Write down 13. So the first partial product is 1,385. (Correct, three digits plus carryover gives a four-digit number here.)\n\nNext, multiply by 7 (tens place). Since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left.\n\n7 \u00d77 =49, write down 9, carryover4.\n\n7 \u00d77=49 +4=53, write down3, carryover5.\n\n7 \u00d72=14 +5=19. So this partial product is 19,390 (since shifted one left).\n\nThird, multiply by4 (hundreds place). Shift two positions left.\n\n4 \u00d77=28, write down 8, carryover2.\n\n4 \u00d77=28 +2=30, write down0, carryover3.\n\n4 \u00d72=8 +3=11. So this partial product is 110,800 (shifted two left).\n\nNow adding them all:\n\n1,385\n\n+19,390\n\n=20,775\n\n20,775 +110,800 =131,575. Perfect, that's consistent. So the correct answer is 131,575.\n\nTherefore, the product of 277 and 475 is 131,575.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 277 and 475 is \\boxed{131575}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 277 and 475, we use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 277 by 5 (ones place):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   5 \\times 7 &= 35 \\quad \\text{(write 5, carry over 3)} \\\\\n   5 \\times 7 &= 35 + 3 = 38 \\quad \\text{(write 8, carry over 3)} \\\\\n   5 \\times 2 &= 10 + 3 = 13 \\quad \\text{(write 13)}\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n   The first partial product is \\(1,385\\).\n\n2. **Multiply 277 by 7 (tens place), shifted one position to the left:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   7 \\times 7 &= 49 \\quad \\text{(write 9, carry over 4)} \\\\\n   7 \\times 7 &= 49 + 4 = 53 \\quad \\text{(write 3, carry over 5)} \\\\\n   7 \\times 2 &= 14 + 5 = 19 \\quad \\text{(write 19)}\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n   The second partial product is \\(19,390\\).\n\n3. **Multiply 277 by 4 (hundreds place), shifted two positions to the left:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   4 \\times 7 &= 28 \\quad \\text{(write 8, carry over 2)} \\\\\n   4 \\times 7 &= 28 + 2 = 30 \\quad \\text{(write 0, carry over 3)} \\\\\n   4 \\times 2 &= 8 + 3 = 11 \\quad \\text{(write 11)}\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n   The third partial product is \\(110,800\\).\n\n4. **Add all partial products:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   1,385 + 19,390 &= 20,775 \\\\\n   20,775 + 110,800 &= 131,575\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 277 and 475 is \\(\\boxed{131575}\\).",
