6 is 2,166. Adding those together:\n\n144,400 + 25,270 = 169,670. Then add 2,166: 169,670 + 2,166 = 171,836. Okay, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method like the lattice method? Hmm, but that might take longer. Alternatively, check with another multiplication approach.\n\nAlternatively, use the standard algorithm again but step by step.\n\nLet me write it out in the vertical multiplication:\n\nFirst, 361 \u00d7 476.\n\nStarting with 6 \u00d7 361:\n\n6 \u00d7 1 = 6.\n\n6 \u00d7 6 = 36, carryover 3.\n\n6 \u00d7 3 = 18 + 3 = 21. So 2166. That's correct.\n\nThen 7 (tens place) \u00d7 361. But since it's the tens place, we shift one to the left.\n\n7 \u00d71=7, so 7 in the tens place (i.e., 70).\n\n7\u00d76=42, so 2 in the hundreds place, carryover 4.\n\n7\u00d73=21 +4=25. So 2527, but shifted left one digit: 25270. Correct.\n\nThen 4 (hundreds place) \u00d7 361. Since it's the hundreds place, shift two left.\n\n4\u00d71=4 (hundreds place?), wait:\n\nWait, 4 (in hundreds place) \u00d7 361 is 361\u00d7400, so 4\u00d7361=1444, then \u00d7100 gives 144,400. Correct.\n\nAdding them up:\n\n2166\n\n25270\n\n144400\n\nTotal: 2166 +25270=27436; 27436+144400=171,836. Same result.\n\nHmm, so that seems consistent. Wait, but let me check with a calculator method. Wait, perhaps I can approximate to see if it's reasonable.\n\n360 \u00d7 480 = (360\u00d7400) + (360\u00d780) = 144,000 + 28,800 = 172,800. Since 361 is 1 more than 360 and 476 is 4 less than 480, so the actual product should be 172,800 - (360\u00d74) + (1\u00d7476) - (1\u00d74). Wait, this might be overcomplicating, but let me see.\n\nAlternatively, approximate difference:\n\nThe approximate is 360\u00d7480 = 172,800. The actual numbers are 361 and 476, so the difference would be (361 - 360)*(476) + (476 - 480)*360.\n\nWhich is 1\u00d7476 + (-4)*360 = 476 - 1440 = -964. So 172,800 - 964 = 171,836. Exactly the result I got. So that's a good check.\n\nTherefore, the product is indeed 171,836.\n\nWait, so I think that's correct. Let me just confirm once again using another method. Maybe breaking down 361 and 476 into smaller components.\n\nFor instance, 361 is 300 + 60 +1, and 476 is 400 +70 +6. But that's essentially the same as the standard algorithm. Alternatively, use (a+b)(c+d) = ac + ad + bc + bd, but with three terms each.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the identity (a)(b) = [(a + b)/2]^2 - [(a - b)/2]^2, but that might not be helpful here.\n\nAlternatively, 361 \u00d7 476. Let me see 361 is 19 squared (since 19\u00d719=361). Maybe that helps? Let's see:\n\nIf 361 = 19\u00b2, then 19\u00b2 \u00d7 476. Hmm, 476 divided by 19? Not sure if that simplifies. 19\u00d725 is 475, so 476 is 19\u00d725 +1, so 19\u00d725 +1. So 19\u00b2 \u00d7 (19\u00d725 +1) = 19\u00b3\u00d725 +19\u00b2. But that might not help in calculation. Let me see:\n\n19\u00b3 is 6859, so 6859\u00d725 + 361. 6859\u00d725: 6859\u00d710=68,590; \u00d720 is 137,180; plus 6859\u00d75=34,295. So 137,180 +34,295 = 171,475. Then add 361: 171,475 +361 = 171,836. Hey, that works! That gives the same answer. So that's another way to verify. So that's reassuring.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 171,836.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 361 and 476 is \\boxed{171836}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 361 and 476, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 361 by 6 (ones place):**\n   \\[\n   361 \\times 6 = 2166\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 361 by 70 (tens place):**\n   \\[\n   361 \\times 7 = 2527 \\quad \\text{(then shift one place to the left)} \\quad \\Rightarrow 25270\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 361 by 400 (hundreds place):**\n   \\[\n   361 \\times 4 = 1444 \\quad \\text{(then shift two places to the left)} \\quad \\Rightarrow 144400\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   2166 + 25270 + 144400\n   \\]\n\n   - First, add \\(2166 + 25270\\):\n     \\[\n     2166 + 25270 = 27436\n     \\]\n\n   - Then add the result to \\(144400\\):\n     \\[\n     27436 + 144400 = 171836\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 361 and 476 is \\(\\boxed{171836}\\).",
