 which simplifies the multiplication instead of doing the standard algorithm step by step. But both approaches lead to the same answer. I think that's solid. So, yeah, the answer is 45,854.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 454 and 101 can be calculated efficiently using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n454 \\times 101 = 454 \\times (100 + 1) = (454 \\times 100) + (454 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute each part:\n- \\( 454 \\times 100 = 45,\\!400 \\)\n- \\( 454 \\times 1 = 454 \\)\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n45,\\!400 + 454 = 45,\\!854\n\\]\n\n**Verification** using standard multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\hphantom{0}454 \\\\\n\\times \\hphantom{0}101 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\hphantom{0}454 \\quad (\\text{454 \u00d7 1}) \\\\\n+ \\hphantom{4540}0 \\quad \\,\\, (\\text{454 \u00d7 0, shifted left by 1}) \\\\\n+ 45,\\!400 \\quad (\\text{454 \u00d7 1, shifted left by 2}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   45,\\!854 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is consistent. \n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{45854}\\)",
