8 = 315,732  \nWait, so that gives me 315,732?\n\nBut let me confirm with another method. Let's do the subtraction digit by digit properly.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nThe number is 317,000  \nSubtracting 1,268:\n\nUnits place: 0 - 8. Need to borrow.  \nTens place is 0, so go to hundreds place (0), still 0. Thousands place is 7. So borrow 1 from thousands place (7 becomes 6). Then the hundreds place becomes 10. Then borrow 1 from hundreds to tens: hundreds becomes 9, tens becomes 10. Then borrow 1 from tens to units: tens becomes 9, units becomes 10.  \nSo units digit: 10 - 8 = 2  \nTens digit: 9 - 6 = 3  \nHundreds digit: 9 - 2 = 7  \nThousands digit: 6 (since we had borrowed 1 from the original 7) minus 1 (from the 1 in 1,268's thousands place). Wait, the number being subtracted is 1,268 which is 1 thousand, 2 hundreds, 6 tens, 8 units. So in the thousands place, the original number had 7 thousands, but after borrowing became 6 thousands. Then we subtract 1 thousand, so 6 - 1 = 5 thousands. Wait, but hold on.\n\nWait, actually, the original thousands digit was 7, but after borrowing 1 for the hundreds place, it's 6. Then when subtracting the 1 (thousands place of 1,268), we do 6 - 1 = 5. So thousands digit is 5.\n\nThen the ten thousands place: original was 1 (from 317,000: 3 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands)), but since we didn't borrow from the ten thousands, it remains 1. Wait, but actually, the hundreds digit was originally 0, so borrowing from thousands didn't affect ten thousands. So ten thousands place is still 1. However, the hundred thousands place is 3, remains 3. So putting it all together:\n\nHundred thousands: 3  \nTen thousands: 1  \nThousands: 5  \nHundreds: 7  \nTens: 3  \nUnits: 2  \nSo the result is 315,732?\n\nWait, but let me check with another method to ensure I didn't make a mistake in borrowing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe convert both numbers to another form or use another multiplication approach.\n\nAlternatively, comput