answers. \n\nOkay, so I think I'm confident that the product is 492,000. But just to be absolutely sure, maybe I can divide the answer by 1000 and see if I get back to 492. Let's try that. 492,000 \u00f7 1000. Dividing by 1000 moves the decimal three places to the left. So 492,000 becomes 492.000, which is 492. Perfect, that checks out. \n\nAnother check: 1000 \u00d7 500 would be 500,000. Since 492 is 8 less than 500, then 1000 \u00d7 8 = 8,000. So subtracting that from 500,000 gives 500,000 - 8,000 = 492,000. Yep, that works too. \n\nAlright, multiple methods confirm that the answer is 492,000. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning. So the product of 1000 and 492 is 492,000.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 1000 and 492 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Since multiplying by 1000 is equivalent to adding three zeros to the end of the number, we can do this step-by-step:\n\n1. Start with 492.\n2. Multiply by 10: \\(492 \\times 10 = 4,\\!920\\).\n3. Multiply by 10 again: \\(4,\\!920 \\times 10 = 49,\\!200\\).\n4. Multiply by 10 one more time: \\(49,\\!200 \\times 10 = 492,\\!000\\).\n\nAlternatively, directly appending three zeros to 492 gives \\(492,\\!000\\).\n\n**Answer:** \\(492,\\!000\\)",
