      "text": "347600 + 3751 = 347600 + 3000 = 350600, then +751 = 350600 + 751 = 351,351? Wait, that might be a miscalculation. Let me do it step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I should align them again:.\n\n   23751.\n+327600.\n--------.\n\nAligning the numbers properly:\n\n23751 has five digits, 327600 has six. So:\n\n    23751.\n  +327600.\n  --------.\n\nAligning the rightmost digits:.\n\n    23751.\n  +327600.\n  --------.\n  ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 1 + 0 =1.\n\nTens:5 +0=5.\n\nHundreds:7 +6=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:3 +7=10 plus the carryover 1 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands:2 +2=4 plus carryover 1=5.\n\nHundred-thousands:3 (from 327600) and nothing else. So the total would be 351,351?\n\nWait, let's do it step by step:\n\nFirst, write 23751 as:\n\n023751 (to match the digits)\n+327600\n= ?\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1 (units) + 0 =1.\n\n5 (tens) +0 =5.\n\n7 (hundreds) +6 (hundreds place of 327600)=13. So write 3, carryover 1.\n\n3 (thousands place of first number) +7 (thousands place of second number) =10, plus the 1 carried over is 11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\n2 (ten-thousands place of first number) +2 (ten-thousands of second number) =4, plus the carried 1 =5.\n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place: 0 (from first number) +3 (from second number) =3.\n\nPutting it all together: 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds?), wait let me see:\n\nWait the digits would be:\n\nHundred-thousands: 3,\n\nTen-thousands:5,\n\nThousands:1,\n\nHundreds:3,\n\nTens:5,\n\nUnits:1.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nWait, when adding:\n\nThe first number is 23751, which is:\n\nDigit 1: units:1,\n\nDigit 2: tens:5,\n\nDigit3: hundreds:7,\n\nDigit4: thousands:3,\n\nDigit5: ten-thousands:2.\n\nSecond number is 327600:\n\nDigit1: units:0,\n\nDigit2:tens:0,\n\nDigit3: hundreds:6,\n\nDigit4: thousands:7,\n\nDigit5: ten-thousands:2,\n\nDigit6: hundred-thousands:3.\n\nSo when adding:\n\nDigit1 (units):1+0=1,\n\nDigit2 (tens):5+0=5,\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):7+6=13,\n\nDigit4 (thousands):3+7=10 +1 (carryover from hundreds)=11,\n\nDigit5 (ten-thousands):2+2=4 +1 (carryover from thousands)=5,\n\nDigit6 (hundred-thousands):0+3=3.\n\nSo the sum is 351,351? Wait, let's see:\n\nHundred-thousands digit:3,\n\nten thousands:5,\n\nthousands:1,\n\nhundreds:3,\n\ntens:5,\n\nunits:1.\n\nSo the number is 351,351? Wait, but the thousands digit is 1, so it's 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 1 (units). So 351,351. Hmm, okay.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method to confirm. Let me add 23751 + 327,600.\n\n327,600 + 20,000 = 347,600,\n\n347,600 + 3,751 = 347,600 + 3,000 = 350,600, then +751 = 351,351. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the total sum of all partial products is 351,351. Therefore, 819 \u00d7 429 = 351,351?\n\nWait, but before I conclude, let me verify using another method to be sure. Maybe breaking down the numbers into smaller components?\n\nAlternatively, use the distributive property: (800 + 19) \u00d7 (400 + 29).\n\nExpanding this:\n\n800\u00d7400 + 800\u00d729 + 19\u00d7400 + 19\u00d729.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n800\u00d7400 = 320,000.\n\n800\u00d729 = 800\u00d730 - 800\u00d71 = 24,000 - 800 = 23,200.\n\n19\u00d7400 = 7,600.\n\n19\u00d729: Let's compute that. 20\u00d729 = 580, minus 1\u00d729 =29, so 580-29=551.\n\nNow, add all those together:\n\n320,000 +23,200 = 343,200,\n\n343,200 +7,600 = 350,800,\n\n350,800 +551 = 351,351.\n\nOkay, so that matches the previous result. So that's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method: 819 \u00d7 429.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the fact that 429 is 430 -1, so 819 \u00d7 (430 -1) = 819\u00d7430 -819\u00d71.\n\nCompute 819\u00d7430 first. 819\u00d7400 = 327,600. 819\u00d730=24,570. So total is 327,600 +24,570 = 352,170. Then subtract 819: 352,170 -819 = 352,170 -800 -19 = 351,370 -19 = 351,351. So that's the same result again.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 351,351. Wait, but just to be thorough, let me try the standard algorithm once again step by step to ensure I didn't make an arithmetic mistake earlier.\n\nOriginal multiplication:\n\n819 \u00d7 429.\n\nFirst, multiply 819 by 9 (units place):\n\n9\u00d79=81, write 1 carryover 8.\n\n9\u00d71=9 +8=17, write 7 carryover 1.\n\n9\u00d78=72 +1=73. So the first partial product is 7371. That's correct.\n\nNext, multiply by 2 (tens place). Since it's the tens place, we'll shift one position:\n\n2\u00d79=18, write 8 carryover 1.\n\n2\u00d71=2 +1=3, write 3.\n\n2\u00d78=16. So the partial product is 1638. But shifted one to the left, so 16,380. Correct.\n\nThird, multiply by 4 (hundreds place). Shift two positions:\n\n4\u00d79=36, write 6 carryover 3.\n\n4\u00d71=4 +3=7, write 7.\n\n4\u00d78=32. So the partial product is 3276, shifted two places gives 327,600. Correct.\n\nAdding 7371 +16,380 +327,600.\n\n7371 +16,380 = 23,751. Then 23,751 + 327,600 = 351,351. Yep, that's the same as before.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident the answer is 351,351.\n\nAlternatively, just to be extra cautious, maybe use a calculator? But since I don't have one here, let me try multiplying 819 \u00d7 429 in another way. Let's see:\n\nAnother approach: 819 \u00d7 429 = (800 +19)(400 +29). Wait, I did that already, but let me recompute those components.\n\n800\u00d7400 = 320,000.\n\n800\u00d729: 800\u00d730=24,000 minus 800\u00d71=800, so 23,200.\n\n19\u00d7400: 19\u00d74\u00d7100 = 76\u00d7100=7,600.\n\n19\u00d729: 20\u00d729=580 minus 1\u00d729=29, so 551.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n320k +23.2k = 343.2k,\n\n343.2k +7.6k = 350.8k,\n\n350.8k +0.551k = 351,351. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, 819 \u00d7 429 is the same as 429 \u00d7 819, so I can reverse the numbers if that's easier.\n\nLet me try multiplying 429 \u00d7 800 = 343,200,\n\nthen 429 \u00d719 = ?\n\n429\u00d710=4,290,\n\n429\u00d79=3,861,\n\nso 4,290 +3,861 = 8,151.\n\nThen total is 343,200 +8,151 = 351,351. Same answer again.\n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and checks, I think the product of 819 and 429 is indeed 351,351. I don't see any errors in my calculations, and all different approaches are confirming the same result. So I'm pretty sure that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 819 and 429 is \\boxed{351351}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 819 and 429, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify using different methods.\n\nFirst, we set up the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   819 \\\\\n\\times 429 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Step 1: Multiply 819 by 9 (units place):**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   819 \\\\\n\\times \\quad 9 \\\\\n\\hline\n   7371 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Multiply 819 by 20 (tens place):**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   819 \\\\\n\\times \\quad 2 \\\\\n\\hline\n   1638 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nShift one position to the left:\n\n\\[\n16380\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Multiply 819 by 400 (hundreds place):**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   819 \\\\\n\\times \\quad 4 \\\\\n\\hline\n   3276 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nShift two positions to the left:\n\n\\[\n327600\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Add all partial products:**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n    7371 \\\\\n  +16380 \\\\\n+327600 \\\\\n\\hline\n  351351 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Verification using the distributive property:**\n\n\\[\n(800 + 19)(400 + 29) = 800 \\times 400 + 800 \\times 29 + 19 \\times 400 + 19 \\times 29\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n800 \\times 400 = 320000 \\\\\n800 \\times 29 = 23200 \\\\\n19 \\times 400 = 7600 \\\\\n19 \\times 29 = 551\n\\]\n\nSumming these results:\n\n\\[\n320000 + 23200 + 7600 + 551 = 351351\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\nThe product of 819 and 429 is \\boxed{351351}.",
