y! So that confirms it. So that's a good way to check.\n\nTherefore, the product of 764 and 131 is 100,084. I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick calculator check in my mind. Alternatively, cross-multiplying:\n\nAlternatively, breaking down 764*131 as 764*(130+1)=764*130 +764*1. 764*130: 764*100=76,400; 764*30=22,920. So 76,400+22,920=99,320. Then +764 gives 99,320+764=100,084. Yep, that's the same result. So I think that's the right answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and the cross-check with estimation and alternative methods confirm it. So I'm confident the product is 100,084.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 764 and 131 is \\boxed{100084}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 764 and 131, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 131 into 100 + 30 + 1. \n\nFirst, we multiply 764 by each part separately:\n- \\( 764 \\times 100 = 76400 \\)\n- \\( 764 \\times 30 = 22920 \\) (since \\( 764 \\times 3 = 2292 \\), then multiplied by 10)\n- \\( 764 \\times 1 = 764 \\)\n\nNext, we add the results of these multiplications together:\n\\[\n76400 + 22920 + 764\n\\]\n\nAdding the first two results:\n\\[\n76400 + 22920 = 99320\n\\]\n\nThen adding the third result:\n\\[\n99320 + 764 = 100084\n\\]\n\nWe verified the result using different methods, including the standard algorithm for multiplication and estimation, confirming the result is correct.\n\nThe product of 764 and 131 is \\(\\boxed{100084}\\).",
