but let me try expanding it as (a + b)(a - b) + cross terms?\n\nWait, actually, (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd.\n\nBut perhaps use (600 + 47)(600 -4):\n\nWait, 600 -4 is 596, so yes, so:\n\nLet me let a=600, b=47, c=600, d= -4. Hmm, not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, (600 + 47)*(600 -4) = 600*600 + 600*(-4) +47*600 +47*(-4).\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n600*600 = 360,000.\n\n600*(-4) = -2,400.\n\n47*600 = 28,200.\n\n47*(-4)= -188.\n\nAdding all these together: 360,000 -2,400 = 357,600; plus 28,200 = 385,800; minus 188 gives 385,612. Exactly the same result. So that's a third method confirming the answer.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident that 647 multiplied by 596 is 385,612.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 647 and 596 is \\boxed{385612}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 647 and 596, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify the result with alternative methods.\n\n1. **Multiplying 647 by 6 (units place):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     647 \\\\\n   \\times \\ 6 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     3882 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   - \\(6 \\times 7 = 42\\) (write 2, carry over 4)\n   - \\(6 \\times 4 + 4 = 28\\) (write 8, carry over 2)\n   - \\(6 \\times 6 + 2 = 38\\)\n\n2. **Multiplying 647 by 9 (tens place, shifted left once):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     647 \\\\\n   \\times \\ 90 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     58230 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   - \\(9 \\times 7 = 63\\) (write 3, carry over 6)\n   - \\(9 \\times 4 + 6 = 42\\) (write 2, carry over 4)\n   - \\(9 \\times 6 + 4 = 58\\)\n\n3. **Multiplying 647 by 5 (hundreds place, shifted left twice):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     647 \\\\\n   \\times \\ 500 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     323500 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   - \\(5 \\times 7 = 35\\) (write 5, carry over 3)\n   - \\(5 \\times 4 + 3 = 23\\) (write 3, carry over 2)\n   - \\(5 \\times 6 + 2 = 32\\)\n\n4. **Adding the results:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     3882 \\\\\n   + 58230 \\\\\n   + 323500 \\\\\n   \\hline\n