t me try that way for another check.\n\n261 \u00d7700 = 261 \u00d77 \u00d7100. 261 \u00d77: 200\u00d77=1400, 60\u00d77=420, 1\u00d77=7. Adding: 1400+420=1820 +7=1827. Then \u00d7100 is 182,700.\n\n261 \u00d750 = 13,050 as before. So subtracting: 182,700 -13,050 = 169,650. Same result. So that works too.\n\nOkay, so using different methods, I keep getting the same answer. Therefore, I think the product of 650 and 261 is indeed 169,650. I can't see any mistakes in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm the result. So I'm confident that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 650 and 261 is \\boxed{169650}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 650 and 261, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down 650 into 600 and 50:\n   \\[\n   650 \\times 261 = (600 + 50) \\times 261\n   \\]\n\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   600 \\times 261 + 50 \\times 261\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(600 \\times 261\\):\n   \\[\n   600 \\times 261 = 6 \\times 100 \\times 261 = (6 \\times 261) \\times 100\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 261 = 1566 \\quad \\text{(since } 6 \\times 200 = 1200, \\, 6 \\times 60 = 360, \\, 6 \\times 1 = 6\\text{)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1566 \\times 100 = 156600\n   \\]\n\n4. Calculate \\(50 \\times 261\\):\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 261 = 5 \\times 10 \\times 261 = (5 \\times 261) \\times 10\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5 \\times 261 = 1305 \\quad \\text{(since } 5 \\times 200 = 1000, \\, 5 \\times 60 = 300, \\, 5 \\times 1 = 5\\text{)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1305 \\times 10 = 13050\n   \\]\n\n5. Add the results of the two parts:\n   \\[\n   156600 + 13050 = 169650\n   \\]\n\nVerification using another method (direct multiplication):\n- Multiply 650 by 1, 60, and 200 separately and sum the results:\n  \\[\n  650 \\times 1 = 650\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  650 \\times 60 = 39000\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  650 \\times 200 = 130000\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  650 + 39000 + 130000 = 169650\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm 