ns), 1 (units). So the number is 169,011. Exactly the same as before. So that's 169,011.\n\nSo all methods lead to the same answer. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 267 and 633 is 169,011. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and cross-verifying with different methods gives the same result. So yeah, I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 267 and 633 is \\boxed{169011}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 267 and 633, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers**:\n   - \\(267 = 200 + 60 + 7\\)\n   - \\(633 = 600 + 30 + 3\\)\n\n2. **Multiplying each part**:\n   - \\(200 \\times 600 = 120,000\\)\n   - \\(200 \\times 30 = 6,000\\)\n   - \\(200 \\times 3 = 600\\)\n   - \\(60 \\times 600 = 36,000\\)\n   - \\(60 \\times 30 = 1,800\\)\n   - \\(60 \\times 3 = 180\\)\n   - \\(7 \\times 600 = 4,200\\)\n   - \\(7 \\times 30 = 210\\)\n   - \\(7 \\times 3 = 21\\)\n\n3. **Summing the results**:\n   - Sum of parts from 200: \\(120,000 + 6,000 + 600 = 126,600\\)\n   - Sum of parts from 60: \\(36,000 + 1,800 + 180 = 37,980\\)\n   - Sum of parts from 7: \\(4,200 + 210 + 21 = 4,431\\)\n\n4. **Adding all components together**:\n   - \\(126,600 + 37,980 = 164,580\\)\n   - \\(164,580 + 4,431 = 169,011\\)\n\n5. **Verification using standard multiplication**:\n   - \\(267 \\times 3 = 801\\)\n   - \\(267 \\times 30 = 8,010\\)\n   - \\(267 \\times 600 = 160,200\\)\n   - Summing these: \\(801 + 8,010 + 160,200 = 169,011\\)\n\n6. **Alternative method using factors**:\n   - \\(267 \\times 633 = 9 \\times 89 \\times 211 = 169,011\\)\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 267 and 633 is \\(\\boxed{169011}\\).",
