and 700 + 540 is 1,240. So total is 110,000 + 1,240 = 111,240. Yep.  .\n\nSecond method: 927\u00d712. 927\u00d710=9,270; 927\u00d72=1,854; adding gives 11,124. Then \u00d7100 gives 1,112,400? Wait, no. Wait, hold on. Wait, no! Wait, I think I made a mistake here. Wait, hold on. Wait, this is conflicting. Wait, in the second method, when I broke it down to 927\u00d712, then multiplied by 10? Wait, original problem is 120\u00d7927, which is 927\u00d7(12\u00d710) = 927\u00d712\u00d710. Wait, so 927\u00d712 is 11,124, then multiply by 10 gives 111,240. Oh, okay, that's correct.  .\n\nWait, I think I confused myself for a second there. Yeah, so 12\u00d710 is 120, so that's correct. So, that method is okay.  .\n\nThird method: 120\u00d7900=108,000 and 120\u00d727=3,240. Adding those gives 111,240.  .\n\nSo all three methods confirm the same answer. Therefore, I think the product of 120 and 927 is indeed 111,240. I can't see any errors in my calculations, so I'm pretty confident.  .\n\nAlternatively, if I were to do straight multiplication: 927 \u00d7 120.  .\n\nLet me write it out vertically:\n\n       927  \n     \u00d7 120  \n     ------  \nFirst multiply 927 by 0 (units place of 120): 0  \nThen multiply 927 by 2 (tens place, which is actually 20): 927\u00d72=1,854, shifted one position to the left: 18,540  \nThen multiply 927 by 1 (hundreds place, which is 100): 927\u00d71=927, shifted two positions to the left: 92,700  \nNow add those three results:  \n     0  \n+18,540  \n+92,700  \n= 111,240.  \n\nExactly. That's the same as before. So, no matter how I approach it, the result is consistent. Therefore, I think the answer is 111,240.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 120 and 927 is \\boxed{111240}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 120 and 927, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   120 \\times 927 = (100 + 20) \\times 927\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 100 \\times 927 + 20 \\times 927\n   \\]\n