t me try the standard algorithm again step by step once more, just to ensure that my initial calculation was correct.\n\nFirst, multiplying 915 \u00d7 2 (units place):\n\n915 \u00d72:\n\n5\u00d72=10, write 0 carryover 1.\n\n1\u00d72=2 +1=3.\n\n9\u00d72=18.\n\nSo that gives 1830. Correct.\n\nThen multiplying 915 \u00d79 (tens place):\n\nBut since it's the tens place, so actually 90, so the result is shifted one position left.\n\n9 \u00d75 is 45, write 5 carryover4.\n\n9\u00d71=9 +4=13, write 3, carryover1.\n\n9\u00d79=81 +1=82. So that gives 8235, then shifted left by one: 82350. Correct.\n\nThen multiplying 915 \u00d72 (hundreds place):\n\nThat's 200\u00d7915, so 2\u00d7915=1830, shifted two places: 183000. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 1830 +82350 +183000.\n\n1830+82350 is 84180, plus 183,000 gives 267,180. Yep, all checks out.\n\nAnother way: Using calculator-style multiplication step-by-step.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute 915 \u00d7292:\n\nBreakdown:\n\nFirst, multiply 915 by 200: 915\u00d7200=183,000.\n\nThen 915\u00d790: 915\u00d79\u00d710=8,235\u00d710=82,350.\n\nThen 915\u00d72=1,830.\n\nAdding these three: 183,000 +82,350 =265,350; plus 1,830 gives 267,180. Same result.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident that the product of 915 and 292 is 267,180.\n\nWait a second, but let me just do a sanity check with approximate numbers to see if this is reasonable.\n\n915 is roughly 900, and 292 is roughly 300. 900\u00d7300 is 270,000. Our answer is 267,180, which is a bit less, which makes sense because we rounded both numbers up. Since 915 is 15 more than 900, and 292 is 8 less than 300. The difference would be (900\u00d7300) - (900\u00d78 + 15\u00d7300 - 15\u00d78). Hmm, maybe that's complicating, but roughly, 270,000 minus (say 900\u00d78 is 7,200 and 15\u00d7300 is 4,500, so total overestimation would be 7,200 - 4,500 + ...). Hmm, maybe this is getting too involved, but since all the exact calculations agree, I think it's okay.\n\nAlternatively, another sanity check: 915 \u0