erfect.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another approach, like the lattice method, but that might take longer. Alternatively, check if the answer makes sense by estimating.\n\nEstimate: 700 \u00d7350 = 245,000. Since 720 is 20 more than 700, and 348 is 2 less than 350. So the actual product should be a bit higher than 245,000. 250,560 is indeed 5,560 more, which seems reasonable. So that approximation checks out.\n\nAlternatively, exact difference:\n\nLet me think of (700 +20)(350 -2) =700\u00d7350 +700\u00d7(-2) +20\u00d7350 +20\u00d7(-2).\n\nWhich is 245,000 -1,400 +7,000 -40 =245,000 + ( -1,400 -40) +7,000.\n\nWait, 245,000 -1,440 +7,000.\n\n245,000 +7,000 =252,000; 252,000 -1,440 =250,560. Exactly! So that's another method confirming the result. So that's perfect.\n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and checks, I can confidently say that the product of 720 and 348 is 250,560.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 720 and 348 is \\boxed{250560}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 720 and 348, we can use the standard multiplication method and verify through multiple approaches:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication:**\n   - Multiply 720 by 8 (units place of 348):\n     \\[\n     720 \\times 8 = 5760\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 720 by 40 (tens place of 348):\n     \\[\n     720 \\times 40 = 28800\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 720 by 300 (hundreds place of 348):\n     \\[\n     720 \\times 300 = 216000\n     \\]\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     5760 + 28800 + 216000 = 250560\n     \\]\n\n2. **Distributive Property:**\n   - Break down 348 into 300, 40, and 8:\n     \\[\n     720 \\times 348 = 720 \\times (300 + 40 + 8)\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     720 \\times 300 = 216000, \\quad 720 \\times 40 = 28800, \\quad 720 \\times 8 = 5760\n     \\]\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     216000 + 28800 + 5760 = 250560\n     \\]\n\n3. **Alternative Breakdown:**\n   - Break down 720 into 700 and 20:\n     \\[\n     720 \\times 348 = (700 + 20) \\times 348\n   