messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 353 and 755?', "<think>Okay, so I need to find the product of 353 and 755. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. I remember multiplication of large numbers can be done using the standard algorithm, breaking them down into smaller parts. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write down the numbers vertically, aligning them to the right:.\n\n```.\n   353.\n× 755.\n-------.\n```.\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the bottom number, which is 5 (from 755). I need to multiply 353 by 5 first.\n\n353 × 5. Let's do that step by step:.\n\n3 × 5 = 15, write down 5 and carry over 1.\n5 × 5 = 25, plus the carried 1 is 26. Write down 6 and carry over 2.\n3 × 5 = 15, plus the carried 2 is 17. So that gives 1765. Wait, hold on, maybe I should write it properly.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying 353 by the units digit (5), it's:.\n\n5 × 3 = 15, write 5, carry 1.\n5 × 5 = 25, plus 1 is 26, write 6, carry 2.\n5 × 3 (the hundreds place) = 15, plus 2 is 17. So the first partial product is 1765. Got that.\n\nNext, moving to the next digit in 755, which is 5 (the tens place). Since it's the tens place, this multiplication will be shifted one position to the left. So we multiply 353 by 50 (since it's the tens digit). But actually, in the standard method, you just multiply by 5 and then shift one place.\n\nSo:.\n\n5 × 3 = 15, write 5, carry 1.\n5 × 5 = 25 +1 =26, write 6, carry 2.\n5 × 3 =15 +2 =17. So that gives 1765 again, but shifted one position to the left, so it becomes 17650.\n\nWait, but hold on, actually, maybe I should write it as:.\n\nSecond partial product (tens place):.\n\n5 (tens) × 353 = 1765, but shifted one place left, so it's 17650.\n\nThen the next digit is 7 in the hundreds place. So that's 700, so we multiply 353 by 700. Again, in the standard algorithm, we multiply by 7 and then shift two places.\n\nSo 353 × 7:.\n\n7 × 3 =21, write 1 carry 2.\n7 ×5 =35 +2=37, write 7, carry3.\n7 ×3=21 +3=24. So that gives 2471, but shifted two places to the left becomes 247100.\n\nNow, adding up all the partial products:.\n\nFirst partial product:    1765.\n\nSecond partial product: 17650.\n\nThird partial product: 247100.\n\nAdding them together. Let me line them up properly:.\n\n     1765.\n    17650.\n+247100.\n----------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nFirst line up the numbers:.\n\n247100.\n\n + 17650.\n\n   + 1765.\n\nLet me write them vertically:.\n\n247100.\n\n+ 17650.\n\n= ?.\n\nAdding 247100 + 17650:.\n\n247100 + 10000 = 257100.\n\n257100 + 7650 = 257100 + 7000 = 264100, then +650 = 264750.\n\nWait, maybe step by step:.\n\n247100.\n\n+17650.\n\nStart from the right:.\n\n0 +0 =0.\n\n0 +5=5.\n\n0 +5=5.\n\n7 (from 247100's thousands place) +6 (from 17650's thousands place)=13. So write 3, carryover 1.\n\n4 (from 247100's ten thousands place) +1 (from 17650's ten thousands place which is 1?) Wait, let me write it properly:.\n\n247100 is:.\n\n2 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (units).\n\n17650 is:.\n\n0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (units).\n\nAdding:.\n\nUnits: 0+0=0.\n\nTens: 0+5=5.\n\nHundreds: 1 +6=7.\n\nThousands:7 +7=14, write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:4 +1 +1 (carryover) =6.\n\nHundred thousands:2 +0 =2.\n\nSo total is 264750.\n\nThen add the last partial product 1765 to this.\n\n264750 + 1765.\n\nLet me compute that.\n\n264750 + 1000 = 265750.\n\n265750 + 700 = 266450.\n\n266450 + 60 = 266510. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
