      "text": "175,924? Wait, but 170,800 +5,124. Let's do this:\n\n170,800 + 5,000 is 175,800. Then add 124: 175,800 + 124 = 175,924. Yes, that seems right.\n\nWait, but let me verify my calculations again because sometimes when adding large numbers it's easy to make a mistake.\n\nLet me check each step again.\n\nFirst step: 427 \u00d7 2 = 854. Correct.\n\nSecond step: 427 \u00d7 10 = 4270. Correct.\n\nThird step: 427 \u00d7 400: 427 \u00d74 = 1708, so \u00d7100 is 170,800. Correct.\n\nAdding 854 +4270: 854 +4000 is 4854, plus 270 is 5124. Correct.\n\nThen adding 5124 +170,800: 170,800 +5,000 = 175,800; 175,800 +124 = 175,924. That seems correct.\n\nHmm, but let me cross-verify using another method to ensure that I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the distributive property: (400 +27)(400 +12). Wait, actually, 427 is 400+27 and 412 is 400 +12. Hmm, maybe expanding that?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using another multiplication method, like the lattice method or another approach. Alternatively, breaking down the numbers differently.\n\nAlternatively, compute 427 \u00d7 412 as follows:\n\nLet me note that 412 = 400 +12, so 427\u00d7412 = 427\u00d7(400 +12) = 427\u00d7400 +427\u00d712.\n\nSimilarly, 427\u00d7400 = 170,800 as before. Then 427\u00d712: let's compute that.\n\n427\u00d712 can be 427\u00d710 +427\u00d72 = 4270 +854 = 5124. Then total would be 170,800 +5,124 = 175,924. Same result as before. So that matches. So that's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe compute 412 \u00d7427 using another approach? Let me see:\n\nAlternatively, let's try using the standard algorithm the other way around, multiplying 412 by 427.\n\nSo writing:\n\n  412.\n\u00d7427.\n--------\n\nFirst multiply 412 by 7 (units digit of 427):\n\n412 \u00d77: 2\u00d77=14, 1\u00d77=7+1=8, 4\u00d77=28, so 2884.\n\nThen multiply by 20 (the tens digit is 2, so \u00d720):\n\n412 \u00d720 = 8,240.\n\nThen multiply by 400 (the hundreds digit is 4, so \u00d7400):\n\n412 \u00d7400 = 164,800.\n\nNow add them together:\n\n2884 + 8240 +164,800.\n\nFirst, 2884 +8240: 2884 +8000=10,884; 10,884 +240=11,124.\n\nThen 11,124 +164,800 = 175,924. Same result. So that's good.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps even use another method, like the difference of squares or another algebraic identity?\n\nHmm, but since 427 and 412 are close to 420? Let me see. Maybe approximate?\n\nAlternatively, think of 427 \u00d7412 as (420 +7)(410 +2). Expanding that:\n\n=420\u00d7410 +420\u00d72 +7\u00d7410 +7\u00d72.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n420\u00d7410: 42\u00d741 \u00d7100. 42\u00d741. Let's compute that.\n\n42\u00d740 =1680, plus 42\u00d71=42, so total 1722. So 1722\u00d7100=172,200.\n\n420\u00d72 =840.\n\n7\u00d7410 =2,870.\n\n7\u00d72 =14.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n172,200 +840 =173,040.\n\n173,040 +2,870 =175,910.\n\n175,910 +14 =175,924. Again, same answer. Hmm, that's a good sign.\n\nSo all methods are leading me to 175,924. So that must be the correct answer. I think that's solid. Maybe I can check with a calculator, but since I can't do that here, multiple methods giving the same result gives me confidence.\n\nAlternatively, let me do the multiplication step by step once again to be thorough.\n\nStarting with 427 \u00d7412.\n\nFirst, write them down:\n\n       427\n     \u00d7412\n     -------\nFirst, multiply 427 by 2 (units place):\n\n427 \u00d72 =854. Write that down.\n\nThen, multiply 427 by 1 (tens place, so actually 10). So 427\u00d71=427, but shifted one position to the left, so it's 4270.\n\nThen, multiply 427 by 4 (hundreds place, which is 400). 427\u00d74=1708, shifted two positions left to become 170,800.\n\nNow add them:\n\nFirst line:    854\n\nSecond line:  4270\n\nThird line: 170800\n\nAdding the first two: 854 +4270.\n\nLet me add 854 +4000 =4854, then +270 =5124.\n\nThen 5124 +170800. Let's break that down.\n\n170,800 +5,000 =175,800. Then 175,800 +124 =175,924.\n\nSame result again. Alright, so I think that's solid.\n\nTherefore, the product of 427 and 412 is 175,924.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 427 and 412 is \\boxed{175924}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 427 and 412, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 427 by the ones digit of 412 (2):\n   \\[\n   427 \\times 2 = 854\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 427 by the tens digit of 412 (1, which represents 10) and shift one place to the left:\n   \\[\n   427 \\times 10 = 4270\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 427 by the hundreds digit of 412 (4, which represents 400) and shift two places to the left:\n   \\[\n   427 \\times 400 = 170800\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the results of the three multiplications:\n   \\[\n   854 + 4270 + 170800\n   \\]\n\n   - First, add \\(854 + 4270\\):\n     \\[\n     854 + 4270 = 5124\n     \\]\n\n   - Then add the result to \\(170800\\):\n     \\[\n     5124 + 170800 = 175924\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 427 and 412 is \\(\\boxed{175924}\\).",
