messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 140 and 127?', "<think>:.\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 140 and 127. Hmm, let me think about the best way to do this. I remember that multiplying numbers can sometimes be broken down into smaller parts to make it easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here? Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I know that 140 times 127 is the same as 140 multiplied by (100 + 20 + 7). Wait, actually, 127 can be broken down into 100 + 20 + 7, right? So maybe I can split it up like that. Let me write that out:.\n\n140 × 127 = 140 × (100 + 20 + 7).\n\nNow, using the distributive property, I can multiply 140 by each of those numbers separately and then add the results together. Let me do each part step by step.\n\nStarting with 140 × 100. Hmm, multiplying by 100 is straightforward. I just add two zeros to the end of 140. So that would be 140 × 100 = 14,000. Okay, that's easy.\n\nNext, 140 × 20. Let me see, 140 times 20. Well, 140 times 2 is 280, so if I multiply that by 10 again (since 20 is 2×10), that would be 280 × 10 = 2,800. Wait, is that right? Let me check another way. 20 times 100 is 2,000 and 20 times 40 is 800, so 2,000 + 800 = 2,800. Yeah, that matches. So that part is 2,800.\n\nNow the last term is 140 × 7. Let me calculate that. 140 times 7. Breaking it down again, maybe 100 × 7 is 700 and 40 ×7 is 280. So adding those together: 700 + 280 = 980. Wait, 100×7 is 700, 40×7 is 280, so total is 980. That seems correct. Let me verify: 140 × 7. 7×100 is 700, 7×40 is 280, so 700+280 is indeed 980. Okay.\n\nNow, I need to add all those three results together: 14,000 + 2,800 + 980. Let me do that step by step.\n\nFirst, 14,000 + 2,800. Adding those together: 14,000 + 2,000 is 16,000, and then +800 would be 16,800. So that gives me 16,800. Now add the last part, which is 980. So 16,800 + 980.\n\nHmm, let me see. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
