efore, I think that's the correct answer.\n\nWait, but just to double-check once more, perhaps using another approach. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, 127 \u00d7140 can be thought of as (130 - 3) \u00d7140. Wait, 127 is 130 minus 3. Maybe that's a different way to compute.\n\nSo, (130 -3) \u00d7140 = 130\u00d7140 -3\u00d7140.\n\nCompute 130\u00d7140 first. 130 \u00d7140. Let's see, 13 \u00d714 \u00d7100. Because 130 is 13\u00d710 and 140 is 14\u00d710. So together, 13\u00d714\u00d710\u00d710=13\u00d714\u00d7100.\n\n13\u00d714: 10\u00d714=140, 3\u00d714=42, so 140+42=182. So 182 \u00d7100 =18,200.\n\nThen subtract 3\u00d7140=420. So 18,200 - 420 = ?\n\n18,200 minus 400 is 17,800, then minus 20 more is 17,780. Yep, same result again.\n\nOkay, so that's a fourth method leading to the same answer. I feel confident now that 17,780 is the correct product of 140 and 127.\n\nAlternatively, another way could be to use the fact that 140 \u00d7127 is the same as 140 multiplied by (100 + 27). Let me try that.\n\n140\u00d7(100 +27)=140\u00d7100 +140\u00d727. So 14,000 + (140\u00d727). Then compute 140\u00d727.\n\n140\u00d727 can be broken into 100\u00d727 +40\u00d727. 100\u00d727=2,700 and 40\u00d727=1,080. Adding those gives 2,700 +1,080=3,780. So total is 14,000 +3,780=17,780. Yep, same answer again.\n\nAlright, so all these different methods are giving me the same result. Therefore, I can be sure that the product of 140 and 127 is indeed 17,780. I think that's solid. No mistakes detected in my calculations. Each approach cross-verifies the others. So I'm confident the answer is 17,780.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 140 and 127 is \\boxed{17780}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 140 and 127, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 127 into 100 + 20 + 7:\n\n\\[\n140 \\times 127 = 140 \\times (100 + 20 + 7)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate each term separately:\n- \\(140 \\times 100 = 14000\\)\n- \\(140 \\times 20 = 2800\\)\n- \\(140 \\times 7 = 980\\)\n\nNext, add the results together:\n\\[\n14000 + 2800 + 980\n\\]\n\nAdding step-by-step:\n- \\(14000 + 2800 = 16800\\)\n- \\(16800 + 980 = 17780\\)\n\nWe can verify this using another method by considering \\(127\\) as \\(130 - 3\\):\n\\[\n(130 - 3) \\times 140 = 130 \\times 140 - 3 \\times 140\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n- \\(130 \\times 140 = 18200\\)\n- \\(3 \\times 140 = 420\\)\n\nSubtracting the results:\n\\[\n18200 - 420 = 17780\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 140 and 127 is \\(\\boxed{17780}\\).",
