ltiplying by the 9 (which is in the tens place), the first multiplication (units place) would have been 3, so the next is the tens place. Therefore, the partial product should be shifted one place to the left. So when I do 238 × 9, that gives 2142, but since it's actually 238 × 90, it's 21420. Wait, actually, that might be a better way to think of it. So instead of shifting, maybe compute 238 × 9 × 10. Hmm, maybe breaking it down like that helps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should just do the multiplication step by step, keeping track of the shifts.\n\nLet me try again:.\n\nFirst partial product (units digit 3):.\n\n3 × 238 = 714.\n\nSecond partial product (tens digit 9, which is 90):.\n\n90 × 238. Let's compute 238 × 9 × 10. 238 × 9 is 2142, then ×10 is 21420.\n\nThird partial product (hundreds digit 6, which is 600):.\n\n600 × 238. Compute 238 × 6 × 100. 238 × 6 is 1428, then ×100 is 142800.\n\nNow, adding up all three partial products:.\n\n714 (from 3×238).\n\n+21,420 (from 90×238).\n\n+142,800 (from 600×238).\n\nLet me add them step by step.\n\nFirst, 714 + 21,420. Let's see:.\n\n714 + 21,420 = 22,134? Wait, 21,420 + 700 is 22,120, then +14 is 22,134. Okay.\n\nThen, adding that to 142,800:.\n\n22,134 + 142,800. Let's compute:.\n\n22,134 + 142,800. Adding the thousands: 22,000 + 142,800 = 164,800. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
