      "text": "31,200 minus 624 equals... Let me compute that. \n\nStarting with 31,200 minus 600 would be 30,600. Then subtract the remaining 24, so 30,600 minus 24 is 30,576. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. \n\nWait, 31,200 minus 624. Let me write it out numerically:\n\n31,200\n-\u00a0\u00a0624\n--------\n?\n\nStarting from the right, 0 - 4. Hmm, can't do that, so borrow from the next digit. But the next digit is also 0, so I need to keep borrowing until I get to a non-zero digit. Let's see:\n\n31,200 is the same as 31,200.00...\n\nStarting subtraction:\n\nThe units place: 0 - 4. Can't do, so borrow 1 from the tens place. But the tens place is 0, so have to go to hundreds place. The hundreds place is also 2, so borrow 1 from the thousands place. \n\nWait, maybe breaking it down differently. 31,200 minus 600 is 30,600. Then minus 24 more gives 30,600 - 24 = 30,576. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nSo, 104 \u00d7 294 = 30,576? Let me check using another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do the multiplication the standard way:\n\nMultiply 104 by 294. Let me write it vertically:\n\n\u2003\u2003104\n\u00d7294\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 104 by 4 (the units digit of 294):\n\n104 \u00d7 4 = 416.\n\nThen multiply 104 by 90 (the tens digit is 9, so 90):\n\n104 \u00d7 90 = 9,360. (Because 104 \u00d7 9 = 936, then add a zero.)\n\nThen multiply 104 by 200 (the hundreds digit is 2, so 200):\n\n104 \u00d7 200 = 20,800.\n\nNow add those three results together: 416 + 9,360 + 20,800.\n\nFirst, 416 + 9,360 = 9,776. Then, 9,776 + 20,800 = 30,576. \n\nOkay, same result. So that seems correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the lattice method, but that might take longer. Alternatively, check with a calculator, but since I can't use one, the two methods both leading to 30,576 gives me confidence. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me try another approach. Let me compute 100 \u00d7 294 first, then add 4 \u00d7 294. \n\n100 \u00d7 294 is 29,400. Then 4 \u00d7 294 is 1,176. Adding those together: 29,400 + 1,176 = 30,576. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlright, so all methods lead me to 30,576. Therefore, I think that's the correct product of 104 and 294. \n\nBut wait, let me just confirm once more with another method. Maybe using the distributive property in a different split. Let's split 104 as 100 + 4 and 294 as 200 + 90 + 4? Hmm, that might complicate, but let's see:\n\n(100 + 4) \u00d7 (200 + 90 + 4). \n\nExpanding this would be 100\u00d7200 + 100\u00d790 + 100\u00d74 + 4\u00d7200 + 4\u00d790 + 4\u00d74.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n100\u00d7200 = 20,000\n\n100\u00d790 = 9,000\n\n100\u00d74 = 400\n\n4\u00d7200 = 800\n\n4\u00d790 = 360\n\n4\u00d74 = 16\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n20,000 + 9,000 = 29,000\n\n29,000 + 400 = 29,400\n\n29,400 + 800 = 30,200\n\n30,200 + 360 = 30,560\n\n30,560 + 16 = 30,576.\n\nSame answer again. So definitely 30,576. \n\nTherefore, I'm confident the product is 30,576.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 104 and 294 is \\boxed{30576}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 104 and 294, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts for easier calculation.\n\nFirst, we express 294 as \\(300 - 6\\):\n\\[\n104 \\times 294 = 104 \\times (300 - 6)\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n104 \\times 300 - 104 \\times 6\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\\[\n104 \\times 300 = 31200\n\\]\n\\[\n104 \\times 6 = 624\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second result from the first:\n\\[\n31200 - 624 = 30576\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by breaking down the multiplication step-by-step:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   104 \\\\\n\\times 294 \\\\\n\\hline\n   416 \\quad (\\text{104 \u00d7 4}) \\\\\n+ 9360 \\quad (\\text{104 \u00d7 90}) \\\\\n+ 20800 \\quad (\\text{104 \u00d7 200}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   30576 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n416 + 9360 + 20800 = 30576\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the distributive property on different splits:\n\\[\n(100 + 4) \\times (200 + 90 + 4)\n\\]\n\nExpanding and calculating each term:\n\\[\n100 \\times 200 + 100 \\times 90 + 100 \\times 4 + 4 \\times 200 + 4 \\times 90 + 4 \\times 4\n\\]\n\\[\n20000 + 9000 + 400 + 800 + 360 + 16 = 30576\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. Therefore, the product of 104 and 294 is \\(\\boxed{30576}\\).",
