roduct is 1875.\n\nNext, the tens digit (7), but since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left. So multiply by 70:\n\n375 \u00d77:  \n7\u00d75=35, write 5, carryover 3.  \n7\u00d77=49 +3=52, write 2, carryover5.  \n7\u00d73=21 +5=26. So that gives 2625, but since it's the tens place, it's 26250.\n\nThird, the hundreds digit (7), which is the hundreds place, so multiply by 700:\n\n375 \u00d77: same as before, 2625, but since it's hundreds, we shift two places, so 262500.\n\nNow add all three partial products:\n\n1875  \n26250  \n+262500  \n------------\n\nAdding 1875 and 26250 first:\n\n1875 +26250 =28125\n\nThen 28125 +262500:\n\n28125 +262500 =290,625.\n\nYep, exactly the same. So definitely, the answer is 290,625. I think I've checked it in multiple ways, so I'm confident that's correct. Probably the first time I was just confused when adding, but after verifying with different methods, it's consistent. So the product of 375 and 775 is 290,625.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 375 and 775 is \\boxed{290625}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 375 and 775, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy. Here are the key steps:\n\n1. **Breaking down using distributive property:**\n   - Split 775 into 700 + 75:\n     \\[\n     375 \\times 775 = 375 \\times (700 + 75) = 375 \\times 700 + 375 \\times 75\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(375 \\times 700\\):\n     \\[\n     375 \\times 700 = 262,500\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(375 \\times 75\\) by further breaking it down:\n     \\[\n     375 \\times 75 = 375 \\times (70 + 5) = 375 \\times 70 + 375 \\times 5\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     375 \\times 70 = 26,250 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 375 \\times 5 = 1,875\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     26,250 + 1,875 = 28,125\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     262,500 + 28,125 = 290,625\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 375 by each digit of 775 and sum the partial products:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{r}\n       \\qu