05 to 102.5, but since we ignore remainders, 102. Double 545 is 1090)\n\n51 | 2180 (halve 102 to 51, exactly. Double 1090 to 2180)\n\n25 | 4360 (halve 51 to 25.5 \u2192 25; double 2180 to 4360)\n\n12 | 8720 (25 \u2192 halve to 12.5 \u219212; double 4360 to 8720)\n\n6 |17,440 (12 \u21926; double 8720)\n\n3 |34,880 (6\u21923; double 17,440)\n\n1 |69,760 (3\u21921.5\u21921; double 34,880)\n\nNow, we stop at 1.\n\nNow, we add the numbers in the second column where the first column is odd:\n\nLooking at the first column entries:\n\n205 (odd) \u2192 545\n\n102 (even)\n\n51 (odd) \u21922180\n\n25 (odd) \u21924360\n\n12 (even)\n\n3 (odd) \u219234,880\n\n1 (odd) \u219269,760\n\nWait, so adding the numbers corresponding to odd first entries:\n\n545 +2180 +4360 +34,880 +69,760.\n\nLet me compute step by step.\n\nFirst, 545 +2180 = 2725\n\n2725 +4360 = 7085\n\n7085 +34,880 = 41,965\n\n41,965 +69,760 = 111,725.\n\nWow, that's the same answer again. Okay, so even with the Russian peasant method, I get 111,725. That's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can check using another multiplication algorithm, like the lattice method?\n\nLet me try that quickly.\n\nThe lattice method requires a grid. Since 205 is a 3-digit number and 545 is also a 3-digit number, we need a 3x3 grid.\n\nDrawing the grid mentally, each digit of 205 is on the top, and each digit of 545 on the right.\n\nSo, the top would be 2, 0, 5 and the right side 5, 4, 5.\n\nEach cell in the grid will be split by a diagonal, and we multiply the corresponding digits.\n\nStarting with the top row and right column:\n\nFirst cell (top 2, right 5): 2\u00d75=10. Write 1 above the diagonal and 0 below.\n\nSecond cell (top 2, right 4): 2\u00d74=8. Write 0 and 8? Wait, maybe need to think carefully.\n\nWait, actually, in lattice multiplication, each cell corresponds to a digit pair. Let's see step by step:\n\nTop digits: 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (units)\n\nRight digits: 5 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 5 (units)\n\nThe grid will have 