ure. Alternatively, 689 *922 = (700 -11)*(900 +22). Wait, that's what I did before, so same thing. So yeah, that gives the same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another multiplication trick. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, 689 *922. Let me write them side by side:\n\n689\n\nx922\n\n-------------\n\nFirst, multiply 689 by 2: 1,378\n\nThen multiply 689 by 20 (shift one position): 13,780\n\nThen multiply 689 by 900 (shift two positions): 620,100\n\nAdding all three together: 1,378 +13,780 =15,158; 15,158 +620,100=635,258. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 689*922 as 689*(900+22)= 689*900 +689*22. Wait, let me try that.\n\n689*900 is 620,100 as before. 689*22. Let me compute that.\n\n689*20 =13,780 and 689*2=1,378. So total is 13,780 +1,378 =15,158. Then adding to 620,100: same as before, 620,100 +15,158 =635,258. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, so through multiple methods: breaking down into hundreds, tens, units; using distributive property with different groupings; verifying via division, all lead to the same answer. So I'm confident that the product of 689 and 922 is 635,258.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 689 and 922 is \\boxed{635258}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 689 and 922, we can break down the multiplication into manageable parts and then sum the results.\n\nFirst, we decompose 922 into 900, 20, and 2:\n\\[\n689 \\times 922 = 689 \\times (900 + 20 + 2)\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate each part separately:\n1. \\(689 \\times 900\\):\n   \\[\n   689 \\times 9 = 6201 \\quad \\text{(then add two zeros)} \\quad 6201 \\times 100 = 620100\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(689 \\times 20\\):\n   \\[\n   689 \\times 2 = 1378 \\quad \\text{(then add one zero)} \\quad 1378 \\times 10 = 13780\n   \\]\n\n3. \\(689 \\times 2\\):\n   \\[\n   689 \\times 2 = 1378\n   \\]\n\nAdding all three results together:\n\\[\n620100 + 13780 + 1378\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(620100\\) and \\(13780\\):\n\\[\n620100 + 13780 = 633880\n\\]\n\nThen add the result to \\(1378\\):\n\\[\n633880 + 1378 = 635258