 the hundreds place):661\u00d7700=462,700.\n\nAdding all three together:\n\n1983 +13,220 =15,203.\n\n15,203 +462,700 =477,903.\n\nSame result. Hmm, that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 661 \u00d7723 by another method, like the standard long multiplication:\n\nLet me write it out vertically again:\n\n          661\n\n        \u00d7 723\n\n        ---------\n\nFirst, multiply 661 by 3:\n\n3\u00d71=3, 3\u00d76=18 (carryover 1), 3\u00d76=18 +1=19. So that's 1983.\n\nThen, multiply by 2 (the tens digit):\n\nBut here, since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left.\n\n2\u00d71=2 (write in the tens place?), wait:\n\nLet me write it step by step:\n\nMultiply 661 by 2 (the second digit from the right, which is the tens place):\n\n2 \u00d71 =2 (this is actually 20\u00d71, so the result is 20, so in the tens place).\n\nSo, starting from the tens place:\n\n2\u00d71=2 (tens place, so write 2 in the tens column, which is the second digit from the right in this partial product).\n\nThen 2\u00d76=12 (hundreds place: since the first digit was 2 in the tens place, so the next digit is hundreds). So we write 2 and carryover 1.\n\nThen 2\u00d76=12 plus the carried 1 is 13 (thousands place). So that gives 13,220. Wait, writing it out:\n\nFirst partial product (3\u00d7661): 1983.\n\nSecond partial product (20\u00d7661):\n\nStart with the second digit (2 in tens place):\n\n2\u00d71=2 (tens place, so the first digit is in the tens column: so the partial product is:\n\n   1322 shifted left by one digit (because it's the tens place), so it becomes 13220.\n\nThird partial product (700\u00d7661):\n\n7\u00d71=7 (hundreds place, so the third partial product is shifted left by two digits):\n\n7\u00d71=7 (hundreds place?), let's see:\n\n7\u00d71=7 (hundreds place? Wait, actually, 7 is in the hundreds place, so it's 700\u00d7661.\n\nSo 7\u00d7661=4627, then times 100 is 462700.\n\nAdding all together gives the same result.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can compute using another method, like breaking down the numbers into smaller components.\n\nLet me see: 661 \u00d7723.\n\nPerhaps, 661 \u00d7700 =462,700.\n\nThen 661\u00d720=13,220.\n\nThen 661\u00d73=1,983.\n\nAdding those together gives the same total. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property in a different way: (600 +60 +1) \u00d7 (700 +20 +3). But that might be more complicated, but let me try.\n\nFirst, expand (a + b +c)(d + e +f) where a=600, b=60, c=1; d=700, e=20, f=3.\n\nSo the product is:\n\na*d + a*e +a*f +\n\nb*d +b*e +b*f +\n\nc*d +c*e +c*f.\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\na*d =600\u00d7700=420,000.\n\na*e=600\u00d720=12,000.\n\na*f=600\u00d73=1,800.\n\nb*d=60\u00d7700=42,000.\n\nb*e=60\u00d720=1,200.\n\nb*f=60\u00d73=180.\n\nc*d=1\u00d7700=700.\n\nc*e=1\u00d720=20.\n\nc*f=1\u00d73=3.\n\nNow, adding all these up:\n\nStart with the largest terms:\n\n420,000 +12,000 +1,800 = 420,000 +12,000 is 432,000; plus 1,800 is 433,800.\n\nThen adding 42,000 +1,200 +180: 42,000 +1,200 is 43,200; plus 180 is 43,380.\n\nThen adding 700 +20 +3: 700+20=720 +3=723.\n\nNow, adding all those together:\n\nFirst, 433,800 +43,380.\n\n433,800 +40,000 =473,800; plus 3,380 is 473,800 +3,380=477,180.\n\nThen add 723 to that: 477,180 +723 =477,903.\n\nOkay, so that also gives the same result: 477,903. So that's consistent.\n\nHmm, so all methods are leading me to 477,903. Let me just verify with a calculator to be absolutely sure, but since I can't use a calculator, perhaps I can check using another method.\n\nAlternatively, let's do the multiplication in a different way. Let me compute 661\u00d7723 as follows:\n\nNote that 661 \u00d7723 =661 \u00d7(700+23)=661\u00d7700 +661\u00d723.\n\nWe already know 661\u00d7700 is 462,700.\n\nNow compute 661\u00d723:\n\n23\u00d7661.\n\nCompute 20\u00d7661 +3\u00d7661.\n\n20\u00d7661=13,220.\n\n3\u00d7661=1,983.\n\nAdding those:13,220 +1,983 =15,203.\n\nThen add to 462,700: 462,700 +15,203 =477,903. Same answer.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can compute 723\u00d7661 by reversing the order, since multiplication is commutative. Let me try 723\u00d7661.\n\n723 \u00d7600 =433,800.\n\n723 \u00d760=43,380.\n\n723 \u00d71=723.\n\nAdding them together: 433,800 +43,380 =477,180 +723=477,903. Same result.\n\nAlright, so all these cross-verifications are giving me the same answer. Therefore, I think the product of 661 and 723 is 477,903.\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, let me check if I might have made an addition error in one of the steps. Let me re-add the partial products again:\n\nFirst partial product: 1,983.\n\nSecond partial product:13,220.\n\nThird partial product:462,700.\n\nAdding first two: 1,983 +13,220.\n\n1,983 +10,000 =11,983.\n\n11,983 +3,220 =15,203. That's correct.\n\nThen adding to 462,700:\n\n462,700 +15,203.\n\n462,700 +10,000 =472,700.\n\n472,700 +5,203 =477,903. Yes, that's correct.\n\nHmm, so I think this is correct. Therefore, the answer should be 477,903.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 661 and 723 is \\boxed{477903}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 661 and 723, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm step by step:\n\n1. **Multiply 661 by 3 (units place):**\n   \\[\n   661 \\times 3 = 1983\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 661 by 20 (tens place):**\n   \\[\n   661 \\times 2 = 1322 \\quad \\text{(then shift one position to the left)} \\quad \\Rightarrow 13220\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 661 by 700 (hundreds place):**\n   \\[\n   661 \\times 7 = 4627 \\quad \\text{(then shift two positions to the left)} \\quad \\Rightarrow 462700\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   1983 + 13220 + 462700\n   \\]\n\n   - First, add \\(1983\\) and \\(13220\\):\n     \\[\n     1983 + 13220 = 15203\n     \\]\n\n   - Then add the result to \\(462700\\):\n     \\[\n     15203 + 462700 = 477903\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 661 and 723 is \\(\\boxed{477903}\\).",
