7 by 8 (hundreds place, which is the first digit from the left in 882):\n\nSo 327 \u00d78 again, but shifted two places to the left (because it's hundreds).\n\nSo 327 \u00d78 =2616, shifted two places becomes 261600.\n\nNow add them all together:\n\n654\n\n+26160\n\n+261600\n\nFirst, 654 +26160:\n\n654 +26160 = let's compute 26160 + 600 =26760, then +54 =26814.\n\nThen add 261600:\n\n26814 +261600 = ?\n\n261,600 +26,814 = ?\n\n261,600 +20,000 =281,600\n\n281,600 +6,814 =288,414. Yes, same result.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is 288,414.\n\nWait, but to be thorough, maybe do a prime factorization approach? Though that might be more time-consuming.\n\nAlternatively, check divisibility by small primes to see if the product makes sense.\n\nFor example, 327 is divisible by 3 because 3+2+7=12, which is divisible by 3. 327 \u00f73= 109. So 327=3\u00d7109.\n\n882 divided by 2 is 441, so it's even, so 2\u00d7441. 441 is 21 squared, so 21\u00d721=441, which is 3\u00d77\u00d73\u00d77. So 882=2\u00d73\u00d73\u00d77\u00d77.\n\nTherefore, 327\u00d7882 = (3\u00d7109) \u00d7 (2\u00d73\u00d73\u00d77\u00d77) = 2\u00d73^3\u00d77^2\u00d7109.\n\nBut not sure if that helps, but maybe compute the product step by step with factors:\n\n2\u00d73^3\u00d77\u00b2\u00d7109.\n\nAlternatively, compute 2\u00d73=6, so 6\u00d73\u00b2\u00d77\u00b2\u00d7109.\n\nWait, but this might not help in getting the numerical value. Alternatively, maybe compute the product as:\n\n327 \u00d7 882 = 327 \u00d7 (900 - 18) = 327\u00d7900 - 327\u00d718 = 294,300 - 5,886 = 288,414, which we did earlier.\n\nAlternatively, maybe compute 882 \u00d7 300 = 264,600 and 882 \u00d727 = ?\n\n882\u00d727:\n\n882\u00d720 =17,640\n\n882\u00d77 =6,174\n\nAdd them:17,640 +6,174 =23,814.\n\nTherefore total is 264,600 +23,814 =288,414. That's another way.\n\nYes, so breaking 327 into 300 +27, then multiplying each by 882 and adding, gives the same result. That's solid.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps check using another method: 327 \u00d7 882.\n\nLet me see, 327 multiplied by 800 is 261,600; by 80 is 26,160; by 2 is 654. Adding those gives 261,600 +26,160 =287,760 +654 =288,414. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can do a sanity check by approximating the numbers.\n\n327 is close to 300, 882 is close to 900. 300\u00d7900 =270,000. The actual product should be higher because both numbers are slightly larger than the approximations. 327 is 27 more than 300, and 882 is 18 less than 900. Hmm, so maybe the difference would be (300 +27)(900 -18) = 300\u00d7900 +300\u00d7(-18) +27\u00d7900 +27\u00d7(-18).\n\nWhich is 270,000 -5,400 +24,300 -486 = ?\n\n270,000 -5,400 =264,600\n\n264,600 +24,300 =288,900\n\n288,900 -486 =288,414.\n\nExactly the same result. So that's another way to see it.\n\nTherefore, I think that's correct. All methods lead to 288,414. I don't see any errors in my calculations, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 327 and 882 is \\boxed{288414}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 327 and 882, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 327 by 2 (units place of 882):**\n   \\[\n   327 \\times 2 = 654\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 327 by 8 (tens place of 882), shifted one position to the left:**\n   \\[\n   327 \\times 8 = 2616 \\quad \\text{(shifted one position left becomes 26160)}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 327 by 8 (hundreds place of 882), shifted two positions to the left:**\n   \\[\n   327 \\times 8 = 2616 \\quad \\text{(shifted two positions left becomes 261600)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add the results of the three partial products:**\n   \\[\n   654 + 26160 + 261600\n   \\]\n\n   - First, add \\(654 + 26160\\):\n     \\[\n     654 + 26160 = 26814\n     \\]\n\n   - Then add the result to \\(261600\\):\n     \\[\n     26814 + 261600 = 288414\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 327 and 882 is \\(\\boxed{288414}\\).",
