o shift).  \nSecond line: 178 \u00d71 (tens digit) = 178, shifted one position to the left, so it becomes 1,780.  \nThird line: 178 \u00d74 (hundreds digit) = 712, shifted two positions to the left, so it becomes 71,200. \n\nThen add all three lines together:  \n1,246  \n1,780  \n71,200  \nTotal: Let's add them step by step. \n\nFirst, 1,246 + 1,780. \n\n1,246 + 1,780:  \n1,000 + 1,000 = 2,000  \n246 + 780 = 1,026  \nSo total is 2,000 + 1,026 = 3,026. \n\nThen add that to 71,200: 3,026 + 71,200. \n\n71,200 + 3,000 = 74,200; then add 26 more: 74,226. \n\nSame result as before. Okay, so that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, maybe do another method, like the lattice method or something else to confirm. But since two methods have given me 74,226, I might be confident that's the right answer. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe do a rough estimation to see if it's in the ballpark. 178 is approximately 200, and 417 is approximately 400. 200 \u00d7 400 = 80,000. Since both numbers were rounded up, the actual product should be less than that. 74,226 is indeed less, so that seems plausible. \n\nAlternatively, 178\u00d7400 is 71,200, and 178\u00d717 is 3,026, so adding them gives 74,226. \n\nHmm, I think that's correct. Let me just try multiplying them in reverse, 417 \u00d7 178, using the standard algorithm to see if it's the same.\n\n417 \u00d7 178: \n\nFirst, write 417  \n\u00d7\u00a0178  \n------------  \n\nMultiply 417 by 8 (units place of 178):  \n400\u00d78=3,200; 17\u00d78=136. So total is 3,200 + 136 = 3,336.  \n\nNext, multiply 417 by 7 (tens place, so actually 70). So 417\u00d77 = 2,919, then shifted left once: 29,190.  \n\nThen multiply 417 by 1 (hundreds place, so 100). 417\u00d71=417, shifted left twice: 41,700.  \n\nNow add all three:  \n3,336  \n29,190  \n41,700  \nAdding first two: 3,336 +29,190 = 32,526. Then add 41,700: 32,526 +41,700 = 74,226. \n\nSame result! Okay, so definitely 74,226. \n\nAlternatively, I can do prime factorization, but that might be mor