thing, but that might not apply here.\n\nAlternatively, check if there's any possible mistake in the initial calculation. Let me retrace.\n\nFirst partial product: 2\u00d7414=828. Correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 4 (tens place) so 40\u00d7414. 4\u00d7414=1,656, so 40\u00d7414=16,560. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 1 (hundreds place), so 100\u00d7414=41,400. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 828 + 16,560 = 17,388. Then +41,400 = 58,788. All steps check out.\n\nHmm, seems solid.\n\nAlternatively, use a calculator to verify, but since I can't use external tools, but given that multiple methods yield the same result, I feel confident.\n\nTherefore, the product of 414 and 142 is 58,788.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 414 and 142 is \\boxed{58788}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 414 and 142, we use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 414 by 2 (units place of 142):**\n   \\[\n   414 \\times 2 = 828\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 414 by 4 (tens place of 142, which is 40):**\n   \\[\n   414 \\times 4 = 1656 \\quad \\text{(then shift one position left)} \\quad 16560\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 414 by 1 (hundreds place of 142, which is 100):**\n   \\[\n   414 \\times 1 = 414 \\quad \\text{(then shift two positions left)} \\quad 41400\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n      828 \\\\\n    16560 \\\\\n   +41400 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     58788 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nVerification using different methods (breaking down the multiplication and adding components) confirms the result. \n\nThe product of 414 and 142 is \\(\\boxed{58788}\\).",
