r method, like the lattice method or something else, but maybe overkill here. Alternatively, use a calculator? But since I don't have one, maybe I can check another way.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 263 \u00d7 361 as (260 +3) \u00d7 (360 +1). Let me try expanding this:\n\n260\u00d7360 + 260\u00d71 +3\u00d7360 +3\u00d71.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n260\u00d7360: 260\u00d7300 = 78,000; 260\u00d760 =15,600. So total is 78,000 +15,600 =93,600.\n\n260\u00d71 =260.\n\n3\u00d7360 =1,080.\n\n3\u00d71=3.\n\nAdding all together: 93,600 +260 =93,860; 93,860 +1,080 =94,940; plus 3 is 94,943. Same result again. So that's good.\n\nHmm, so three different methods give me 94,943, so I think that must be correct. Wait, but just to be absolutely sure, let me try doing the multiplication step by step again, carefully:\n\nMultiplying 263 \u00d7 361.\n\nFirst, write it as:\n\n\u2003\u2003263  \n\u00d7361  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n\nStart with the 1 (units place):\n\n1 \u00d7 263 = 263. Write that down.\n\nNext, the 6 (tens place):\n\n6 \u00d7 263:\n\n6\u00d73 =18 (write down 8, carryover 1).\n\n6\u00d76 =36 +1 =37 (write down 7, carryover 3).\n\n6\u00d72 =12 +3 =15 (write down 15). So this gives 1,578. But since it's the tens place, we shift one to the left, so it becomes 15,780.\n\nNext, the 3 (hundreds place):\n\n3 \u00d7 263:\n\n3\u00d73=9.\n\n3\u00d76=18 (write down 8, carryover 1).\n\n3\u00d72=6 +1=7. So that gives 789. Since it's the hundreds place, shift two to the left, so 78,900.\n\nNow add them all:\n\n263  \n15,780  \n78,900  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n\nAdding the first two:\n\n263 +15,780 =16,043.\n\n16,043 +78,900:\n\n16,043 +78,900:\n\n16,043 +70,000 =86,043.\n\n86,043 +8,900 =94,943.\n\nYes, so that's the same answer again. Therefore, I think the product is indeed 94,943. Wait, but let me confirm with another approach. Let me try using the difference of squares or some other algebraic identity if possible. Hmm, 263 and 361... Not sure if they can be expressed in a way that would make that easier. Alternatively, maybe approximate the numbers to check if 94,943 is reasonable.\n\nApproximating 263 is about 260, 361 is about 360. So 260\u00d7360 is 93,600. Our answer is 94,943, which is about 1,343 more, which makes sense because we rounded both numbers down. 263 is 3 more than 260, and 361 is 1 more than 360. The difference would be (263\u00d7361 vs 260\u00d7360). The difference can be calculated as (260+3)(360+1) -260\u00d7360 =260\u00d71 +3\u00d7360 +3\u00d71 =260 +1,080 +3=1,343. Which matches exactly. So the exact answer is 93,600 +1,343=94,943. So that's another way to see it. Therefore, confident that this is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check with another multiplication:\n\nWait, let me see if 263 \u00d7 361 equals 94,943. Let me divide 94,943 by 263 and see if I get 361.\n\nLet me try that division. 94,943 \u00f7263. Let's see:\n\nFirst, estimate how many times 263 goes into 94,943.\n\n263 \u00d7 300 =78,900.\n\nSubtract that from 94,943: 94,943 -78,900 =16,043.\n\n263 \u00d760 =15,780.\n\nSubtract from 16,043: 16,043-15,780=263.\n\nThen 263\u00d71=263. So total is 300+60+1=361. Exactly. So that confirms that 263 \u00d7361=94,943.\n\nOkay, so after multiple methods and checks, I can be confident that the product is 94,943. I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 263 and 361 is \\boxed{94943}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 263 and 361, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n    \\quad 263 \\\\\n\\times 361 \\\\\n\\hline\n    \\quad 263 \\quad \\text{(263 \u00d7 1)} \\\\\n   +15780 \\quad \\text{(263 \u00d7 60)} \\\\\n+78900 \\quad \\text{(263 \u00d7 300)} \\\\\n\\hline\n    94943 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down step by step:\n\n1. Multiply 263 by 1 (units place):\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 1 = 263\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 263 by 60 (tens place):\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 60 = 15780\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 263 by 300 (hundreds place):\n   \\[\n   263 \\times 300 = 78900\n   \\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\\[\n263 + 15780 + 78900 = 94943\n\\]\n\nVerification using different methods (expanding the product, checking with division, and algebraic identities) confirms the result is correct.\n\nThe product of 263 and 361 is \\boxed{94943}.",
