2 (ten thousands?), 8 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (units). Wait, 28,896. So when adding to 632,100:.\n\n632100 is 6 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (units).\n\nAdding to 28,896 (which is 0 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (units)):.\n\nSo:.\n\nHundred thousands: 6 +0 =6.\n\nTen thousands: 3 +2 =5.\n\nThousands:2 +8 =10, so write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:1 +8 =9 plus the carryover 1 makes 10, write 0 carryover 1.\n\nTens:0 +9=9 plus carryover 1 is 10, write 0 carryover 1.\n\nUnits:0 +6=6, plus carryover 1? Wait, no, units place was already done. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait maybe I made a mistake here. Let me write it out:.\n\n632100.\n\n+\xa0 28896.\n\n------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nUnits place: 0 +6 =6.\n\nTens place: 0 +9 =9.\n\nHundreds place:1 +8 =9.\n\nThousands place:2 +8 =10, write down 0, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place:3 +2 =5, plus the carried over 1 makes 6.\n\nHundred-thousands place:6 +0 =6.\n\nWait, that gives 6 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 9 (hundreds),9 (tens),6 (units). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
