g logarithms? Wait, that might be overcomplicating, but just for fun. Wait, log(384) + log(750) = log(product). But I don't know the exact logs, so maybe not helpful here. But since all the methods above are consistent, I think it's safe.\n\nAlternatively, maybe approximate to check. 384 is close to 400, and 750 is exact. 400\u00d7750=300,000. But since 384 is 16 less than 400, so subtract 16\u00d7750=12,000. 300,000 -12,000=288,000. So that's the same as before. So that approximation method also gives the same answer. So that's another check.\n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and checks, I can be confident the product of 384 and 750 is 288,000.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 384 and 750 is \\boxed{288000}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 384 and 750, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking Down the Numbers**:\n   - Recognize that 750 can be written as \\(75 \\times 10\\).\n   - First, compute \\(384 \\times 75\\):\n     - Break down 75 into \\(70 + 5\\):\n       - \\(384 \\times 70 = 384 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 2688 \\times 10 = 26880\\)\n       - \\(384 \\times 5 = 1920\\)\n     - Add the results: \\(26880 + 1920 = 28800\\)\n   - Multiply by 10: \\(28800 \\times 10 = 288000\\)\n\n2. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 384 by each digit of 750:\n     - \\(384 \\times 0 = 0\\)\n     - \\(384 \\times 50 = 19200\\) (shifted one position left)\n     - \\(384 \\times 700 = 268800\\) (shifted two positions left)\n   - Add the results: \\(19200 + 268800 = 288000\\)\n\n3. **Alternative Method Using Distributive Property**:\n   - Express 384 as \\(400 - 16\\):\n     - \\(400 \\times 750 = 300000\\)\n     - \\(16 \\times 750 = 12000\\)\n   - Subtract the results: \\(300000 - 12000 = 288000\\)\n\n4. **Using Fractions**:\n   - Recognize that 750 is \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) of 1000:\n     - \\(384 \\times \\frac{3}{4} = 288\\)\n     - Multiply by 1000: \\(288 \\times 1000 = 288000\\)\n\nAll methods consistently yield the result \\(288000\\).\n\n### Final Answer\nThe product of 384 and 750 is \\boxed{288000}.",
