ition to the left. So we'll write a 0 in the units place for the next partial product.\n\nMultiplying 928 by 3 (tens place):.\n\n- 3×8 = 24. Write down 4, carryover 2.\n- 3×2 = 6, plus 2 = 8. Write 8.\n- 3×9 = 27, write 7, carryover 2. Then add the carried-over 2, so 27 + 2 = 29? Wait, no, actually, when multiplying 3×9, it's 27, plus the carryover 2 from the previous step? Wait, let me do it step by step again.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying the tens digit (3), the process is:.\n\n- Start with the units digit of the top number (8), but shifted one place to the left. So first, units digit of the partial product will be 0 (because of the shift), then:.\n\n- 3×8 = 24. Write down 4 in the tens place (since we shifted), carryover 2.\n- 3×2 = 6, plus the carried-over 2 = 8. Write that in the hundreds place.\n- 3×9 = 27. Write 7 in the thousands place and carryover 2. But since there are no more digits, we add the carried-over 2 to the next higher place. So it becomes 27, but with the carryover, so 27 + 2? Wait, no, maybe I need to track this better.\n\nWait, maybe I should write it out step by step:.\n\nMultiply 928 by 3 (tens place):.\n\n- Units place (after shift): 0 (since we're in the tens place now).\n- Tens place: 3×8 = 24. Write 4 in the tens place, carryover 2 to the hundreds.\n- Hundreds place: 3×2 = 6 plus carryover 2 = 8. Write 8 in the hundreds place.\n- Thousands place: 3×9 = 27 plus any carryover? Wait, the previous step didn't have a carryover beyond the hundreds. Wait, the carryover from the hundreds place? Let me see:.\n\nWait, actually, after multiplying the hundreds digit (9), 3×9 = 27. Since we were at the thousands place, we need to write 7 in the thousands place and carryover 2 to the ten-thousands place. Since there are no more digits, we just add that 2 in the ten-thousands place. So the partial product here is 27,840? Wait, no, let's see:.\n\nWait, the partial product when multiplying by the tens digit (3) should be 928×30 = 27,840. Let me check that calculation:.\n\n928 × 3 = 2,784. Then since it's the tens place, multiply by 10, so 2,784 ×10 = 27,840. Yes, that's correct. So the second partial product is 27,840.\n\nWait, but when I did the step-by-step earlier, I might have messed up the placement. Let me confirm again:.\n\nStarting with 3 (tens place):.\n\n- First digit (units of 928): 3×8 =24. So write 4 in the tens place (since shifted), carryover 2.\n- Second digit (tens place of 928): 3×2=6, plus 2 is 8. Write in hundreds place.\n- Third digit (hundreds place of 928): 3×9=27. Write 7 in thousands place, carryover 2 to ten-thousands place. Then the carried over 2 is written in the ten-thousands place. So the number is 27,840. Yes, that matches.\n\nOkay, so second partial product is 27,840.\n\nNow, moving to the next digit in the multiplier, which is 2 (in the hundreds place). Since it's the hundreds place, we need to shift two positions to the left, so we'll add two zeros at the end of the partial product before multiplying.\n\nSo, multiplying 928 by 2 (hundreds place):.\n\nFirst, the partial product will be shifted two places, so effectively multiplying by 200. So 928 × 200 = 185,600.\n\nAlternatively, let's do it step by step:.\n\nMultiplying 928 by 2 (hundreds place):.\n\n- Since it's the hundreds place, we start two places to the left, so we have two zeros at the end before multiplying.\n\nWait, actually, the process is similar to before, but shifted two places:.\n\nMultiply each digit of 928 by 2 (hundreds digit), and shift two places (i.e., multiply by 200).\n\nSo:.\n\n- 2×8 =16. Write 6 in the hundreds place (since shifted two places, units and tens are zeros?), wait, maybe better to do it step by step.\n\nWait, let's see:.\n\nMultiplying by the hundreds digit (2), so:.\n\n- Units place (after shift): two zeros.\n- Tens place: also part of the shift, so zeros.\n- Starting from the hundreds place:.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to compute 928 ×2 = 1,856, then shift two places (multiply by 100)