usands: 5\n\n- Ten thousands: 4\n\nThird number: 352,800:\n\n- Units: 0\n\n- Tens: 0\n\n- Hundreds: 8\n\n- Thousands: 2\n\n- Ten thousands: 5\n\n- Hundred thousands: 3\n\nHmm, this might be confusing. Maybe it's better to add them step by step.\n\nAlternatively, let me add the first two numbers first, then add the third.\n\nFirst, 504 + 45,360 = 45,864 as I had before. Then adding 352,800 to that:\n\n45,864 + 352,800. Let's do this addition carefully.\n\n45,864 + 352,800. Let's break it down:\n\n352,800 + 45,864.\n\nAdding the thousands: 352,800 + 45,000 = 397,800.\n\nThen add the remaining 864: 397,800 + 864 = 398,664.\n\nHmm, so that gives me 398,664. But let me verify this with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another multiplication approach. Let's see if there's a smarter way to compute 504 \u00d7 791 without the standard algorithm.\n\nPerhaps breaking down the numbers into smaller components. Let me try using the distributive property.\n\n504 \u00d7 791 can be written as 504 \u00d7 (700 + 90 + 1) = 504\u00d7700 + 504\u00d790 + 504\u00d71, which is exactly what I did earlier. So that gives me the same partial products, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using 500 + 4 instead?\n\nLet me try that. So, express 504 as 500 + 4, and then multiply by 791.\n\nSo (500 + 4) \u00d7 791 = 500\u00d7791 + 4\u00d7791.\n\nFirst compute 500\u00d7791. 500\u00d7700 = 350,000; 500\u00d790 = 45,000; 500\u00d71 = 500. So adding those together: 350,000 + 45,000 = 395,000; plus 500 is 395,500.\n\nThen compute 4\u00d7791: 4\u00d7700 = 2,800; 4\u00d790 = 360; 4\u00d71 = 4. Adding those: 2,800 + 360 = 3,160; plus 4 is 3,164.\n\nNow add those two results: 395,500 + 3,164 = 398,664. Same result as before. Good, that matches.\n\nAlternatively, let me try using another method, perhaps the lattice method or another way, just to confirm again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using estimation to check if 398,664 is reasonable.\n\n504 is approximately 500, and 791 is appr