      "index": 0,
      "text": "140,715? Let me check.\n\nWait, 34,515 + 100,000 =134,515. Then add 6,200: 134,515 +6,200 = 140,715. Yes, that seems right.\n\nWait, but let me verify with another method to be sure. Maybe using the distributive property: (500 + 30 +1) \u00d7 (200 +60 +5).\n\nExpanding this, it would be 500\u00d7200 +500\u00d760 +500\u00d75 +30\u00d7200 +30\u00d760 +30\u00d75 +1\u00d7200 +1\u00d760 +1\u00d75.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n500\u00d7200 = 100,000.\n\n500\u00d760 = 30,000.\n\n500\u00d75 = 2,500.\n\n30\u00d7200 =6,000.\n\n30\u00d760 =1,800.\n\n30\u00d75=150.\n\n1\u00d7200=200.\n\n1\u00d760=60.\n\n1\u00d75=5.\n\nNow add all these up:\n\n100,000 +30,000 =130,000.\n\n130,000 +2,500 =132,500.\n\n132,500 +6,000 =138,500.\n\n138,500 +1,800 =140,300.\n\n140,300 +150 =140,450.\n\n140,450 +200 =140,650.\n\n140,650 +60 =140,710.\n\n140,710 +5 =140,715.\n\nOkay, so that matches the previous result. Good, so that's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the lattice method? Hmm, but that might take longer. Alternatively, perhaps approximate to check?\n\n531 \u00d7265. Let's see, 500\u00d7250=125,000, and then adding the extra 31\u00d7250=7,750, so 125,000+7,750=132,750, then 500\u00d715=7,500, so 132,750+7,500=140,250, then adding 31\u00d715=465, so total would be 140,250+465=140,715. Wait, that also gives the same answer. Hmm, that's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using calculator-like step-by-step.\n\nAlternatively, note that 531\u00d7265 =531\u00d7(200+60+5) as above. So seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard algorithm again, but more carefully.\n\nWait, let me recheck the partial products again to be thorough.\n\nFirst partial product: 531\u00d75.\n\n5\u00d71=5, 5\u00d73=15 (carry 1), 5\u00d75=25 +1=26. So 2655. That's correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 531\u00d76 (tens place, so \u00d760). So 531\u00d76:\n\n6\u00d71=6, 6\u00d73=18 (carry 1), 6\u00d75=30 +1=31. So 3186. Then shift one to the left: 31,860. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 531\u00d72 (hundreds place, so \u00d7200). 531\u00d72=1062, shifted two places: 106,200. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 2655 +31860 =34,515; 34,515 +106,200 =140,715. So that's correct.\n\nWait, just to confirm once more, perhaps using another breakdown.\n\nLet me compute 531 \u00d7 265 as (500 +31) \u00d7 (200 +65). Maybe:\n\nFirst, (500+31)\u00d7(200+65)=500\u00d7200 +500\u00d765 +31\u00d7200 +31\u00d765.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n500\u00d7200=100,000.\n\n500\u00d765= 500\u00d760 +500\u00d75 =30,000 +2,500=32,500.\n\n31\u00d7200=6,200.\n\n31\u00d765: Let's compute that. 30\u00d765=1,950; 1\u00d765=65. So total is 1,950+65=2,015.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n100,000 +32,500 =132,500.\n\n132,500 +6,200 =138,700.\n\n138,700 +2,015 =140,715. Yep, same result. So that's consistent again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard multiplication in another way. Let me try multiplying 265 by 531 instead to see if it's the same.\n\n265 \u00d7531.\n\nSet it up:\n\n    265\n  \u00d7531\n  -------\n\nStarting with 1 (units place) \u00d7265 =265.\n\nThen 3 (tens place) \u00d7265, so 3\u00d7265 =795, shifted one left:7,950.\n\nThen 5 (hundreds place) \u00d7265, so 5\u00d7265=1,325, shifted two left:132,500.\n\nAdding them:\n\n265 +7,950 =8,215.\n\n8,215 +132,500 =140,715. Same result. So commutative property holds, which is good.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 140,715. Seems solid.\n\nAlternatively, just to ensure I didn't make a mistake in the initial partial products, perhaps do 531 \u00d7265 step-by-step.\n\nAlternatively, use a calculator. Wait, since I can't actually use a calculator, but given that all manual methods give the same result, I can be confident.\n\nTherefore, the product of 531 and 265 is 140,715.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 531 and 265 is \\boxed{140715}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 531 and 265, we use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 531 by 5 (units place of 265):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     531 \\\\\n   \\times \\ 5 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     2655 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Result: 2655\n\n2. **Multiply 531 by 6 (tens place of 265, which is 60):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     531 \\\\\n   \\times \\ 6 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     3186 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Shift one place to the left: 31860\n\n3. **Multiply 531 by 2 (hundreds place of 265, which is 200):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     531 \\\\\n   \\times \\ 2 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     1062 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Shift two places to the left: 106200\n\n4. **Add all partial products:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     2655 \\\\\n   + 31860 \\\\\n   +106200 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     140715 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(500 + 30 + 1) \\times (200 + 60 + 5) = 100000 + 30000 + 2500 + 6000 + 1800 + 150 + 200 + 60 + 5 = 140715\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 531 and 265 is \\boxed{140715}.",
