65,076. There we go again. So even prime factorization leads to the same answer. Definitely, 65,076 is the product.\n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and cross-verifying, I can confidently say the product of 174 and 374 is 65,076.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 174 and 374 is \\boxed{65076}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 174 and 374, we can use multiple methods to verify the result:\n\n1. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Break down 374 into 300 + 70 + 4.\n   - Calculate each part:\n     - \\(174 \\times 300 = 52,200\\)\n     - \\(174 \\times 70 = 12,180\\)\n     - \\(174 \\times 4 = 696\\)\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     52,200 + 12,180 + 696 = 65,076\n     \\]\n\n2. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 174 by each digit of 374 and sum the results:\n     - \\(174 \\times 4 = 696\\)\n     - \\(174 \\times 70 = 12,180\\)\n     - \\(174 \\times 300 = 52,200\\)\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     696 + 12,180 + 52,200 = 65,076\n     \\]\n\n3. **Alternative Breakdown**:\n   - Express 374 as \\(380 - 6\\):\n     - \\(174 \\times 380 = 66,120\\)\n     - \\(174 \\times 6 = 1,044\\)\n   - Subtract the results:\n     \\[\n     66,120 - 1,044 = 65,076\n     \\]\n\n4. **Prime Factorization**:\n   - Factorize 174 and 374:\n     - \\(174 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 29\\)\n     - \\(374 = 2 \\times 11 \\times 17\\)\n   - Multiply the factors:\n     \\[\n     (2 \\times 3 \\times 29) \\times (2 \\times 11 \\times 17) = 2^2 \\times 3 \\times 11 \\times 17 \\times 29 = 65,076\n     \\]\n\nAll methods consistently yield the same result. Therefore, the product of 174 and 374 is \\(\\boxed{65076}\\).",
