ultiple times with the same result, I can be more confident in 727,324.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute using another method, like the lattice method, but that might take longer. Alternatively, check divisibility or digit sums?\n\nLet me check the digit sum method for verification. The digit sum (mod 9) of a product should be equal to the product of the digit sums mod 9.\n\nFirst, compute digit sums of 923 and 788:\n\nDigit sum of 923: 9 + 2 + 3 =14, then 1+4=5. So mod9, it's 5.\n\nDigit sum of 788:7+8+8=23, 2+3=5. Mod9, it's 5.\n\nMultiply those digit sums:5*5=25, mod9: 25 mod9 is 25-2*9=25-18=7.\n\nNow compute the digit sum of the result 727,324:7+2+7+3+2+4=25, which is 2+5=7 mod9. So that matches. That\u2019s a good consistency check.\n\nAlternatively, check with another modulus, like mod 11.\n\nDigit sum alternating for 923: (9 - 2 +3)=10 mod11 is 10.\n\nFor 788: (7 -8 +8)=7 mod11 is 7.\n\nMultiply:10*7=70 mod11. 11*6=66, so 70-66=4 mod11.\n\nNow the result 727,324: compute alternating sum:\n\nStarting from the right: 4 (units) -2 (tens) +3 (hundreds) -7 (thousands) +2 (ten thousands) -7 (hundred thousands) ?\n\nWait, the number is 727,324. Let's write the digits: 7 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands),7 (thousands), 3 (hundreds),2 (tens),4 (units). So positions are:\n\nDigits: 7 (100,000), 2 (10,000),7 (1,000),3 (100),2 (10),4(1).\n\nAlternating sum from right: starting with the units digit as positive:\n\n4 (position 1) -2 (position 2) +3 (position3) -7 (position4) +2 (position5) -7 (position6).\n\nWait, depending on the direction, sometimes it's from the right. Let me recall: the alternating sum for divisibility by 11 is (sum of digits at odd positions) - (sum at even positions).\n\nPositions are counted from the right, starting at 1.\n\nSo for 727324:\n\nFrom right:\n\nPosition 1 (units):4\n\nPosition2:2\n\nPosition3:3\n\nPosition4:7\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition6:7\n\nSo sum of odd positions (1,3,5):4 +3 +2=9\n\nSum of even positions (2,4,6):2 +7 +7=16