y another method to verify. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nLet me write it out as:\n\n       347\n     \u00d7262\n     -------\nFirst, multiply 347 by 2 (units place):\n\n347 \u00d7 2 = 694.\n\nThen multiply 347 by 60 (tens place, so shift one position):\n\n347 \u00d7 6 = 2082, then add a zero gives 20,820.\n\nThen multiply 347 by 200 (hundreds place, shift two positions):\n\n347 \u00d7 2 = 694, add two zeros gives 69,400.\n\nNow add all three:\n\n694\n\n20,820\n\n69,400\n\nAdding them together:\n\n694 + 20,820 = 21,514\n\n21,514 + 69,400 = 90,914. So that's the same as before. So seems 90,914 is the product.\n\nWait but let me cross-verify with another method. Maybe using the distributive property in another way.\n\n347 \u00d7 262 can be written as (300 + 40 +7) \u00d7 (200 + 60 + 2). Then expanding this would give all possible products. That might take longer, but perhaps verify.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use another multiplication technique, like the lattice method or something else, but maybe that's overcomplicating. Alternatively, check using a calculator method mentally?\n\nAlternatively, let me see if 347 \u00d7 262 is indeed 90,914. Let me do a quick estimation to check if that makes sense. 300\u00d7250=75,000. 350\u00d7260=91,000. So 347\u00d7262 should be around there. 90,914 is very close to 91,000, so that seems plausible.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can compute 347 \u00d7 260 + 347 \u00d72. Since 262 is 260 +2.\n\n347 \u00d7260: let's compute that. 347\u00d7200=69,400. 347\u00d760=20,820. Adding those gives 69,400 +20,820=90,220. Then add 347\u00d72=694. So total is 90,220 +694=90,914. Yep same result. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can check with another multiplication approach.\n\nLet me compute 347 \u00d7 262 as follows:\n\nFirst, note that 262 is 260 +2. So 347\u00d7260 is 347\u00d726\u00d710. Let me compute 347\u00d726 first.\n\n347 \u00d726: Break it down as 347\u00d720 + 347\u00d76.\n\n347\u00d720=6,940.\