ferent methods all converge to the same result, so I'm confident that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 347 and 262 is \\boxed{90914}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 347 and 262, we can use the standard multiplication method by breaking down the multiplication into smaller parts and then summing the results.\n\nFirst, we multiply 347 by each digit of 262, starting from the rightmost digit:\n\n1. **Multiply 347 by 2 (units place):**\n   \\[\n   347 \\times 2 = 694\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 347 by 60 (tens place):**\n   \\[\n   347 \\times 6 = 2082 \\quad \\text{(then add a zero)} \\quad 20820\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 347 by 200 (hundreds place):**\n   \\[\n   347 \\times 2 = 694 \\quad \\text{(then add two zeros)} \\quad 69400\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum the results of these multiplications:\n\\[\n694 + 20820 + 69400\n\\]\n\nAdding these step-by-step:\n1. Add 694 and 20820:\n   \\[\n   694 + 20820 = 21514\n   \\]\n\n2. Add the result to 69400:\n   \\[\n   21514 + 69400 = 90914\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by breaking down the numbers:\n- Express 347 as \\(350 - 3\\) and 262 as \\(260 + 2\\)\n- Use the distributive property:\n  \\[\n  (350 - 3)(260 + 2) = 350 \\times 260 + 350 \\times 2 - 3 \\times 260 - 3 \\times 2\n  \\]\n- Compute each term:\n  \\[\n  350 \\times 260 = 91000, \\quad 350 \\times 2 = 700, \\quad -3 \\times 260 = -780, \\quad -3 \\times 2 = -6\n  \\]\n- Sum the terms:\n  \\[\n  91000 + 700 - 780 - 6 = 90914\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. Therefore, the product of 347 and 262 is \\(\\boxed{90914}\\).",
