dd the entries in the right column where the left column is odd.\n\nLet me do this step by step:\n\nLeft (a) | Right (b)\n\n662 | 356\n\n331 (halved, since 662/2=331, which is integer?) Wait, 662 divided by 2 is 331, which is exact, so no remainder. So 331 is next.\n\n331 | 712 (356\u00d72)\n\nNow 331 is odd, so we keep it, and proceed.\n\nNext row: 331/2 =165.5 \u2192 discard remainder \u2192165.\n\nRight column: 712\u00d72=1,424.\n\nLeft:165 | Right:1,424\n\n165 is odd, so note it.\n\n165/2=82.5 \u219282.\n\nRight:1,424\u00d72=2,848.\n\nLeft:82 | Right:2,848\n\n82 is even, so skip.\n\n82/2=41.\n\nRight:2,848\u00d72=5,696.\n\nLeft:41 | Right:5,696\n\n41 is odd, so note it.\n\n41/2=20.5\u219220.\n\nRight:5,696\u00d72=11,392.\n\nLeft:20 | Right:11,392\n\n20 is even, skip.\n\n20/2=10.\n\nRight:11,392\u00d72=22,784.\n\nLeft:10 | Right:22,784\n\n10 is even, skip.\n\n10/2=5.\n\nRight:22,784\u00d72=45,568.\n\nLeft:5 | Right:45,568\n\n5 is odd, note it.\n\n5/2=2.5\u21922.\n\nRight:45,568\u00d72=91,136.\n\nLeft:2 | Right:91,136\n\n2 is even, skip.\n\n2/2=1.\n\nRight:91,136\u00d72=182,272.\n\nLeft:1 | Right:182,272\n\n1 is odd, so note it.\n\nNow, we stop when we reach 1 on the left.\n\nNow, the numbers in the right column that correspond to odd numbers in the left column are:\n\n331: 712\n\n165:1,424\n\n41:5,696\n\n5:45,568\n\n1:182,272\n\nNow, adding those together:\n\nLet's add them step by step.\n\nStart with 712 +1,424 = 2,136.\n\n2,136 +5,696 =7,832.\n\n7,832 +45,568 =53,400.\n\n53,400 +182,272 =235,672.\n\nSame result again! Wow, so that's consistent. So that gives me more confidence.\n\nTherefore, after multiple methods: standard algorithm, distributive property breakdown, and Russian peasant multiplication, all leading to 235,672, I can be confident that the product of 662 and 356 is 235,672.\n\nJust to ensure I didn't make a mistake in any step, let me do a quick check using another method, maybe modulo 9 or something.\n\nCompute the digital roots of 662 and 356, m