\nCalculating each term:\n\n700\u00d7600 = 420,000\n\n700\u00d726 = 18,200 (since 700\u00d720=14,000 and 700\u00d76=4,200; 14,000+4,200=18,200)\n\n20\u00d7600 =12,000\n\n20\u00d726 =520\n\nNow adding all these together:\n\n420,000 +18,200 =438,200\n\n438,200 +12,000 =450,200\n\n450,200 +520 =450,720. \n\nSame answer again. So that must be correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator would be fastest, but since I don't have one here, verifying through multiple methods seems to confirm that 450,720 is the correct product. \n\nWait, but just to ensure, let me do the multiplication step by step once again:\n\n720 \u00d7626.\n\nBreak down 626 as 600 + 20 +6.\n\nSo:\n\nFirst, 720 \u00d7 600 = 432,000.\n\nSecond, 720 \u00d7 20 =14,400.\n\nThird, 720 \u00d76 =4,320.\n\nAdding them up: 432,000 +14,400 = 446,400. Then, 446,400 +4,320 = 450,720.\n\nYes, that's consistent. \n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and checks, I'm confident that the product of 720 and 626 is 450,720. I must have made an addition error when I thought I got 454,720 before. So the correct answer is 450,720.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 720 and 626 is \\boxed{450720}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 720 and 626, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\n1. Calculate \\(720 \\times 600\\):\n   \\[\n   720 \\times 600 = 432,000\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(720 \\times 20\\):\n   \\[\n   720 \\times 20 = 14,400\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(720 \\times 6\\):\n   \\[\n   720 \\times 6 = 4,320\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the results of the three calculations together:\n   \\[\n   432,000 + 14,400 + 4,320\n   \\]\n\nFirst, add \\(432,000\\) and \\(14,400\\):\n   \\[\n   432,000 + 14,400 = 446,400\n   \\]\n\nThen, add the result to \\(4,320\\):\n   \\[\n   446,400 + 4,320 = 450,720\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 720 and 626 is \\(\\boxed{450720}\\).",
