0 = 560 \u00d7 (1000/2) = (560 \u00d71000)/2 = 560,000 / 2 = 280,000. That works too.\n\nI think all these methods consistently give me 280,000, so I'm pretty confident that's the correct answer. Wait, but let me check with a calculator just to be sure. Hmm, since I can't use a calculator, I can do another manual check. Let's see, 560 \u00d7 500. If I break down 500 into 500, then 560\u00d7500. Alternatively, maybe 56\u00d75\u00d710,000. Wait, 560 is 56\u00d710 and 500 is 5\u00d7100. So 56\u00d710 \u00d75\u00d7100 = (56\u00d75) \u00d7 (10\u00d7100) = 280 \u00d71,000 = 280,000. Yep, that still holds.\n\nAlternatively, let me do the multiplication step by step without breaking it down. 560 times 500. Let's write it as 560 multiplied by 500. Since 500 is three digits, but the last two are zeros. So when multiplying, the non-zero digit is the 5 in the hundreds place. So 5 (hundreds) \u00d7 560 = 2800 hundreds. Since 2800 hundreds is 280,000. Exactly. That's the same as before.\n\nAnother way: 560 \u00d7 500 = 560 \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 100) = (560 \u00d7 5) \u00d7 100 = 2800 \u00d7 100 = 280,000.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can think of 560 \u00d7 500 as (500 + 60) \u00d7 500 = 500\u00d7500 + 60\u00d7500. 500\u00d7500 is 250,000, and 60\u00d7500 is 30,000. Adding those gives 250k +30k = 280k. Yep, same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe even breaking down 560 into 500 + 60, as above. All methods lead to the same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe even think of it as 560 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 100. 560 \u00d75 is 2800, then \u00d7100 is 280,000.\n\nHmm, so all these different approaches are giving me the same answer. I think I can be confident that 560 multiplied by 500 is 280,000. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I think that's the correct product.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 560 and 500 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the multiplication**:\n   \\[\n   560 \\times 500 = 560 \\times (5 \\times 100)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Multiply 560 by 5**:\n   \\[\n   560 \\times 5 = 2,\