ed one, so 0000. So the second partial product is 0000. .\n\nNow moving to the next digit, which is 4 in the hundreds place. Wait, hold on. The bottom number is 402, so the digits are 4 (hundreds), 0 (tens), and 2 (units). So actually, when setting it up, the 4 is the hundreds place, so when multiplying by that, you have to shift two places to the left. Wait a second, maybe I messed up the order here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, actually, the standard algorithm is to multiply by each digit starting from the right, so the first digit is units, then tens, then hundreds. So the bottom number 402 has digits 2 (units), 0 (tens), and 4 (hundreds). Therefore, when we do the multiplication:.\n\nFirst partial product: 2 × 473 = 946 (units place, no shift).\nSecond partial product: 0 × 473 = 0, but shifted one to the left (tens place), so it's 0000 (with a zero in the tens place, so effectively 0).\nThird partial product: 4 × 473, but shifted two places to the left (hundreds place), so that would be 4 × 473 = 1892, then shifted two places, making it 189200?.\n\nWait, let me confirm. So if I multiply 473 by 400 (since the 4 is in the hundreds place), that would be 473 × 4 × 100. So 473 × 4 is 1892, then times 100 is 189200. So yes, the third partial product is 189200.\n\nThen, adding up all the partial products: 946 + 0 + 189200. .\n\nAdding 946 and 0 is 946. Then adding 946 to 189200. Let me do that step by step. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
