ect that. The second partial product (from the 9 in the tens place) is 5022, but shifted one place to the left, so it's 50220? Wait, no. Wait, actually, when you write it down:.\n\nThe first multiplication (by units digit 2) gives 1116.\n\nThen, the next line is multiplying by the 9 (tens digit), so you write it shifted one to the left, so it's 5022 but shifted, so the 2 is in the tens place, so it would be 5022 shifted left once, meaning 50220? Wait, let's see:.\n\nIf you have:.\n\n       558.\n     x 192.\n     -------.\n        1116  (2 x 558).\n       5022   (9 x 558, but shifted one position to the left).\n      558     (1 x 558, shifted two positions to the left).\n     ---------.\n      ?.\n\nWait, actually, when you shift one position to the left, the 5022 becomes 50220? Because adding a zero at the end? Or is it written as 5022 but indented one space?.\n\nYes, in the standard multiplication, each subsequent line is shifted left by one more position. So multiplying by the tens digit, you shift left once, so the first digit is in the tens column. So writing 5022 shifted left once would be 50220? Wait, no, actually, no. Let me think numerically:.\n\nIf you have 558 x 9 (the tens digit), the result is 5022, but since it's the tens place, you need to multiply by 10, so 5022 x10 = 50220. Then the next digit is the hundreds place (1 in 192), so 558 x1 x100 = 55800.\n\nTherefore, adding them up:.\n\n1116.\n\n+50220.\n\n+55800.\n\nWait, let me add these numbers step by step.\n\nFirst, 1116 + 50220 = ?.\n\n1116 + 50220:.\n\nAdding units digits: 6 + 0 =6.\n\nTens: 1 + 2 = 3.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 2 =3.\n\nThousands: 1 +0 =1 (from 1116) and 5 (from 50220). Wait, actually, let me write them out:.\n\n50220.\n\n+ 1116.\n\n= ?.\n\nStart from the right:.\n\n0 +6 =6.\n\n2 +1 =3.\n\n2 +1 =3.\n\n5 +1 =6.\n\nSo it would be 51336?.\n\nWait, let's do it properly:.\n\n50220.\n\n+  1116.\n\n= 50220 + 1000 is 51220, plus 116 is 51336. Yes.\n\nThen adding the third partial product: 55800.\n\n51336 +55800:.\n\n51336 + 55800:.\n\nAdding the thousands: 51,000 + 55,000 = 106,000.\n\nThen the remaining 336 + 800 = 1136.\n\nWait, perhaps better to do:.\n\n51336.\n\n+55800.\n\n= ?.\n\nAdding units digits: 6 +0 =6.\n\nTens: 3 +0 =3.\n\nHundreds: 3 +8 =11, write down 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 1 (from 51336) +5 (from 55800) +1 (carryover) =7.\n\nTen-thousands: 5 (from 51336) +5 (from 55800) =10.\n\nWait, perhaps step-by-step:.\n\n51336.\n\n+55800.\n\nStart from right:.\n\n6 +0 =6.\n\n3 +0=3.\n\n3 +8=11, write 1, carryover 1.\n\n1 (from 51336 thousands place) +5 (from 55800 hundreds place?) Wait, maybe aligning properly:.\n\n51336.\n\n+55800.\n\n--------.\n\nLet me write them aligned:.\n\n  51336.\n\n+55800.\n\n--------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\n6 + 0 =6.\n\n3 +0=3.\n\n3 +8=11, write 1, carryover 1.\n\n1 (from 51336's thousands place) +5 (from 55800's thousands place) +1 (carryover) =7.\n\n5 (from 51336's ten-thousands place) +5 (from 55800's ten-thousands place) =10.\n\nSo putting it all together: 10 (ten-thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 6 (units). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
