      "index": 0,
      "text": "74529 - 1 = 74528.\n\nWait, hold on, but let me confirm that. Let me check if I did that correctly. The original problem was 274 * 272. If I use the difference of squares, since 274 is 273 +1 and 272 is 273 -1, then yes, their product is 273\u00b2 -1. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do the multiplication the standard way to verify?\n\nLet me multiply 274 by 272 the traditional method to cross-verify.\n\n274 * 272:\n\nFirst, break down 272 into 200 + 70 + 2. Wait, maybe it's better to do the standard multiplication:\n\n       274\n     x272\n     -------\n       274 * 2 = 548\n      274 * 70 = 19,180 (since 274*7=1,918, so times 10 is 19,180)\n     274 * 200 = 54,800 (since 274*2=548, times 100 is 54,800)\n     Now add them all together:\n\n     54,800\n    +19,180\n    +    548\n\nLet me add 54,800 + 19,180 first. 54,800 + 19,180 = 73,980. Then add 548: 73,980 + 548 = 74,528. \n\nOkay, that matches the previous result of 74,528. So that's good. So both methods give the same answer, so I can be confident that the product is 74,528.\n\nAlternatively, another way to do it might be using the distributive property in another fashion, but I think the difference of squares was the fastest here since the numbers were equidistant from 273. That was a useful shortcut. \n\nJust to recap: When two numbers are equally distant from a central number (in this case, 273), their product can be expressed as the square of the central number minus the square of the distance. Since the distance here was 1 (273 +1 and 273 -1), it simplified to 273\u00b2 -1. That's a neat trick to remember for similar problems.\n\nSo yeah, I think that's solid. Both methods give the same answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just check the 273 squared again. 270 squared is 72,900. Then 270*3*2 is 1,620. Then 3 squared is 9. Adding those gives 72,900 + 1,620 = 74,520, plus 9 is 74,529. Subtract 1 gives 74,528. Yep, that's correct. And the standard multiplication also gives 74,528. \n\nI think that's the right answer. I can't see any errors in my steps. So I'm confident the product of 274 and 272 is 74,528.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 274 and 272 is \\boxed{74528}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 274 and 272, we can use the difference of squares formula. \n\nFirst, note that 274 and 272 are equidistant from 273. Specifically, 274 is \\(273 + 1\\) and 272 is \\(273 - 1\\). Using the difference of squares formula \\((a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n274 \\times 272 = (273 + 1)(273 - 1) = 273^2 - 1^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(273^2\\). Breaking it down using the formula \\((a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\\) where \\(a = 270\\) and \\(b = 3\\):\n\n\\[\n273^2 = (270 + 3)^2 = 270^2 + 2 \\times 270 \\times 3 + 3^2\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n- \\(270^2 = 72900\\)\n- \\(2 \\times 270 \\times 3 = 1620\\)\n- \\(3^2 = 9\\)\n\nAdding these together:\n\n\\[\n72900 + 1620 + 9 = 74529\n\\]\n\nThus, \n\n\\[\n273^2 - 1 = 74529 - 1 = 74528\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can also perform the multiplication directly:\n\n\\[\n274 \\times 272 = 274 \\times (200 + 70 + 2) = 274 \\times 200 + 274 \\times 70 + 274 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n- \\(274 \\times 200 = 54800\\)\n- \\(274 \\times 70 = 19180\\)\n- \\(274 \\times 2 = 548\\)\n\nAdding these together:\n\n\\[\n54800 + 19180 + 548 = 74528\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. The product of 274 and 272 is \\(\\boxed{74528}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
