good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method: standard multiplication.\n\nLet me write 440\n\n\u00d7714\n\n--------\n\nMultiply 440 by 4 (units place of 714): 440 \u00d74 = 1,760.\n\nMultiply 440 by 1 (tens place of 714, which is actually 10): 440 \u00d710=4,400. But since it's the tens place, it's actually 440 \u00d71 (tens digit) \u00d710, so shifted one position. So 4,400.\n\nThen multiply 440 by 7 (hundreds place of 714, which is 700): 440 \u00d7700 = 308,000.\n\nNow add them all together:\n\n1,760 (from units)\n\n+44,000 (wait, hold on, maybe I messed up shifts here)\n\nWait, actually, standard multiplication requires:\n\nWhen you multiply by the tens digit, you shift one position to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 10), and by the hundreds digit, you shift two positions (equivalent to multiplying by 100).\n\nWait, let me do it step by step:\n\nFirst line: 440 \u00d74 (units) = 1,760\n\nSecond line: 440 \u00d71 (tens place) but shifted one position left \u2192 4,400 (because 1\u00d7440=440, but shifted left gives 4,400)\n\nThird line: 440 \u00d77 (hundreds place) shifted two positions left \u2192 440 \u00d77 = 3,080, shifted two places becomes 308,000.\n\nNow sum them up:\n\n1,760\n\n+4,400\n\n+308,000\n\n= 1,760 +4,400 = 6,160; 6,160 +308,000 = 314,160. Same result again. Okay, so that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the lattice method or something else, but since I already got the same answer three different ways, I think it's safe to say 314,160 is correct.\n\nBut just to be absolutely thorough, let me try another approach. Let me convert both numbers into factors and see if that helps, but I don't see an obvious factorization here. Alternatively, maybe using calculator-like step-by-step.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking down 714 into 700 + 14 is what I did earlier, so that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, let me see if there's another way. Let's try 440 \u00d7714.\n\nNotice that 440 \u00d7714 = (44 \u00d710) 