pport the result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 506 \u00d7 901 as (500 + 6) \u00d7 (900 + 1) and expand it:\n\n= 500\u00d7900 + 500\u00d71 + 6\u00d7900 + 6\u00d71\n\n= 450,000 + 500 + 5,400 + 6\n\nAdding those up:\n\n450,000 + 500 = 450,500\n\n450,500 + 5,400 = 455,900\n\n455,900 +6 = 455,906. \n\nThere we go, that's the same result again. So that seems solid. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check with a calculator, but since I can't use one, but I can do another multiplication method.\n\nLet me try the standard vertical multiplication step by step again:\n\nMultiplying 506 by 901.\n\nStarting with the ones digit:\n\n506 \u00d7 1 = 506. Write that down.\n\nThen the tens digit is 0, so 506 \u00d7 0 = 0, but shifted one place to the left (so it's like \u00d710), so it becomes 0000 (but effectively 0).\n\nThen the hundreds digit is 9, so 506 \u00d7 9. Let's compute that again:\n\n506 \u00d7 9:\n\n6\u00d79=54, write down 4, carryover 5.\n\n0\u00d79=0 + 5 carried over =5.\n\n5\u00d79=45, write 5, carryover 4.\n\nThen the remaining 4? Wait, 506 is three digits, so 5 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones). So 9 times 506:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6\u00d79=54, so units place is 4, carryover 5.\n\nNext digit: 0\u00d79 + 5 = 5. So tens place is 5.\n\nNext digit: 5\u00d79 =45. So hundreds place is 5 and carryover 4? Wait, but 5 is in the hundreds place, so 5\u00d79=45, so that's 45 hundreds, which is 4500. Wait, maybe I need to think in terms of place values. Anyway, the result is 4554, right?\n\nYes, 506 \u00d79 is 4554. Then since it's the hundreds digit (the 9 in 901), we shift two places to the left, so it becomes 455400.\n\nAdding up the partial products:\n\nFirst partial product: 506\n\nSecond: 0 (shifted one, so 0000)\n\nThird: 455400\n\nAdding them together:\n\n455400 + 0 = 455400\n\n455400 + 506 = 455,906. So that's correct.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods lead to the same answer, so I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in the calculations. Let me just check one more time with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the difference of squares or some other algebraic identity? Let's see.\n\nWait, 506 \u00d7 901. Hmm, not sure if that can be expressed as a difference of squares, but maybe if I can write it as (something squared minus something else squared). Alternatively, maybe breaking down the numbers into smaller components. Let me try:\n\nAlternatively, 901 is 900 +1, so that's the same as before. Alternatively, 506 is 500 +6.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think of 506 \u00d7 901 as (500 + 6)(900 +1). Which is exactly what I did earlier, and that gave 455,906. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe compute 506 \u00d7 900 first, which is 506 \u00d7 9 \u00d7 100. 506 \u00d79 is 4554, so \u00d7100 is 455,400. Then add 506 to get 455,906. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 506 \u00d7 901 = 506 \u00d7 (900 +1) = 506\u00d7900 +506\u00d71, which again is the same.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps check using another multiplication technique, like the Russian peasant method? Let's try that.\n\nRussian peasant multiplication involves doubling and halving:\n\nTo multiply 506 and 901:\n\nWe can write down two columns, starting with 506 and 901.\n\nWe halve the first number (ignoring remainders) and double the second number until the first column reaches 1.\n\nBut since 506 is even, let's start:\n\nFirst pair: 506 | 901\n\nHalve 506: 253, double 901: 1802.\n\nSecond pair: 253 | 1802\n\nHalve 253: 126.5, but we ignore fractions, so 126, double 1802: 3604.\n\nThird pair: 126 | 3604\n\nHalve 126:63, double 3604:7208.\n\nFourth pair:63 |7208\n\nHalve 63:31.5\u219231, double 7208:14416.\n\nFifth pair:31 |14416\n\nHalve 31\u219215.5\u219215, double 14416\u219228832.\n\nSixth pair:15 |28832\n\nHalve 15\u21927.5\u21927, double 28832\u219257664.\n\nSeventh pair:7 |57664\n\nHalve7\u21923.5\u21923, double\u2192115328.\n\nEighth pair:3 |115328\n\nHalve3\u21921.5\u21921, double\u2192230656.\n\nNow the first column is 1, so stop here.\n\nNinth pair:1 |230656\n\nNow, we need to sum the numbers in the second column that correspond to odd numbers in the first column.\n\nSo let's list the pairs:\n\n1. 506 | 901 (506 even, so not included)\n\n2. 253 |1802 (253 is odd, include 1802)\n\n3.126 |3604 (126 even, skip)\n\n4. 63 |7208 (63 odd, include)\n\n5.31 |14416 (31 odd, include)\n\n6.15 |28832 (15 odd, include)\n\n7.7 |57664 (7 odd, include)\n\n8.3 |115328 (3 odd, include)\n\n9.1 |230656 (1 odd, include)\n\nWait, so we need to add up all the second column numbers except for the first and third entries (since those first numbers were even). Let's see:\n\nSo adding:\n\n1802 (from 253)\n\n+7208 (from 63)\n\n+14416 (from31)\n\n+28832 (from15)\n\n+57664 (from7)\n\n+115328 (from3)\n\n+230656 (from1)\n\nLet me add these step by step:\n\nStart with 1802 +7208 = 9010\n\n9010 +14416 = 23426\n\n23426 +28832 = 52258\n\n52258 +57664 = 109,922\n\n109,922 +115,328 = 225,250\n\n225,250 +230,656 = 455,906.\n\nWow, that's the same result! So the Russian peasant method also gives 455,906. So that's another confirmation.\n\nHmm, so this makes me confident that the answer is correct. \n\nAlternatively, let me try another check using digit sums, maybe?\n\nThe digit sum (casting out nines) method. Let's see if the digit sum of the product matches the product of the digit sums modulo 9.\n\nFirst, compute digit sum of 506:\n\n5 + 0 +6=11. 1+1=2. So digit sum is 2 mod9.\n\nDigit sum of 901: 9+0+1=10\u21921 mod9.\n\nMultiply those: 2\u00d71=2 mod9.\n\nNow compute the digit sum of the result 455,906:\n\n4+5+5+9+0+6 = 4+5=9, 9+5=14, 14+9=23, 23+0=23, 23+6=29. 29 mod9: 2+9=11, 11 mod9=2. So digit sum is 2 mod9, which matches. That's a good sign.\n\nAnother check: Let's see if 455,906 divided by 506 gives 901.\n\nDivide 455,906 \u00f7506.\n\nLet me compute 506 \u00d7900 = 455,400. Subtract that from 455,906: 455,906 -455,400=506. So 506 \u00f7506=1. So total is 900+1=901. Perfect, so that division checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe divide