ond partial product should be shifted one place, so it's actually 2060 (but with a zero at the end?), no. Wait, perhaps I need to align them properly.\n\nLet me write them all aligned by their place values:.\n\nFirst partial product (×2): \xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0824.\n\nSecond partial product (×50): \xa0\xa02060 (but shifted one, so it's 2060 but written as 2060 with the zero in the units place? Wait, actually, when multiplying by the tens digit, the first digit of the partial product is in the tens place. So the second partial product is 2060, but written as:.\n\n\u2003\xa0412.\n×\xa0652.\n-------.\n\u2003\xa0824 \xa0\xa0(×2).\n+2060 \xa0\xa0(×50, shifted left once).\n+247200 (×600, shifted left twice).\n-------.\n?.\n\nWait, actually, when you multiply by the tens digit (5), you should write the result shifted one place to the left, so it's 2060, but when written under, it's:.\n\n\u2003\xa0412.\n×\xa0652.\n-------.\n\u2003\xa0824 \xa0\xa0(2×412).\n+2060 \xa0\xa0(50×412).\n+247200 (600×412).\n-------.\nTotal.\n\nSo adding them up:.\n\nStart with 824 + 2060 = 2884.\n\nThen 2884 + 247200 = ?.\n\nLet me compute that step by step:.\n\n2884 + 247200:.\n\n247200 + 2000 = 249200.\n\n249200 + 884 = 250,084?.\n\nWait, 247,200 + 2,884. Let me check:.\n\n247,200 + 2,000 = 249,200.\n\n249,200 + 884 = 250,084.\n\nHmm, but let me confirm the addition again. Maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, 824 + 2060:.\n\n824 + 2000 = 2824.\n\n2824 + 60 = 2884. That's correct.\n\nThen adding 247,200 to 2,884:.\n\n247,200 + 2,884 = ?.\n\n247,200 + 2,000 = 249,200.\n\n249,200 + 884 = 250,084. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the total would be 250,084.\n\nWait, but let me verify this with another method to be sure. Maybe using the distributive property: (400 + 12) × (600 + 52). Hmm, that might be more complicated, but let's see.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking down the numbers:.\n\n412 × 652 = 412 × (600 + 50 + 2) = 412×600 + 412×50 + 412×2, which is exactly what we did earlier, so that gives the sa