8. Yep, same result. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, I can do another method, like the distributive property again, but maybe breaking down numbers differently. Let me see. Maybe 667 times 300 is 200,100, then 667 times 4 is 2,668, so total is 202,768. That's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another approach, like (600 + 67) \u00d7 304. Let's see if that works.\n\n600\u00d7304 = 600\u00d7300 + 600\u00d74 = 180,000 + 2,400 = 182,400  \n67\u00d7304 = ?\n\nHmm, calculating 67\u00d7304. Let's break that down:\n\n67\u00d7300 = 20,100  \n67\u00d74 = 268  \nAdding those: 20,100 + 268 = 20,368  \nNow, adding to the previous part: 182,400 + 20,368 = 202,768. Same result again. Okay, so this method also works. So that's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard algorithm again but in a different way. Let me write it out again:\n\nMultiply 304 by 667. Wait, but since multiplication is commutative, it's the same as 667 \u00d7 304. Let me try writing it as 304 \u00d7 667. Maybe that helps.\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0304  \n\u00d7\u00a0667  \n________\n\nFirst, multiply 304 by 7 (units place): 304\u00d77 = 2,128  \nThen multiply 304 by 6 (tens place, so shifted one left): 304\u00d76 = 1,824 \u2192 shifted left becomes 18,240  \nThen multiply 304 by 6 (hundreds place, shifted two left): 304\u00d76 = 1,824 \u2192 shifted two left becomes 182,400  \nNow add all three results:\n\n2,128  \n18,240  \n182,400  \n________  \nAdding them: \n\nFirst, 2,128 + 18,240 = 20,368  \nThen 20,368 + 182,400 = 202,768. Same answer again. So that's consistent.\n\nHmm, so all the different methods give me 202,768. I think that must be the correct answer. Wait, but let me just do a quick estimation to check if this is reasonable. \n\n667 is approximately 700, and 304 is approximately 300. 700\u00d7300 is 210,000. Since both numbers are a bit less than those approximations, the actual product should be a bit less than 210,000. 202,768 is about 7,232 less, which se