ome other multiplication trick. For example, maybe (400 + 24)(600 + 12). Let's expand that:\n\n400\u00d7600 + 400\u00d712 + 24\u00d7600 + 24\u00d712\n\nCompute each term:\n\n400\u00d7600 = 240,000\n\n400\u00d712 = 4,800\n\n24\u00d7600 = 14,400\n\n24\u00d712 = 288\n\nAdding all those together:\n\n240,000 + 4,800 = 244,800\n\n244,800 +14,400 = 259,200\n\n259,200 + 288 = 259,488\n\nSame answer. So that's three different methods giving me 259,488. Hmm, seems like that's the right answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 424 \u00d7 612 as (420 + 4)(610 + 2). Let me try expanding that:\n\n420\u00d7610 + 420\u00d72 + 4\u00d7610 + 4\u00d72\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n420 \u00d7 610. Hmm, that's 420 \u00d7 600 + 420 \u00d7 10 = 252,000 + 4,200 = 256,200\n\nThen 420 \u00d72 = 840\n\n4 \u00d7610 = 2,440\n\n4\u00d72 = 8\n\nNow add them all up:\n\n256,200 + 840 = 257,040\n\n257,040 + 2,440 = 259,480\n\n259,480 +8 = 259,488. Yep, same result again.\n\nOkay, so all methods lead me to 259,488. I think that's solid. Let me just do a quick check with approximate estimation to see if that makes sense.\n\n424 is approximately 400, and 612 is approximately 600. 400\u00d7600 = 240,000. The actual answer is 259,488, which is a bit higher, which makes sense since both numbers are a bit higher than 400 and 600. The difference is about 19,488, which is reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, using calculator-like step-by-step:\n\nMultiplying 424 \u00d7 612.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nFirst partial product: 424 \u00d7 2 = 848\n\nSecond partial product: 424 \u00d7 1 (tens place, so \u00d710) \u2192 4240\n\nThird partial product: 424 \u00d7 6 (hundreds place, so \u00d7600) \u2192 254,400\n\nAdding them:\n\n848 + 4240 = 5088\n\n5088 + 254,400 = 259,488. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another approach. Let me see if I can compute 424 \u00d7 600 first, which is 254,400, then add 424 \u00d712 (since 612=600+12). Which is exactly what I did first. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, do 612 \u00