      "index": 0,
      "text": "425 + 12750 is 13175, then 13175 + 42500. Let me compute that: 13175 + 42500. 13175 + 40000 is 53175, then +2500 is 55675. Wait, that seems off. Wait, 13175 + 42500. Let me compute 13175 + 42500. 42500 + 10000 = 52500, then subtract 10000 - 13175? Wait, no. Alternatively, 42500 + 13175. Let me add the numbers digit by digit:\n\n42500\n\n+13175\n\n=?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 +5 =5\n\n0 +7=7\n\n5 +1=6\n\n2 +3=5\n\n4 +1=5\n\nWait, but written vertically:\n\n42500\n\n+\u00a013175\n\n=?\n\nLet me write it out:\n\n42500\n\n+\u00a013175\n\n--------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n0 +5 =5\n\n0 +7 =7\n\n5 +1 =6\n\n2 +3 =5\n\n4 +1 =5\n\nSo that would be 55675. Wait, yes, so 42500 + 13175 = 55675. So total is 55,675? Hmm, but let me check with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in shifting. Let me verify the shifting again. Let's see:.\n\nFirst partial product: 425 \u00d71 = 425 (no shift).\n\nSecond partial product: 425 \u00d730 = 12,750 (shifted one place left, so 12,750 is correct).\n\nThird partial product: 425 \u00d7100 = 42,500 (shifted two places left, so 42,500 is correct).\n\nAdding those three: 425 + 12,750 = 13,175; then 13,175 + 42,500. Let's do that again:\n\n13,175 + 42,500.\n\nAdding the thousands: 13,000 + 42,000 = 55,000.\n\nAdding the remaining 175: 55,000 + 175 = 55,175? Wait, no. Wait, 13,175 + 42,500:\n\n13,175\n\n+42,500\n\n=?\n\n13,175 + 40,000 = 53,175.\n\n53,175 + 2,500 = 55,675. Yes, that's correct. So total is 55,675.\n\nHmm, but let me confirm with another method, maybe using the distributive property.\n\n425 \u00d7 131 can be written as 425 \u00d7 (100 + 30 +1) = 425\u00d7100 +425\u00d730 +425\u00d71, which is exactly what I did. So that gives 42,500 + 12,750 + 425 = same as above, so that's 55,675.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way for cross-verification. Let me try the standard multiplication again step by step.\n\nMultiplying 425 \u00d7 131:\n\nFirst, write 425 \u00d7131.\n\nMultiply 425 by 1 (units digit): 425.\n\nMultiply 425 by 3 (tens digit): 425\u00d73=1275. Since it's the tens place, shift one to the left: 12750.\n\nMultiply 425 by 1 (hundreds digit): 425\u00d71=425, shifted two to the left: 42500.\n\nAdding them all: 425 + 12750 = 13175; 13175 +42500=55675. So that's the same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 425 \u00d7131 as (400 +25) \u00d7(100 +30 +1). Let's see:\n\nFirst expand the terms:\n\n400\u00d7100 = 40,000\n\n400\u00d730 = 12,000\n\n400\u00d71 = 400\n\n25\u00d7100 = 2,500\n\n25\u00d730 = 750\n\n25\u00d71 =25\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n40,000 +12,000=52,000\n\n52,000 +400=52,400\n\n52,400 +2,500=54,900\n\n54,900 +750=55,650\n\n55,650 +25=55,675. Yep, same result. So that's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, another way: maybe breaking 131 into 130 +1.\n\nSo 425\u00d7(130 +1)=425\u00d7130 +425\u00d71.\n\nCompute 425\u00d7130:\n\n425\u00d7100=42,500\n\n425\u00d730=12,750\n\nAdding those gives 42,500 +12,750 =55,250\n\nThen add 425\u00d71=425, so total is 55,250 +425=55,675. Same answer.\n\nOkay, so that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps do the multiplication straight without breaking down:.\n\nMultiplying 425 \u00d7131.\n\nStart with the units digit: 1 \u00d7425 =425.\n\nThen the tens digit: 3 \u00d7425. Let me compute 3\u00d7400=1200, 3\u00d725=75, so total 1275. Since it's the tens place, write 1275 shifted left by one, so 12750.\n\nThen the hundreds digit: 1 \u00d7425, shifted left by two, so 42500.\n\nAdding them all gives 55,675.\n\nHmm, so all methods point to 55,675. But just to be absolutely sure, maybe do the multiplication in another way, perhaps using a calculator method mentally.\n\nAlternatively, note that 425 \u00d7131.\n\nLet me think of 425 \u00d7100 =42,500.\n\n425 \u00d730 =12,750.\n\n425 \u00d71 =425.\n\nAdding those three gives 42,500 +12,750 is 55,250 +425 is 55,675. Yep, same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check via estimation to see if it's reasonable.\n\n425 is approximately 400, 131 is approx 130. 400\u00d7130=52,000. The actual answer should be a bit higher since 425 is 25 more than 400, so 25\u00d7130=3,250. So 52,000 +3,250=55,250. Then we also have the extra 1 from the 131 (since 130 +1). Wait, perhaps my estimation is getting confused, but the exact calculation is 55,675, which is 55,250 plus 425, so that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, another way: let me compute 425 \u00d7131 as follows.\n\nFirst, 425 \u00d7131 =425 \u00d7 (130 +1)=425\u00d7130 +425\u00d71.\n\nAs above.\n\nAlternatively, 425\u00d7131 = (400 +25)(130 +1) = 400\u00d7130 +400\u00d71 +25\u00d7130 +25\u00d71.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n400\u00d7130 =52,000\n\n400\u00d71=400\n\n25\u00d7130=3,250\n\n25\u00d71=25\n\nAdding them all:52,000 +400=52,400; 52,400 +3,250=55,650; 55,650 +25=55,675. Same result again.\n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and cross-verifying, I feel confident that the product of 425 and 131 is 55,675.\n\nWait, but just to be absolutely thorough, let me try another approach, perhaps using the complement method or something else? Hmm, maybe not necessary, but let me try the standard multiplication again step by step, writing each partial product.\n\nLet me write it out in the vertical form step by step.\n\nFirst, write 425 on top and 131 below:\n\n```.\n    425\n\n  x131\n\n  -------\n```.\n\nMultiply 425 by 1 (units digit):\n\n1\u00d75 =5, write down 5.\n\n1\u00d72=2, write down 2.\n\n1\u00d74=4, write down 4.\n\nSo first partial product: 425.\n\nNext, multiply by 3 (tens digit). Since it's the tens place, we start the next product one position to the left.\n\n3\u00d75 =15. Write down 5, carryover 1.\n\n3\u00d72 =6, plus the carried 1 is 7. Write 7.\n\n3\u00d74=12, write down 2, carryover 1.\n\nBut since we are in the hundreds place, the carried over 1 would go to the thousands place. Wait, let me do it step by step.\n\nWait, writing it out:\n\nMultiplying by the 3 (tens place):\n\n3\u00d75 (units) =15. Write down 5, carryover 1.\n\n3\u00d72 (tens) =6 +1 =7. Write 7.\n\n3\u00d74 (hundreds) =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nBut since we are in the hundreds place, the carryover 1 would be in the thousands place. Wait, but since we're multiplying by the tens digit, the first digit (units) of this partial product is in the tens place? Wait, actually, when multiplying by the tens digit, each digit's place shifts left by one. So the first digit (units of 3\u00d7425) would be in the tens place of the total.\n\nWait, perhaps an error here. Let me detail:\n\nWhen multiplying by the 3 (tens place), each digit's result is shifted left by one. So:\n\nStarting with the units digit of 425:\n\n3\u00d75 =15. The units place here becomes the tens place in the total, so the 5 is written in the tens column. The 1 is carried over to the next multiplication.\n\nNext digit: 3\u00d72 (tens place of 425) =6, plus the carried 1 =7. This is in the hundreds place of the total. So write 7 in the hundreds column.\n\nNext digit: 3\u00d74 (hundreds place of 425) =12. This would be in the thousands place, so write 2 and carryover 1 to the next (ten-thousands place). Since there are no more digits, the carried 1 is written in the next column.\n\nTherefore, the second partial product is 1275 shifted left by one, so it becomes 12750. Which matches what we had before.\n\nThird partial product, multiplying by the 1 (hundreds place of 131):\n\n1\u00d75 (units) =5, which goes in the hundreds place (since shifted two left). Wait, let me do step by step again.\n\nMultiplying by the 1 (hundreds place):\n\n1\u00d75 =5. Since it's the hundreds place, this 5 is in the hundreds column? Wait, no. Wait, when multiplying by the hundreds digit, each digit shifts two places to the left. So the units digit of the partial product will be in the hundreds place.\n\nWait, perhaps better to think:\n\nMultiplying 425 \u00d71 (hundreds place, so actually \u00d7100):\n\nSo each digit is shifted two places left. So:\n\n1\u00d75 =5, which is in the hundreds place (so written as 500).\n\n1\u00d72 =2, shifted two places becomes thousands place (2000).\n\n1\u00d74 =4, shifted two becomes ten-thousands place (40,000).\n\nHence, 40,000 + 2,000 + 500 =42,500. Which is exactly the third partial product.\n\nAdding all three partial products:\n\n425 (units) + 12,750 (tens) +42,500 (hundreds).\n\nAdding 425 +12,750 =13,175; then +42,500 =55,675. So that's consistent again.\n\nTherefore, after multiple checks and methods, I believe the correct product is 55,675. I don't see any miscalculations in my steps, so I think that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 425 and 131 is \\boxed{55675}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 425 and 131, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 425 by 1 (units place):**\n   \\[\n   425 \\times 1 = 425\n   \\]\n   This gives the first partial product: 425.\n\n2. **Multiply 425 by 3 (tens place):**\n   \\[\n   425 \\times 3 = 1275\n   \\]\n   Since this is the tens place, shift the result one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   1275 \\times 10 = 12750\n   \\]\n   This gives the second partial product: 12750.\n\n3. **Multiply 425 by 1 (hundreds place):**\n   \\[\n   425 \\times 1 = 425\n   \\]\n   Since this is the hundreds place, shift the result two positions to the left:\n   \\[\n   425 \\times 100 = 42500\n   \\]\n   This gives the third partial product: 42500.\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   425 + 12750 + 42500\n   \\]\n   - First, add 425 and 12750:\n     \\[\n     425 + 12750 = 13175\n     \\]\n   - Then add the result to 42500:\n     \\[\n     13175 + 42500 = 55675\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 425 and 131 is \\(\\boxed{55675}\\).",
