d 320,000 to 340,000. Our answer is 331,700, which is within that range, so it seems reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, let's do another verification using the difference of squares or another method? Hmm, maybe not straightforward here. Alternatively, perhaps I can compute 428 \u00d7775 and see if it equals 331,700.\n\nAlternatively, let me check with another multiplication method. Let's try to compute 428 \u00d7775 as follows:\n\nFirst, note that 775 = 700 + 70 +5, which we already did, but let me see another way. Maybe 775 is 1000 - 225. Hmm, not sure if that helps. Alternatively, 775\u00d7428. Let me think of 428 \u00d7775 as (400 +28) \u00d7 (700 +75). Maybe expanding that?\n\nWait, maybe that's more complicated. Alternatively, let me compute 428 \u00d7775 by breaking down into 400\u00d7775 +28\u00d7775.\n\nFirst, 400\u00d7775. 400 \u00d7700 = 280,000. 400\u00d775 = 30,000. So total 280,000 +30,000 =310,000.\n\nThen 28\u00d7775. Let's compute 28\u00d7700 =19,600 and 28\u00d775 = 2,100. So 19,600 +2,100 =21,700.\n\nAdding both parts: 310,000 +21,700 =331,700. Same result. Okay, so that's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, let's do 28\u00d775. Wait 28\u00d775: 28\u00d770=1960 and 28\u00d75=140, so 1960+140=2100, yes. So that works. So 28\u00d7775=28\u00d7(700+75)=19,600 +2,100=21,700. Then 400\u00d7775= 310,000. So 310k +21.7k is 331.7k, so 331,700. Perfect.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check with calculator steps.\n\nWait, since I can't use a calculator, but let me check the addition again:\n\nFirst partial products:\n\n428\u00d75 =2140\n\n428\u00d770=29,960\n\n428\u00d7700=299,600\n\nAdding those:\n\n2140 +29,960 =32,100\n\n32,100 +299,600 =331,700. Yep, same as before.\n\nHmm, seems consistent. Let me also check with another approach: multiplying 775 by 428 step by step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 775 \u00d7400, 775\u00d720, 775\u00d78, then add them.\n\n775\u00d7400 = 310,000 (as above)\n\n775\u00d720 =15,500\n\n775\u00d78 =6,200\n\nAdding those: 310,000 +15,500 =325,500;