, hold on. Wait, actually, when multiplying by the hundreds digit (8), it should be shifted two places to the left, so 988 \u00d7800 = 790,400, right? So the partial products are:\n\n3,952 (from \u00d74)\n\n0 (from \u00d70, but actually, the middle digit is 0, so when multiplied by 988 and shifted, it's 0000?)\n\nWait, maybe I messed up here. Let me write it step by step properly:\n\nMultiplying 988 \u00d7804:\n\nFirst, 988 \u00d74 = 3,952\n\nSecond, 988 \u00d70 (tens place) = 0, but since it's the tens place, it's actually 988 \u00d70 \u00d710 = 0, so writing this as 0 shifted one place to the left, which would be 00 (but actually, it's just 0).\n\nThird, 988 \u00d78 (hundreds place) = 7,904, but since it's the hundreds place, we need to shift two places to the left, so that's 7,904 \u00d7100 = 790,400.\n\nNow, adding all three:\n\n3,952\n\n+     0\n\n+790,400\n\n= 794,352. Okay, so that matches my previous result. So that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method, such as using (1000 - 12) \u00d7 (800 + 4). Wait, 988 is 1000 - 12, right? Let me see:\n\n988 = 1000 - 12,\n\n804 = 800 + 4.\n\nThen, (1000 -12)(800 +4) = 1000\u00d7800 + 1000\u00d74 -12\u00d7800 -12\u00d74.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n1000\u00d7800 = 800,000,\n\n1000\u00d74 = 4,000,\n\n-12\u00d7800 = -9,600,\n\n-12\u00d74 = -48.\n\nAdding all together: 800,000 +4,000 = 804,000,\n\nThen subtract 9,600: 804,000 -9,600 = 794,400,\n\nThen subtract 48: 794,400 -48 = 794,352. Same answer! So that's a third method, and it still gives 794,352. Hmm, seems like that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 988 \u00d7 800 first, then add 988 \u00d74.\n\n988 \u00d7800: 988 \u00d78 \u00d7100. 988 \u00d78 is 7,904, so \u00d7100 is 790,400.\n\nThen 988 \u00d74 is 3,952 as before. Adding 790,400 +3,952 = 794,352. Yep, same result.\n\nI think that's solid. All methods lead to 794,352. Therefore, I can be confident the answer is correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 988 and 804 is \\boxed{794352}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 988 and 804, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(900 + 88) \\times (800 + 4)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n900 \\times 800 + 900 \\times 4 + 88 \\times 800 + 88 \\times 4\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n1. \\(900 \\times 800 = 720,000\\)\n2. \\(900 \\times 4 = 3,600\\)\n3. \\(88 \\times 800 = 70,400\\)\n4. \\(88 \\times 4 = 352\\)\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\n\\[\n720,000 + 3,600 + 70,400 + 352\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(720,000 + 3,600\\):\n\n\\[\n720,000 + 3,600 = 723,600\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(723,600 + 70,400\\):\n\n\\[\n723,600 + 70,400 = 794,000\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(794,000 + 352\\):\n\n\\[\n794,000 + 352 = 794,352\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by multiplying directly:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n    988 \\\\\n\\times 804 \\\\\n\\hline\n    3952 \\quad (\\text{988} \\times 4) \\\\\n    0 \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (\\text{988} \\times 0 \\text{ shifted one place left}) \\\\\n+790400 \\quad (\\text{988} \\times 8 \\text{ shifted two places left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n    794352 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 988 and 804 is \\(\\boxed{794352}\\).",
