t one place to the left, so it becomes 25760. Wait, but when written as partial products, you would write 2576 with a zero in the units place? Hmm, actually, maybe not. Wait, no, when you do the multiplication step by step, you don\'t write the zero, you just shift the entire number one place to the left, so the 2576 becomes 25760. So when you write it under the first partial product:.\n\nFirst partial product (units digit):  966.\n\nSecond partial product (tens digit): 25760.\n\nThird partial product (hundreds digit): ?.\n\nNow, moving to the hundreds place of 683, which is 6. Since this is the hundreds place, the multiplication will be 322 multiplied by 600, so we need to multiply 322 by 6 and then shift two places to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 100).\n\nCalculating 322 x 6:.\n\n2 x 6 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nNext digit (tens): 2 x 6 = 12 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nNext digit (hundreds): 3 x 6 = 18 +1 = 19. So the result is 1932. Since this is the hundreds place, we need to shift two places, so 1932 x 100 = 193,200. Therefore, the third partial product is 193,200.\n\nNow, adding up all three partial products:.\n\nFirst:       966.\n\nSecond:    25,760.\n\nThird:   193,200.\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:.\n\n      966.\n\n    25,760.\n\n  193,200.\n\n----------.\n\nAdding them together.\n\nStart with the first two: 966 + 25,760 = let\'s see. 25,760 + 900 is 26,660, then +66 is 26,726.\n\nThen add that to 193,200:. Therefore, my final answer is: ']]
