mation to check if this is reasonable. 400 times 500 is 200,000. 400 times 600 would be 240,000, but since 565 is between 500 and 600, so the product should be between 200,000 and 240,000. 226,565 is in that range, so that seems reasonable. Also, 400*565 is 226,000, so adding another 565 makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using a calculator would be the surest way, but since I don't have one here, but since all methods are giving me the same result, I can be confident that 226,565 is the correct product.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me do the multiplication step-by-step as in the standard algorithm again, digit by digit.\n\nMultiplying 401 by 5 (units place):\n\n401 x 5: 5*1=5, 5*0=0, 5*4=20. So writing that down: 2005.\n\nThen multiplying 401 by 6 (tens place, so actually 60). Since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left.\n\n401 x 6: 6*1=6, 6*0=0, 6*4=24. So that gives 2406, but shifted one place left: 24,060.\n\nThen multiplying 401 by 5 (hundreds place, which is 500). So shift two places left.\n\n401 x5: same as before, 2005, shifted two places: 200,500.\n\nAdding all three:\n\n2005\n\n+24,060\n\n=26,065\n\n26,065 + 200,500 = 226,565. Yep, same result.\n\nI think that's solid. Maybe even another approach: breaking down 565 into 560 +5. So 401*(560 +5) = 401*560 + 401*5.\n\nCompute 401*560: 401*500 = 200,500; 401*60 = 24,060. So 200,500 +24,060 = 224,560.\n\nWait, hold on, 401*560 is 401*(500+60)=200,500 +24,060=224,560. Then 401*5=2005. Adding 224,560 +2005 = 226,565. Same answer again.\n\nHmm, all methods lead to the same conclusion, so I think that's the correct product. I can't see any arithmetic errors in my steps, so I feel confident.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 401 and 565 is \\boxed{226565}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 401 and 565, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\nFirst, we express the numbers as:\n\\[\n401 = 400 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n565 = 500 + 60 + 5\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n(400 + 1)(500 + 60 + 5) = 400 \\cdot (500 + 60 + 5) + 1 \\cdot (500 + 60 + 5)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n1. For \\(400 \\cdot (500 + 60 + 5)\\):\n   \\[\n   400 \\cdot 500 = 200000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   400 \\cdot 60 = 24000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   400 \\cdot 5 = 2000\n   \\]\n   Adding these results:\n   \\[\n   200000 + 24000 + 2000 = 226000\n   \\]\n\n2. For \\(1 \\cdot (500 + 60 + 5)\\):\n   \\[\n   1 \\cdot 500 + 1 \\cdot 60 + 1 \\cdot 5 = 500 + 60 + 5 = 565\n   \\]\n\nAdding the two parts together:\n\\[\n226000 + 565 = 226565\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- Multiply 401 by 5 (units place):\n  \\[\n  401 \\times 5 = 2005\n  \\]\n- Multiply 401 by 60 (tens place):\n  \\[\n  401 \\times 60 = 24060\n  \\]\n- Multiply 401 by 500 (hundreds place):\n  \\[\n  401 \\times 500 = 200500\n  \\]\n- Adding all results:\n  \\[\n  2005 + 24060 + 200500 = 226565\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{226565}\n\\]",
