      "text": "31,000 + 1,160 = 32,160.\n\nWait a second, let me verify that addition again to be sure. 26,800 plus 5,360. Breaking it down:\n\n26,800 + 5,000 = 31,800. Then add the remaining 360: 31,800 + 360 = 32,160. Yeah, that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way, vertically, to cross-verify. Let me try that method as well to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWriting 268 \u00d7 120. Since 120 is three digits, but the multiplication can be done by considering the place values. Let me write it out:\n\n\u2003\u2003268\n\u00d7 120\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 268 by 0 (the units place of 120), which gives 0. Then multiply 268 by 2 (the tens place), but since it's actually the tens place, it's 20, so we need to shift one position to the left. Wait, maybe I should write it properly.\n\nWait, actually, 120 has a 1 in the hundreds place, 2 in the tens place, and 0 in the units. Wait no, 120 is 1 hundred, 2 tens, and 0 ones. So when multiplying, we can break it down as:\n\n268 \u00d7 0 (units place) = 0\n\n268 \u00d7 2 (tens place) = 536, but since it's the tens place, it's 536 \u00d7 10 = 5,360\n\n268 \u00d7 1 (hundreds place) = 268, but since it's the hundreds place, it's 268 \u00d7 100 = 26,800\n\nNow, add all three results: 0 + 5,360 + 26,800 = 32,160. That matches my previous result. Good, so that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, another way to think about multiplying by 120 is to multiply 268 by 12 first and then multiply by 10, since 120 is 12 \u00d7 10. Let me try that method as well to check.\n\n268 \u00d7 12. Let's compute that. 268 \u00d7 10 is 2,680 and 268 \u00d7 2 is 536. Adding those together gives 2,680 + 536 = 3,216. Then multiply by 10: 3,216 \u00d7 10 = 32,160. Again, the same answer. Okay, so that's another confirmation.\n\nHmm, so all three methods give me 32,160. That seems pretty solid. I think that's the correct product. Let me just do a quick estimation to see if it's in the right ballpark. 120 \u00d7 268. If I round 268 to 270, then 120 \u00d7 270 = 32,400. Since 268 is 2 less than 270, the actual result should be 120 \u00d7 2 less, which is 240 less. 32,400 - 240 = 32,160. Exactly matches. That estimation method also aligns with the result. \n\nTherefore, I can be confident that the product of 120 and 268 is indeed 32,160.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 120 and 268 is \\boxed{32160}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 120 and 268, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into smaller steps:\n\n\\[\n120 \\times 268 = (100 + 20) \\times 268\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate each part separately:\n\\[\n100 \\times 268 = 26800\n\\]\n\\[\n20 \\times 268 = 2 \\times (10 \\times 268) = 2 \\times 2680 = 5360\n\\]\n\nNext, add the results together:\n\\[\n26800 + 5360 = 32160\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by breaking down the multiplication vertically:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   268 \\\\\n\\times 120 \\\\\n\\hline\n   0 \\quad \\text{(268 \u00d7 0)} \\\\\n  5360 \\quad \\text{(268 \u00d7 20, shifted one position left)} \\\\\n26800 \\quad \\text{(268 \u00d7 100, shifted two positions left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n32160 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification method involves multiplying 268 by 12 first and then by 10:\n\\[\n268 \\times 12 = 268 \\times (10 + 2) = 2680 + 536 = 3216\n\\]\n\\[\n3216 \\times 10 = 32160\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 120 and 268 is \\(\\boxed{32160}\\).",
