ousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (units). The 10,311 is 1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (units). Wait, actually, let's line them up properly:\n\n441,900 is:\n\n4 (100,000s place)\n\n4 (10,000s)\n\n1 (1,000s)\n\n9 (100s)\n\n0 (10s)\n\n0 (1s)\n\n10,311 is:\n\n0 (100,000s)\n\n1 (10,000s)\n\n0 (1,000s)\n\n3 (100s)\n\n1 (10s)\n\n1 (1s)\n\nWait, actually, 10,311 is:\n\n1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nWait, so adding:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes place: 0 +1 =1.\n\nTens place:0 +1=1.\n\nHundreds place:9 +3=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:1 (from 441,900) +0 (from 10,311's thousands place)=1, plus the carried 1 gives 2.\n\nTen-thousands place:4 +1 (from 10,311's ten thousands place)=5.\n\nHundred-thousands place:4 remains.\n\nSo the total is 452,211. Yes, that's correct.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident that the product of 491 and 921 is 452,211.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 491 and 921 is \\boxed{452211}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 491 and 921, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 491 by the units digit of 921 (1):**\n   \\[\n   491 \\times 1 = 491\n   \\]\n   This gives the first partial product: 491.\n\n2. **Multiply 491 by the tens digit of 921 (2), which is 20:**\n   \\[\n   491 \\times 2 = 982 \\quad \\text{(then add a zero)} \\quad \\Rightarrow 9820\n   \\]\n   This gives the second partial product: 9820.\n\n3. **Multiply 491 by the hundreds digit of 921 (9), which is 900:**\n   \\[\n   491 \\times 9 = 4419 \\quad \\text{(then add two zeros)} \\quad \\Rightarrow 441900\n   \\]\n   This gives the third partial product: 441900.\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     491 \\\\\n   + 9820 \\\\\n   +441900 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     452211 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   - First, add 491 and 9820:\n     \\[\n     491 + 9820 = 10311\n     \\]\n   - Then add the result to 441900:\n     \\[\n 