u00a0\u00a0\u00a0383\n\u00d7\u00a0210\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 383 by 0 (the units place of 210), which gives 0.\n\nThen multiply 383 by 1 (the tens place of 210, which is actually the hundreds place since it's the second digit from the right). Wait, hold on. Wait, 210 is 2 in the hundreds place, 1 in the tens place, and 0 in the ones place. So actually, when multiplying:\n\nFirst step: Multiply 383 by the ones digit: 0. So that's 0.\n\nSecond step: Multiply 383 by the tens digit: 1. But since it's the tens place, it's actually 10. So 383 \u00d7 10 = 3,830. So we write that shifted one position to the left.\n\nThird step: Multiply 383 by the hundreds digit: 2. Since it's the hundreds place, it's 200. So 383 \u00d7 200 = 76,600. Then shift two positions to the left.\n\nWait, actually, in standard multiplication:\n\nWait, no, let me correct that. Let me think again. When writing 210 as 2 hundreds, 1 ten, and 0 ones, so when multiplying:\n\nMultiply 383 \u00d7 0 (units place) = 0\n\nMultiply 383 \u00d7 1 (tens place) = 383 \u00d7 10 = 3,830\n\nMultiply 383 \u00d7 2 (hundreds place) = 383 \u00d7 200 = 76,600\n\nThen add those three results together: 0 + 3,830 + 76,600 = 80,430. Yep, same result. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the distributive property in another way. Let's see, 383 \u00d7 210. Maybe break down 383 as 300 + 80 + 3, but that might complicate. Alternatively, perhaps 383 \u00d7 200 + 383 \u00d7 10, which is what I did before. Either way, same result.\n\nAlternatively, I can check using a calculator, but since I don't have one, maybe do an approximate check. Let's see, 383 is close to 400, and 210 is 200 + 10. 400 \u00d7 200 is 80,000, and 400 \u00d7 10 is 4,000, so total would be 84,000. But since 383 is 17 less than 400, so 17 \u00d7 200 = 3,400. So subtract that from 84,000 gives 80,600. Hmm, but since I also have the 10 part, maybe my approximation isn't exact here. Wait, perhaps this is getting too convoluted. Alternatively, maybe my initial calculation is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 383 \u00d7 210 as (383 \u00d7 21) \u00d7 10. Let's see. 383 \u00d7 21 first. Then multiply by 10.\n\n383 \u00d7 21. Let's compute that:\n\n383 \u00d7 20 = 7,660\n\n383 \u00d7 1 = 383\n\nAdding those together: 7,660 + 383 = 8,043. Then multiply by 10 gives 80,430. Yes, that's the same answer again. So that seems consistent.\n\nAnother way: Let me do 210 \u00d7 400, which would be 84,000, but since it's 383, which is 17 less than 400, so subtract 17 \u00d7 210 from 84,000. So 17 \u00d7 200 is 3,400 and 17 \u00d7 10 is 170, so total 3,570. So 84,000 - 3,570 = 80,430. Perfect, that's the same answer again. So this cross-verification works.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident that 383 multiplied by 210 is indeed 80,430. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just do one more check with another method. Maybe using the standard algorithm step-by-step again.\n\nMultiplying 383 by 210:\n\nFirst, write 383 \u00d7 210.\n\nSince 210 has three digits, we can break it down into multiplying by each digit and shifting accordingly.\n\nStarting with the units digit of 210, which is 0. 383 \u00d7 0 = 0. So that's a row of zeros.\n\nNext, the tens digit is 1. Since it's the tens place, we multiply 383 by 1 and shift one position to the left, so 383 \u00d7 1 = 383, shifted becomes 3,830.\n\nThen the hundreds digit is 2. Multiply 383 by 2 and shift two positions to the left, which is 383 \u00d7 2 = 766, shifted becomes 76,600.\n\nAdding those three results:\n\n0\n\n+ 3,830\n\n+76,600\n\n= 80,430. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, so all methods are converging to 80,430, so I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations now. Initially, when I was adding 76,600 + 3,830, I had a moment of confusion but then corrected myself. But after verifying through different methods, it's clear that 80,430 is the correct product.\n\nJust to recap the steps clearly:\n\n1. Break down 210 into 200 + 10.\n\n2. Multiply 383 by 200:\n\n   - 383 \u00d7 2 = 766\n\n   - 766 \u00d7 100 = 76,600\n\n3. Multiply 383 by 10:\n\n   - 383 \u00d7 10 = 3,830\n\n4. Add the two results together:\n\n   - 76,600 + 3,830 = 80,430\n\nTherefore, the product is 80,430. All verification methods confirm this. I think that's solid. No mistakes detected now. Confident in the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 383 and 210 is \\boxed{80430}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 383 and 210, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\n1. Break down 210 into 200 + 10.\n2. Multiply 383 by 200:\n   \\[\n   383 \\times 200 = 383 \\times 2 \\times 100 = 766 \\times 100 = 76,600\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 383 by 10:\n   \\[\n   383 \\times 10 = 3,830\n   \\]\n4. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   76,600 + 3,830 = 80,430\n   \\]\n\nVerification using alternative methods (standard multiplication algorithm and approximation) confirms the result. \n\nThe product of 383 and 210 is \\boxed{80430}.",
