think that's solid.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident the answer is 346,020.\n\nWait, but let me just do a quick check with approximate estimation. 365 is roughly 350, 948 is roughly 950. 350\u00d7950. 350\u00d7900=315,000. 350\u00d750=17,500. Total 332,500. But our answer is 346,020, which is a bit higher, which makes sense because we rounded 365 down to 350 and 948 down to 950 (wait, actually 948 is rounded down to 950? Wait 948 is 2 less than 950, so actually it's rounded up. Hmm, perhaps better to see:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n365 vs 350 (difference +15)\n\n948 vs 950 (difference -2)\n\nSo the estimate is 350\u00d7950=332,500, but the actual numbers are a bit higher and lower: (350+15)*(950-2). So expanding that would be 350\u00d7950 +15\u00d7950 -350\u00d72 -15\u00d72.\n\nWhich is 332,500 +14,250 -700 -30 =332,500 +14,250=346,750 -730=346,020. Exactly! So that's a precise way to see why the approximation works. So that's another verification step. So that's really thorough.\n\nHence, after multiple methods and checks, I can confirm that 365 multiplied by 948 is indeed 346,020.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 365 and 948 is \\boxed{346020}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 365 and 948, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify the result through multiple methods.\n\n1. **Multiplying by the units digit (8):**\n   \\[\n   365 \\times 8 = 2920\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiplying by the tens digit (40):**\n   \\[\n   365 \\times 40 = 14600\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiplying by the hundreds digit (900):**\n   \\[\n   365 \\times 900 = 328500\n   \\]\n\n4. **Adding the partial products:**\n   \\[\n   2920 + 14600 + 328500\n   \\]\n   - First, add \\(2920\\) and \\(14600\\):\n     \\[\n     2920 + 14600 = 17520\n     \\]\n   - Then add the result to \\(328500\\):\n     \\[\n     17520 + 328500 = 346020\n     \\]\n\n5. **Verification using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   365 \\times 948 = 365 \\times (900 + 40 + 8) = 365 \\times 900 + 365 \\times 40 + 3